{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-16 07:05:00 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-10-16 14:05 +07\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1)\n     4\tSession-ID: 87537dc6-afdd-4961-b966-e516a18bb2ea\n     5\t\n     6\t# Slice 6d plan, refreshed: any-pc snapshots as a primary invariant\n     7\t\n     8\tRole: Architect. Worktree `datomworld-6d-plan` = `origin/master` at `ce76c846` (phases 7 and 8 merged; G1, G2, follow-up-1 and module-pins commit 1 merged). Read-only; the only file written is this report. Every `file:line` below was re-read in this tree today; where the 2026-10-13 assessment (`collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md`, \"the assessment\") cited a line that moved, the new line is given and the old one is not repeated. Vocabulary per UCF §7.5.3: *cursor* (immutable stream value), *cursor cell* (the carrier's box), *cursor-ref* (the sealed reference), *cell* (the heap box only).\n     9\t\n    10\t**The owner's ruling, applied.** Preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm`. Three consequences follow at once and the rest of this plan is their working-out: (1) a lift that refuses a machine whose `:control` is set (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) is a defect against the invariant, not a scope choice; (2) a running root is a custody subject, so the holder, the ledger and the grantor must admit a fourth kind; (3) the de Bruijn backends were retained (`yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` §0.1 ruling 8, §4.6), so the cross-engine half is live work on the documented fragment. The assessment's two blocking questions (Q1 kernels, Q2 custody) are both answered by rulings now on record, and its verdict moves from NOT READY to PLAN-READY.\n    11\t\n    12\t**Summary for the owner.**\n    13\t\n    14\t1. The kernel already materialises the any-pc state: `vm-hot` at fuel exhaustion returns `put-registers machine seg pc W E K` (`semantic.cljc:142-143`, `:50-55`); `vm/step` is `vm-hot` with fuel 1 (`:331-332`). Nothing about the machine changes. What changes is the carrier (one new state kind `:run`, one new root kind `:running`), the validator (rules R1-R3 beside A1-A6), the lift's kind step, the layouts' running mode, and the custody surfaces that enumerate `#{:blocked :parked :halted}`.\n    15\t2. **Since the assessment**, phase 7 landed custody on rows (every kind table it wrote lists three kinds and must list four), phase 8 renamed the kernel (`yin.vm.semantic`, record `SemanticVM`) and deleted the old stack-shaped UCF halves, G1 added `:try?` to link pendings (no effect on 6d), G2 added `:retained-ffi` to `lower-defect` (no effect), and module-pins commit 1 landed on master (`check-install-admission!`, `rows.cljc:1886-1895`, `:2302-2303`). Module-pins commit 2 is **not written**: it is blocked on the host-profile carrier decision (known-gaps row \"Q5\"). Its prospective hunks in `rows.cljc` (`requires`, `:1689-1691`; a receiver re-discharge in or beside `check-dependencies!`, `:1913-1940`) do not overlap 6d's hunks (`:1650-1663`, `:1692-1694`, `:1877-1882`, `:2264-2267`, `:1261`, `:1297`, `:1704-1716`). Recommended order: **6d first**; it re-mints no fixture. Commit 2 changes the root's `requires` slot and re-mints every pinned root; if it unblocks mid-6d, land it first and rebase 6d (trivial), so 6d's own `:running` fixtures are minted once (§1.4).\n    16\t3. **The holder today cannot preempt.** `driver/run-cycle` runs the machine with `vm/run` to a halt or park (`driver.cljc:2120-2122`) and rechecks tenure only before and after (`:2118`, `:2123`); a long pure computation holds the program step past the lease bound and the run then ends `:lease-bound` with the machine gated `:ended` and lost to this holder (`step-active`, `:2252-2253`). Cooperative preemption is one driver change: run the machine for a fuel budget per cycle, through a new public `semantic/run-fuel` (§2.2). After the cycle the machine is pure data at an instruction boundary; `hand-off` lifts it as `:running`.\n    17\t4. **Custody for a running root is the blocked/parked arm, not the halted arm**: full custody slot (occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq, origin), grant checks on lower, gate `:running` on restore, offerable by the grantor. `protect!` is vacuous (no retained writes). Nothing about parked roots regresses: every refusal that exists today for a parked root stays, and the ready-queue refusal stays for running roots too (§2.5).\n    18\t5. **The quiescence rule is restated honestly, not weakened.** `:yin.k/not-quiescent` remains for any non-empty ready queue. For a machine with `:control` nil a caller crosses it with one `vm/step` (as the assessment said); for a machine with `:control` set it is crossed only when the active activation parks and the sweep resumes the queued entry (`run-loop`, `engine.cljc:2205-2219`), which is organically reachable only in a multi-waiter machine (§2.5).\n    19\t6. **Doc amendments** are given as exact replacement text in §3 for UCF §7.4.1, §7.4.2, §7.7.4, §7.8, §7.9, §7.10, §7.11 and §7.11.1; the frozen design §4.1-4.3; the continuation design §0.2, §1.1-1.2, §2.3, §2.6, §3.2, §4.5, §5.4, §5.6, §7.1-7.2, §8.3 and §11 Q6; the transport-tuples §1, §5.2, §5.5, §9; the v2 amendment §7.1 (the one sentence that says \"Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth kind\"); `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` §14.1; and the assessment's own §F.\n    20\t7. **Slices**: 6d-a reference lane (validator, row kind, lift/lower, laws 1/2/6 at every pc), 6d-b holder/custody wiring for running roots, 6e cross-engine (layouts' running mode, the two static checks at every pc, laws 4/5 at every pc, the register-kernel refusal allowlist, the tail-call dead layout). 6d-b **can ship with 6d-a** as a second commit on the same branch (§4.2 says why) and must land before any composition exports a running root; the order 6d-a → 6d-b → 6e is forced by data dependency only between a and the other two.\n    21\t8. **Effort**: 6d-a 2 implementation days + 3 review rounds; 6d-b 1.5 + 2; 6e 2.5 + 3; docs 1 + 1. Routing: codex (gpt-6.1-sol, workspace-write) implements; Fable reviews; glm/cmd as non-author fallback reviewers (owner routing 2026-10-15).\n    22\t9. **Owner decisions still needed**: none that block 6d-a. Two policy knobs for 6d-b (fuel budget as a holder config key; whether the driver auto-exports at the lease bound or only on `hand-off`) and one grammar choice (where the `:run` cref lives in the root: slot 13 reused, recommended, vs. a new slot that re-mints every root) are stated with a recommendation each (§5).\n    23\t10. **Reclassification**: the gap list called 6d \"a capability gap only\" (G7). The owner's invariant makes it a **P1 defect against a primary invariant** of the kernel's continuation format: the format cannot express a state the kernel is in between any two instructions. Every sentence that currently asserts the safepoint-only rule is listed in §6 with its disposition so none survives.\n    24\t\n    25\t---\n    26\t\n    27\t## 1. What changed since the assessment, and what it does to 6d\n    28\t\n    29\t### 1.1 Renames and moved lines (mechanical)\n    30\t\n    31\t| Assessment cited | Now | Note |\n    32\t|---|---|---|\n    33\t| `yin.vm.semantic-register`, `semantic_register.cljc` | `yin.vm.semantic`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc`, record `SemanticVM` (`:16-46`) | 8f-2 |\n    34\t| `vm-hot` `:134-268`, fuel exit `:142-143`, `put-registers` `:50-55`, `vm/step` `:331-332` | unchanged lines | verified |\n    35\t| `rows.cljc` `lift-task*` `:1456-1461`, `live-pcs` `:1386-1399`, `:1495` id-counter | `:1650-1698`, `:1579-1592`, `:1692` | phase 7 inserted the custody block above |\n    36\t| `frame.cljc:104` (`0 < pc`) | `:122` (`(pos? n)`) | S1 at `:96-98`, C3 at `:124-127`, P1 at `:162-164` |\n    37\t| `layout.cljc` `safepoint!` `:318-330`, `canonical-task` `:724-725`, `state-prim` `:863-874`, `lower-defect` `:916-947` | `:318-330`, `:715-755` (refusal `:724-725`), `:863-874`, `:916-953` | G2 added the `:retained-ffi` clause `:942-944` |\n    38\t| `derive.cljc` `allocation` `:161-189`, `register-image` `:200-214` | `:123-151`, `:154-184` | 8e removed the old lowerers from the file; the allocation algorithm is unchanged: live points are `L(pc) ∪ def(pc)` (`:127-130`), a slot frees only when the interval end `< pc` (`:140-143`) |\n    39\t| `engine.cljc` `handle-put` gate `:532-533`, `observe-put` `:498`, `run-loop` `:2171-2179`, `scheduler-round` `:2286-2287`, `advance-install` `:1437-1446`, `park-id` `:2290-2294` | `:532-533`, `:495-498`, `:2205-2219`, `:2315-2326`, `:1458-1491`, `:2329-2333` | phase 7 inserted the gate/apply family above |\n    40\t| harness `:1870-1872` ad-hoc read of a stepped kernel; `:1901-1925` `layout-refusals` | `:1899-1904`; `:1886-1962` | the \"not at a safepoint\" refusal tests are `:1012-1025` (reference lane) and `:1887-1904` (backends) |\n    41\t\n    42\tThe analysis of the assessment §A (every boundary is a canonical state; `L(p)` is the window; A.3 injectivity; A.4 stack layout; A.5 atomic steps; A.6 cross-engine) re-verifies line for line against the renamed files. Nothing in phases 7 or 8 touched `analysis.cljc` (`live` `:86-89`, `saved` `:92-97`, `successors` `:15-28`), the kernel's step loop, or the layouts' static half (`stack-layouts` `layout.cljc:118-131`, `virt` `:224-237`, `stack-layout` `:240-244`).\n    43\t\n    44\t### 1.2 Phase 7: custody on rows (the part that makes 6d-b necessary)\n    45\t\n    46\tPhase 7 wrote the kind tables that a `:running` root must join. Every one of them is a three-element set today:\n    47\t\n    48\t| Site | Today | 6d-b change |\n    49\t|---|---|---|\n    50\t| `rows.cljc:1261` `task-ops`, `:1297` `custody-baseline` | `#{:blocked :parked :halted}` | add `:running` |\n    51\t| `rows.cljc:1704-1716` `header-custody` | halted → origin only; else the four keys + origin | `:running` takes the four-key arm (it is a lease subject) |\n    52\t| `rows.cljc:1877-1882` `check-pendings!` | `case` over three kinds, default `:undecodable :kind` | `:running` arm: waits empty, no parked-id, no result, exactly one frame of kind `:run` |\n    53\t| `rows.cljc:2023-2039` `check-custody!` | grant checks unless fenced or halted | `:running` runs `accept-grant!` like blocked/parked |\n    54\t| `rows.cljc:2042-2064` `with-custody` | gate `:ended` iff root halted, else `:running` | unchanged (a `:running` root gates `:running`) |\n    55\t| `rows.cljc:2264-2267` `restore-task` | three arms | `:running` arm (§4.1) |\n    56\t| `rows.cljc:1200-1245` `check-root-custody!` | `:halted-header` for a halted root with more than origin | unchanged; `:running` is validated as blocked/parked |\n    57\t| `export.cljc:274-282` `kind-of` | parked → blocked → **else halted** | `(some? (:control m)) → :running` tested first; today a running machine would be mislabelled `:halted` |\n    58\t| `export.cljc:121-149` `enter` | refuses exporting/ended, queue, holds; detaches waits and parked | unchanged logic; the running state stays in the machine's `:control :window :env :k` fields, which the lift reads; `:record` needs no new key |\n    59\t| `export.cljc:408-427` `valid-state?` | gate ∈ `#{nil :running :exporting :ended}` | unchanged |\n    60\t| `ledger.cljc:264-284` `offer-defect` `:277` | `#{:blocked :parked}` offerable | add `:running` |\n    61\t| `authority/grant.cljc:74-75` `offer-decision` | `#{:blocked :parked}` offerable | add `:running` |\n    62\t| `driver.cljc:2291-2316` `step-validating` | halted → `:safepoint` phase; else awaiting-grant | unchanged (a `:running` root takes the awaiting-grant path through `lowered {:exclusive true}`) |\n    63\t| `driver.cljc:2147-2154` `at-safepoint?` | halted, no waits, no queue | unchanged: it detects the *halt*; the running case is `hand-off` |\n    64\t| `driver.cljc:1118-1134` `hand-off` | \"parked or blocked at a liftable safepoint\" | docstring only; the body (`arm-exit` → `export/enter`) already works on a running machine once `kind-of` is fixed |\n    65\t| `driver.cljc:313-356` `source` | `:machine` \"parked, blocked or halted\" | docstring; `check!` does not test the kind |\n    66\t| `driver.cljc:2111-2144` `run-cycle` | `vm/run` under `:running` | **the one behavioural change**: fuel (§2.2) |\n    67\t| `rows/checkpoint.cljc` inspector | reads kind through `custody-baseline` | follows `:1297` |\n    68\t\n    69\tFenced rehydration (`rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`, `export/rehydrate-fenced` `export.cljc:480-`) restores a `:running` root with `:control` set and gate `:exporting`. The driver never runs an exporting machine (`run-cycle` `:2120`). But a direct `vm/step` or `vm/run` on it would execute **pure** instructions, because the gate guards effects only (`gate-refuse!` `engine.cljc:484-492` is consulted by `handle-put`, `handle-next`, `handle-close`, `stream/cursor`, `resume-continuation`, `ffi/put-request` `ffi.cljc:61`, never by `vm-hot`'s pure arms). For parked roots this was moot: an exporting machine has `:control` nil and `vm-hot` falls into `scheduler-round` (`semantic.cljc:137-138`), which observes nothing under a gate (`check-wait-set` `:2161-2163`). For a running root the \"exporting machine is pure data\" claim of UCF §7.7.4 needs one guard: `vm-hot` refuses to step under `:exporting`/`:ended` (§4.2 item 3). It is a two-line engine edit, inside the kernel's entry, not in the hot loop.\n    70\t\n    71\t### 1.3 Phase 8: cutover and deletions\n    72\t\n    73\t- The old stack kernel, its lowerers, the v3 canonicalizer and \"stack-shaped safepoint halves\" of `yin.vm.ucf` died (8f-1, 8f-3; known-gaps row 27). Nothing 6d needs died: `yin.vm.ucf` is now `code-address` only; the frame validator, the row codec and the layouts are the phase-6 files.\n    74\t- The de Bruijn kernels **remain as backends** (continuation §0.1 ruling 8, §4.6; phase 7e `fdd6ce74`). The assessment's Q1 is answered: the cross-engine half (6e) is live work. Its scope is the **documented fragment** (61 law-4 cases, 350 law-5 transfers; rows 7-12 and 17 excluded, row 4 not lowered into a backend). 6e must not widen it (owner 2026-10-13); the any-pc lanes run on the same 61 cases.\n    75\t- Descriptor hashes are BLAKE3 (8ab); `:dim/lift-to` is gone. 6e reads kernel fields only; no descriptor edit.\n    76\t- The UCF §7.4.1 table is marked \"superseded for the register-shaped machine by `yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1\" (`universal-continuation-format.md:658-666`) but still states the rule \"**a continuation may be lifted only at a safepoint**\" (`:672-674`) as the machine-independent rule. It is the first sentence §3 replaces.\n    77\t\n    78\t### 1.4 G1, G2, follow-up-1, module-pins\n    79\t\n    80\t- **G1** (`:try?` on link pendings): the `:pending` table (`rows.cljc:40-46`) and P2′ (`frame.cljc:174-178`) changed; a `:run` state has no pending, so no interaction.\n    81\t- **G2** (`:retained-ffi` refusal in `lower-defect` `:942-944`): a `:running` root has no waits, so the clause never fires on it; 6e's running mode is unaffected.\n    82\t- **follow-up-1**: `bindings=` in `frame.cljc:64-77` (NaN-reflexive free-env compare) applies to a `:run`'s `E` through E2 unchanged; map/set key collapse refusals are value-level.\n    83\t- **module-pins commit 1** (on master): `install-node` strips `:yin.admit/*` (`:1637-1639`), `check-install!` refuses carried admission (`:1849`), `check-install-admission!` (`:1886-1895`) runs after custody and before attach (`:2301-2303`). A `:running` root carries install children exactly as a blocked one does (the walk at `:1675` is kind-independent), so commit 1 covers it with no change.\n    84\t- **module-pins commit 2** (not written; blocked on the host-profile schema): will edit `requires` (`:1689-1691`), add a receiver re-discharge beside `check-dependencies!` (`:1913-1940`), and re-mint `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` and every pinned root (the `requires` slot is inside the root row). 6d's hunks are in `lift-task*` lines `:1652-1663` and `:1692` (kind and the task node's slot 2 and 13), `check-pendings!` `:1877-1882`, `restore-task` `:2264-2267`, `task-ops`/`custody-baseline` `:1261`/`:1297`, `header-custody` `:1704-1716`, and `check-custody!` `:2039`. The nearest pair is `:1663` (6d) and `:1689` (commit 2) in one function, 26 lines apart: no textual conflict. **Order**: 6d first, because it is unblocked and re-mints nothing (it adds a kind and a row kind; no existing row changes shape, so no pinned id moves). If commit 2 unblocks while 6d is in review, land commit 2 first and rebase 6d, so that any `:running` root fixture 6d pins is minted once. 6d's acceptance pins **step counts**, not root ids (§4.1 item 4), so even the other order costs only a rebase.\n    85\t\n    86\t### 1.5 The REPL and Stage E\n    87\t\n    88\tStage E (`stage_e_scenarios.cljc`, `stage_e_world.cljc`) drives holders by phase (`[:until \"h1\" :safepoint n]`) and uses the semantic kernel only. No scenario has a running-root handoff; 6d-b adds one (§4.2 item 6). The comment at `stage_e_scenarios.cljc:184-186` (\"holds an unapplied observation and is no liftable safepoint, UCF 7.4.1\") is about a **hold**, which stays a refusal; the comment's citation changes (§6).\n    89\t\n    90\t---\n    91\t\n    92\t## 2. Custody consequences of a running root\n    93\t\n    94\t### 2.1 What a running root is\n    95\t\n    96\tA machine with `:control {:segment seg :pc pc}` set, `:k` a vector or nil, `:window W`, `:env E`, `:blocked? false`, `:halted? false`, `:value` irrelevant, and `:wait-set []`. The last is an invariant of the kernel, not a choice: every arm of `vm-hot` that creates a wait entry also clears `:control` (`run-effect` `:stop` arm `semantic.cljc:96-97`; `:ffi-call` `:252-254`; `register-restore` `request-sent` arm `:110-111`), and nothing re-sets `:control` while a wait exists except a resume, which first pops the wait or the queue. A machine with `:control` set and a non-empty wait set is therefore malformed, and the lift refuses it as `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` (§4.1 R0). The ready queue may be non-empty while `:control` is set (§2.5).\n    97\t\n    98\tIts canonical state is the assessment's `:run` form, unchanged:\n    99\t\n   100\t```clojure\n   101\t{:yin.k/state :run\n   102\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n          ; the instruction about to execute\n   103\t :yin.k/env E  :yin.k/window {r V …}    ; exactly L(n); nil present as [:lit nil]\n   104\t :yin.k/k [ …return frames… ]}          ; no deliver, no pending\n   105\t```\n   106\t\n   107\t### 2.2 How the holder pauses it: cooperative preemption at an instruction boundary\n   108\t\n   109\tToday (`driver/run-cycle` `driver.cljc:2111-2144`): `(vm/run m0)` when the gate is `:running` and the machine is not halted (`:2120-2122`); `require-tenure!` before (`:2118`) and after (`:2123`). `vm/run` is `ffi/maybe-run` over `register-run-scheduler` (`semantic.cljc:271-274`, `:333`), a `run-loop` that steps `(vm-hot % nil)` until the activation parks or halts. A pure loop of a billion instructions holds `run-cycle` for its duration; the next `require-tenure!` throws `:lease-bound`, `step-active` ends the run (`:2252-2253`), the machine is gated `:ended`, and the occurrence is regrantable from its **last checkpoint**, which is the pack this holder lowered. The computation since that checkpoint is lost. This is exactly the violation the owner names.\n   110\t\n   111\tThe change (6d-b item 1):\n   112\t\n   113\t- `yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`: `(if (engine/active-continuation? machine) (vm-hot machine n) (scheduler-round machine))`, public, telemetry-free; `vm-hot` with fuel `n` already answers `put-registers` at the boundary (`:142-143`). It is **not** `n` calls of `vm/step` (each emits a telemetry snapshot and runs `ready-for-ingress?`, `:331-332`).\n   114\t- `driver/run-cycle` reads a holder config key `:fuel` (positive integer or nil). With nil it runs `vm/run` as today (no behaviour change for existing compositions and tests). With `n` it runs `(run-fuel m0 n)`: the machine comes back either parked/halted (as before) or **at an instruction boundary with `:control` set**. The cycle continues (replay, emit, reader step) exactly as today; those read the machine's resources and wait set, not its control.\n   115\t- `cycle-result` (`:2166-2185`) is unchanged: the phase stays `:running`; the next program tick runs another `n` instructions. `require-tenure!` between cycles is the preemption point: tenure dying between two fuel slices ends the run with the machine **at a boundary**, and the holder, instead of losing it, can arm the exit (`hand-off`) **before** the bound. The driver's renewal cadence (half the duration, `:60-65`) already gives the composition a window; the composition picks `n` so that one slice is short against that window. The driver has no clock-to-instruction mapping and should not acquire one (no clock in the VM; `dao.lease` owns liveness).\n   116\t- `hand-off` (`:1118-1134`) on a machine at a boundary: `arm-exit` → `export/enter` → `prepare`/`encode` → `kind :running`. Nothing else in the exit half reads the kind except through `custody-baseline` and `kind-of`.\n   117\t\n   118\t**Whether the driver auto-exports at the bound** is a policy knob (§5, decision 2). Recommendation: no automatic export; `hand-off` remains the composition's explicit act (UCF §7.8 \"composition-driven, never automatic\"), and 6d-b exposes a status detail `:yin.k/at-boundary true` on the holder state after a fuel-exhausted cycle so a composition can decide.\n   119\t\n   120\t### 2.3 What the source does after the grant\n   121\t\n   122\tUnchanged from a parked root. The source's driver is a candidate over its own bytes (`driver.cljc:109-112`): it never resumes its local machine on data; it resumes only through a grant to itself. After `enter`, the local machine is gated `:exporting` with `:control` still set and `:wait-set []`; the driver does not run it (`:2120`); `vm-hot` refuses to step it (6d-b item 3); a direct `:resume` is refused (`resume-continuation` `engine.cljc:2359`). `abort` (`export.cljc:578-`) returns the gate to its prior value and the machine continues from the same boundary. On a grant to another holder the source's machine is ended and released as today; the successor occurrence lowers the pack as a `:running` root and continues from `pc`.\n   123\t\n   124\t### 2.4 Occurrence, lease, fencing, exclusive handoff for a running root\n   125\t\n   126\t- **Custody slot**: the blocked/parked form (`{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on a successor). A running root is a lease subject: it has a next observable step. `header-custody` (`rows.cljc:1704-1716`) takes the four-key arm for `:running`.\n   127\t- **Operation ids**: a running root carries no retained write, so `protect!` (`:1719-1737`) checks nothing and `custody-baseline`'s `:yin.k/ops` is empty; `next-op-seq` travels and the receiver's writer continues numbering from it. `:yin.k/issue` numbering (`with-custody` `:2052-2056`) numbers zero waits. The `:op-seq-exhausted` check (`:1769`) applies to a `:running` root as to blocked/parked.\n   128\t- **Grant on lower**: `check-custody!` (`:2023-2039`) runs `accept-grant!` for `:running` (today it skips only `fenced?` and `:halted`). Evidence, binding, lease, holder, arbitration, epoch, tenure and the input prefix are checked exactly as for a blocked root; the pack address is the carrier's.\n   129\t- **Offer**: `ledger/offer-defect` (`:277`) and `grant/offer-decision` (`:74-75`) admit `:running`; a `:running` offer is otherwise shaped as a blocked one. The checkpoint inspector follows `custody-baseline`.\n   130\t- **Gate on restore**: `with-custody` gates the tree `:running` (root not halted). The restored machine's `:control` is set, so the holder's next program tick runs it under the gate: `:stream-put` parks without observing (`handle-put` `:532-533`), `:stream-next` parks (`handle-next` `:677`), `:stream-cursor` mints an unminted cell (`:2654-2656`), `:stream-close` queues (`:754`), `:ffi-call`'s `ffi/put-request` answers `full` so the request is retained for the driver (`ffi.cljc:61-62`). The first effect the resumed computation reaches is therefore the driver's to perform, exactly as for a resumed parked root.\n   131\t- **In-flight host effects**: none exist at a boundary. Each `vm-hot` arm is one pure function of the pre-state and, where it observes, one observation's outcome inside the arm (assessment §A.5, re-verified: `observe-put` `engine.cljc:495-498` is called from `handle-put` inside `run-effect` inside the step; `:ffi-call` mints the park and appends inside one arm `semantic.cljc:237-254`). An exception inside an arm leaves the loop locals behind and the caller sees the pre-state. Under the `:running` gate the observe halves do not run at all (above). The owner's monad sentence is true of the gated machine and of every retry; the restatement stands: **a step is atomic; effects are observed only inside a step or inside the sweep; every state between steps is pure data.**\n   132\t- **Successor chain**: the exit mints the successor occurrence once (`exit-mint` `driver.cljc:1137-`), origin names the predecessor occurrence and lease; unchanged.\n   133\t- **Recovery format 2**: `freeze` stores the pack and the holder-local state (`export.cljc:344-375`); `recovery-metadata` records per-wait issue stamps (zero for a running root) and the gate; `rehydrate-fenced` restores `:control` set, gate `:exporting`, `:yin.k/recovery-waits []`. Re-preparation must reproduce the pack byte for byte, which holds because the `:run` frame is a pure function of the restored fields.\n   134\t\n   135\t### 2.5 The exporting phase and the quiescence rule\n   136\t\n   137\t`export/enter` (`export.cljc:121-149`) requires, in order: not already exporting/ended; **empty ready queue in every task of the tree** (`first-refusal` `:107-114`); no hold (`refusal-at` `:83-104`: `:observe`, `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`, `:link-cursor-not-installed`). It then sets the gate, empties the wait set and the parked map into the record. For a running root nothing is detached (no waits); the parked map still moves (a running root may hold parked records reachable by `:resume`). `lift-task*` repeats the first two refusals (`:1652-1655`) and keeps them.\n   138\t\n   139\t**The ready queue stays a refusal.** The assessment's §B.1 reasoning holds: a woken entry is a performed effect's result (a woken `:put` has appended; a woken `:next` holds the successor cursor in `:resource-updates`, `make-woken-run-queue-entries` `engine.cljc:442-463`) that no row kind carries, and the engine never leaves one at rest in its own API. One correction to the assessment's \"a caller crosses it with one `vm/step`\": that is true when `:control` is nil (`vm-hot` runs `scheduler-round`, which pops the first entry, `:2315-2326`). When `:control` is set, `vm/step` executes one instruction of the active activation and the queue is untouched; it drains only when the activation parks or halts and `run-loop` reaches the `(seq q)` arm (`:2216-2218`). So a running root with a non-empty ready queue lifts only once its activation has parked and the queued entry has been resumed, which is `vm/run` to the next park. Organically this needs two waiters woken in one sweep (the harness's `two-callers`, `:1069`); an organic one-control machine has at most one waiter and the queue is empty whenever `:control` is set. The holder under fuel sees this case only in multi-waiter machines; `hand-off` then answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent` and the composition retries after the next cycle, as it does today for a queue it observes. No `:ready` row kind (as before).\n   140\t\n   141\t**Nothing about parked roots regresses**: every refusal above applies to parked roots unchanged; the only parked-root behaviour that changes is that `kind-of` and the kind tables gain a branch that parked roots never take.\n   142\t\n   143\t---\n   144\t\n   145\t## 3. Design amendments, exact replacement text\n   146\t\n   147\tMarkers: each clause carries \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\". Cite the owner's sentence once, in the continuation design §0.1, and point to it elsewhere.\n   148\t\n   149\t### 3.1 `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md`\n   150\t\n   151\t**§0.1, new ruling 9 (after ruling 8):**\n   152\t\n   153\t> 9. **Preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm`** (owner, 2026-10-17: \"preemptive migration, the ability to pause and continue at any PC, is a primary invariant of yin.vm. It is why yin.vm is defined as a continuation VM based on the CESK model. The UCF being only defined at a wait or parked state is a violation of this invariant\"). The carrier therefore expresses the evaluator's state between **any** two instructions, not only its two delivery states; the parking pcs are where the evaluator stops by itself, and a safepoint is a delivery-bearing special case of the general state. Slice 6d lands the general state on the reference lane and in custody; slice 6e lands it in the backends' layouts.\n   154\t\n   155\t**§0.2, first sentence**, replace \"A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return` frames, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait (frozen §2.4, §4.2)\" with:\n   156\t\n   157\t> A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, which is a `run` state `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` at any instruction boundary (§1.3), or one of its two delivery states, `act` or `tail`, with the saved window, the delivery record and the pending wait the evaluator mints when it parks (frozen §2.4, §4.2)\n   158\t\n   159\tand after \"`:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and `:parked-table` rows\" insert \", the `:run` row (6d)\".\n   160\t\n   161\t**§1.1, after the three shapes**, add a fourth line and bullet:\n   162\t\n   163\t> ```clojure\n   164\t> run  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K}      ; between instructions; no deliver\n   165\t> ```\n   166\t> - `run` is the state `vm-hot` materialises when fuel runs out (`put-registers`, `semantic.cljc:142-143`, `:50-55`): `pc` is the instruction **about to execute**, `W` is the kernel's untrimmed body-local window and the carrier trims it to `L(pc)` (`analysis/live`), `K` as for `act`. A `run` at `n` differs by kind from an `act` at `n`: the `act` has executed `n−1`, carries `saved(n, rd)` and awaits `rd`; the `run` has not executed `n` and carries `L(n)`, which may contain that `rd`.\n   167\t\n   168\t**§1.2**, add the keyed form after the tail state:\n   169\t\n   170\t> ```clojure\n   171\t> ;; run state (6d)\n   172\t> {:yin.k/state :run\n   173\t>  :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n                ; the instruction about to execute, 0 ≤ n < (count v)\n   174\t>  :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n   175\t>  :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly L(n); sorted by r on the wire\n   176\t>  :yin.k/k [ … ]}                            ; no deliver, no pending\n   177\t> ```\n   178\t\n   179\t**New §1.3 \"States between instructions\"** (the assessment's §F text, updated):\n   180\t\n   181\t> **1.3 States between instructions.** The two delivery states of §1.1 are the states at which the evaluator parks. They are special cases of the evaluator's general state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, which `vm-hot` materializes whenever fuel runs out (`semantic.cljc:142-143`) and which the carrier expresses as the `run` state of §1.2 with no `deliver` and no `pending`. A step is atomic: effects are observed only inside a step or inside the scheduler's sweep, so no state between steps holds a half-issued effect. The static correspondence of §4 holds at every pc: distinct virtuals in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` hold distinct physical registers (`derive/allocation`, `derive.cljc:123-151`), and the stack kernel's operand stack at A pc `p` is `stack-layout(p) ++ uses(p)`; both are checks (§5.6). A machine with `:control` set and a non-empty wait set is malformed and refused `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state`. The one task state the carrier refuses by choice is a non-empty ready queue: a woken entry is a performed effect's result that lives in neither a pending nor a window, and the engine itself never leaves one at rest (`run-loop`); with `:control` nil a caller crosses it with one `vm/step`, with `:control` set by running the activation to its next park. A root whose task is a `run` state has kind `:running` (§2.6) and is a custody subject like a blocked or parked root (§7.3).\n   182\t\n   183\t**§2.3 row table**, add a row and amend `:wait`'s note:\n   184\t\n   185\t> | `:run` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref` | the between-instructions state; no delivery, no pending; five slots (an `:act` has six) |\n   186\t\n   187\tand `:wait`: \"`state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row, never a `:run`\".\n   188\t\n   189\t**§2.6 root**, `kind` comment: `; :blocked | :parked | :halted | :running`; `frames` comment: `; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order (:blocked, :parked); exactly one cref to a :run row (:running)`. Kind rules paragraph (also tuples §5.5): add \"`:running` has exactly one frame, a `:run`, and neither parked-id nor result; its wait set is empty.\"\n   190\t\n   191\t**§3.2 S1**: \"`:yin.k/state` is `:act`, `:tail` or `:run`; … a `run` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/k` and never `:yin.k/pending`\". **C2**: \"`0 < pc < (count v)` for an `act` and every return frame; `0 ≤ pc < (count v)` for a `run` (a body-start pc is a legal running state); `0 ≤ site < (count v)`\". **C3**: \"for the act and every return frame; **not** for a `run` (`body(pc−1) = body(pc)` is meaningless at a body start)\". New block after the tail site:\n   192\t\n   193\t> **The running activation (`run` only; a `run` skips A1-A6 and T1).**\n   194\t>\n   195\t> | # | Rule | Kind |\n   196\t> |---|---|---|\n   197\t> | R1 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n   198\t> | R2 | `(set (keys window)) = (analysis/live a pc)` exactly | `:window-extra` / `:window-missing` |\n   199\t> | R3 | no `:yin.k/deliver`, no `:yin.k/pending` (S1) | `:frame-shape` |\n   200\t>\n   201\t> There is no `:window-self` analogue: a `run` has no destination. `window-defect` is parameterised by the wanted set: `saved(pc, rd)` for an act and a return frame, `live(pc)` for a run.\n   202\t\n   203\t**P1**: \"`context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured`, `:parked` and `:running` require it absent; a `run` is admitted in context `:running` only, and `:running` admits only a `run`\" (`:pending-presence`). E1, E2, K1-K3, X1 unchanged (\"a `run`'s `E` is checked at its own `pc`'s chain\").\n   204\t\n   205\t**§3.3**: add \"and the root's `:run` frame in context `:running`\" to the list judged in step 7.\n   206\t\n   207\t**§4.5**, replace the last clause (\"never invents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking instruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2)\") with:\n   208\t\n   209\t> never invents a state: a kernel pc outside every body, or a `:push` pc of H (which is no A pc), is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2, as amended); every other kernel pc lifts, as a delivery state when the kernel is parked and as a `run` state when it is between instructions (6e). Until 6e lands, a de Bruijn kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` as a documented limit of the backends, not of the carrier.\n   210\t\n   211\t**§5.4 law 4**: \"one program at one **instruction boundary** (its parking boundaries included) lifts to row sets whose root ids are equal …\". Law 1, 2, 6: add \"at every reachable pc of the stepping driver (§5.7)\".\n   212\t\n   213\t**§5.6**, generalise items 3 and 5 and add 10:\n   214\t\n   215\t> 3. … at every reachable pc `p` (not only boundary pcs), `virt(p, s)` is single-valued on the physical image of `L(p)`; the existing `live-out*(p)` clause stays for parked entries.\n   216\t> 5. … `(set (stack-layout p)) ∪ (set (uses v[p])) = L(p)` for every reachable non-tail pc; `(set (uses v[p])) = L(p)` for every reachable tail-call pc; the parked-boundary form `(set (stack-layout p)) = saved(p+1, rd)` stays.\n   217\t> 10. Stepping: for every case the reference lane's step count to the first park and to the halt is pinned per host; the three lanes agree on it after the stack lane's `:push` steps are subtracted (`correspondence`).\n   218\t\n   219\t**New §5.7 \"The stepping driver\"**: the text of §4.1 item 4 below, verbatim.\n   220\t\n   221\t**§7.1 steps 1-2**, replace:\n   222\t\n   223\t> 1. **Quiescence.** Refuse `:yin.k/not-quiescent` on a non-empty ready queue; refuse the holds … as `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`; refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` when `:control` is set and the wait set is not empty.\n   224\t> 2. **Kind.** `:running` when `:control` is set; else `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked table holds, else `:blocked` on a non-empty wait set, else `:halted` with an empty queue; anything else is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a machine with no control, no wait, not halted: unreachable by construction).\n   225\t\n   226\tand in step 4 add \"a `:running` root's state node is the control's `run` state with the window trimmed to `L(pc)` (`select-keys`), contributing the context `(A, pc, E)` to discharge (`live-pcs` from `pc`)\". Delete \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set\" from the `canonical-task` paragraph and write \"a kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` until 6e, then a `run` state through `lift-running`\".\n   227\t\n   228\t**§7.2**, restore: \"`:running`: `:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set []`; nothing runs; `vm/run` continues from `pc`\". Add \"**§7.3 Custody of a running root**\": the content of §2.4 above in design voice (slot by kind table gains the row `root | running | exclusive | the four keys plus origin on a successor`; `check-custody!` runs the grant checks; `protect!` is vacuous; the exporting machine keeps `:control` and the kernel refuses to step it under `:exporting`/`:ended`).\n   229\t\n   230\t**§8.3**, item 12: \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel at a `:push` pc of H or outside every body; a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts as a `run` state (6e; until then the backend refusal is pinned as a limit)\". Add §8.6 (6d-a), §8.7 (6d-b), §8.8 (6e) = §4 of this report.\n   231\t\n   232\t**§9**: add \"**Claims that are checks**: `L(p)` is sufficient at every pc (law 1 at every step); injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc (item 3); the stack equation at every pc (item 5).\"\n   233\t\n   234\t**§11 Q6** becomes live and should be answered in 6d-a: with any-pc lifts, a lift **before** a constant-dead branch is routine, and `live-pcs` (edge-reachability, no constant propagation) refuses `ghost` there. Recommendation: keep \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\" (the owner's \"derive, don't persist\" and the linker's conservative discharge both argue for it), and make the harness's `ghost` case assert the refusal at the pre-branch pc as a pinned, documented false refusal.\n   235\t\n   236\t### 3.2 `yin.vm.semantic.md` (frozen design)\n   237\t\n   238\t**§4.1 first paragraph**, replace \"A safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\" with:\n   239\t\n   240\t> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** The machine's state between any two instructions, `⟨seg, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, is canonical and lifts as the `run` state (continuation design §1.3): preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm` (owner, 2026-10-17). A **safepoint** is the delivery-bearing special case: a transition at which the machine parks by itself, carrying a `deliver` record and, except for an explicit park, a pending wait. The table below is the table of those parking transitions; it is not the set of liftable pcs, which is every pc of every body. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n   241\t\n   242\t**§4.2**, after the tail state add the run state (as §3.1's keyed form) and amend \"The validator … site/pc pairing (`pc` is a safepoint successor of the right kind for `reason`; …)\" to \"(for an `act`, `pc` is a safepoint successor …; for a `run`, `pc` is any pc of a body and the window is exactly `L(pc)`)\". Row carrier sentence: add \"`:run [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`\".\n   243\t\n   244\t**§4.3**, replace \"The reconstruction obligation is: for an `act` state, …; for a `tail` state, …\" with the two existing clauses plus:\n   245\t\n   246\t> and for a `run` state at pc `p`, produce the segment `A`, `p`, every register of `L(p)`, `E`, `K` with each frame's validated window and `rd`, and no delivery record or pending; a physical location outside `L(p)`'s image is never read, and an engine that cannot hold a value of `L(p)` refuses before attachment (the register kernel's `:continuation-registers`). `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` holds for admitted canonical frames of all three states.\n   247\t\n   248\t**§10 phase 6** (record): append \"*(Amended 2026-10-16: the phase-6 harness proved the delivery states only; slices 6d and 6e extend the gate to every instruction boundary under the owner's any-pc invariant.)*\"\n   249\t\n   250\t### 3.3 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n   251\t\n   252\t**Blocker 1 (line 17)** \"The safepoint set must be exactly the machine's parking transitions\" → append \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc):** the parking transitions are where the machine stops by itself; the set of liftable configurations is every instruction boundary of the register-shaped machine (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1), and the safepoint table enumerates the delivery states within it.\"\n   253\t\n   254\t**§7.4.1 title and rule.** Title → \"7.4.1 Every instruction boundary lifts; the safepoint table is the machine's parking transitions\". Replace \"UCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be lifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the reference machine at which the machine parks.\" with:\n   255\t\n   256\t> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17).** The register-shaped machine has no such interior: its state between any two instructions is `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, canonical and liftable (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1; continuation design §1.3). **A continuation may be lifted at any instruction boundary**; a *safepoint* is a boundary at which the machine parks by itself and carries a delivery record and a pending wait. The paragraph above describes the stack-shaped machine, which left at phase 8.\n   257\t\n   258\tThe keyed-form block gains the `run` state. **Quiescence** paragraph: append \"A machine between instructions (`:yin.k/state :run`, root kind `:running`) is quiescent when its ready queue is empty; its wait set is empty by construction.\"\n   259\t\n   260\t**§7.4.2**, rules: \"`lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on **every pc of A** the engine reaches and undefined only at pcs that are no A pc (a `:push` of the stack image) or outside every body.\" Replace the `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` bullet with:\n   261\t\n   262\t> - A request to lift at a pc that is no pc of A (a stack image's `:push`, a pc outside every body) returns `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc; the name is historical, the meaning is \"no canonical state exists here\". An engine may not invent states a record does not have, and two engines that disagree about the state set of one address are not both conforming. An engine that cannot hold a value of `L(pc)` at some pc refuses before attachment with its own kind (the register backend's `:continuation-registers`); that is a limit of the engine, not of the address.\n   263\t\n   264\tLast paragraph: \"a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of the safepoint set by construction\" → \"a debugger's breakpoint set is any subset of the pcs of A; the safepoint set is where the program will stop by itself\".\n   265\t\n   266\t**§7.7.4** step 3 \"Neutralize control: the record's control and K are already absent (the machine is parked or blocked)\" → \"Neutralize control: a parked or blocked machine has no control and no K; a machine between instructions (root kind `:running`) keeps its `:control`, `:window`, `:env` and `:k` as the state the lift reads, and the kernel refuses to step a machine whose gate is `:exporting` or `:ended` (`yin.vm.semantic/vm-hot`), so the exporting machine is pure data in both cases; direct `:resume` …\". In the \"(M-next D8/D9, as built.)\" paragraph, after \"under `:running` internal computation proceeds\" add \"for the holder's fuel budget per program tick (`driver/run-cycle`, `:fuel`), so a long computation is preempted at an instruction boundary and may be exported there\".\n   267\t\n   268\t**§7.8 Lift** step 1 → \"The machine is parked, blocked, or between instructions (`:running`), with a local record `r`, and its ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\" Step 3 → \"`lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at an instruction boundary of A (§7.4.1), else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; …\". Step 9 → \"At the next park, halt, or preemption boundary, produce the successor occurrence …\". §7.7.6 (line 2161) \"runs to its next safepoint\" → \"runs to its next park, halt, or preemption boundary\".\n   269\t\n   270\t**§7.9** row: `| :yin.k/not-at-safepoint | lift | :yin.k/segment, :yin.k/pc: the pc is no pc of A (a :push of a stack image, or outside every body) |`.\n   271\t\n   272\t**§7.10** \"No implicit control flow\": \"Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define\" → \"Resume happens only at an instruction boundary the canonical vector defines (a parking transition, or the boundary a holder's fuel budget stopped at); the successor chain …\".\n   273\t\n   274\t**§7.11 Safepoint conformance harness**: rename \"Continuation conformance harness\"; \"to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table — effect-producing calls included —\" → \"to **every instruction boundary** (the stepping driver, continuation design §5.7), the complete §7.4.1 table's parking transitions included,\"; the cross-engine sentence likewise \"one program at one instruction boundary\". **§7.11.1** Invariant → \"At every instruction boundary, lifting and lowering preserve the next observable step (and, at a parking transition, its wait); a pc that is no pc of A refuses.\" Setup: \"a nonsafepoint pc\" → \"a `:push` pc of the stack image and a body-start pc\"; expected: \"An undeclared pc gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`\" → \"A pc that is no pc of A gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; every pc of A lifts as a `run` state when the machine is between instructions\".\n   275\t\n   276\t### 3.4 `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`\n   277\t\n   278\t**§1 stance item 1**: append \"Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc): a *safepoint* is a parking `(segment-address, pc)`; the carrier expresses every `(segment-address, pc)` of A as a `:run` state when the machine is between instructions.\" **§5.2**: add the `:run` row; `:wait`'s note \"an `:act` or a `:tail` row, never a `:run`\". **§5.5**: kind comment and the kind-rules paragraph as §3.1 (`:running` has exactly one frame, a `:run`, …); custody table row `root | running | exclusive | the four keys plus origin on a successor` and `root | running | fork | nil`. **§8 step 2**: unchanged text, the §5.5 rules now include `:running`. **§9 Lift**: \"refuse unless quiescent\" stays; \"quiescence and safepoint, kind\" → \"quiescence, kind\"; **Cross-engine** law: \"Running the corpus to each safepoint\" → \"to each instruction boundary\". **§11**: \"Code identity (A), safepoints,\" → \"Code identity (A), the parking transitions,\".\n   279\t\n   280\t### 3.5 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md` §7.1\n   281\t\n   282\tThe document is historical (versions 0-2), but §7.1 states a principle. Replace \"Only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks at the existing engine export boundary are eligible. Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth kind.\" with:\n   283\t\n   284\t> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** For the stack-shaped machine of versions 0-2, only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks were eligible and running snapshots were not a fourth kind. For the register-shaped machine and body version 3 this is reversed by the owner's invariant: a task between instructions is the fourth kind, `:running` (continuation design §1.3, §2.6), and is eligible for export and custody.\n   285\t\n   286\t### 3.6 `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` §14.1\n   287\t\n   288\tTable 14.1.1: add the row `| Between instructions (:running) | No active wait; the window is exactly L(pc); continue at pc. |`. Line 3827 \"Validate the safepoint and empty ready queue\" → \"Validate the instruction boundary (§7.4.1 as amended) and empty ready queue\". Line 3900-3901 \"A non-safepoint pc and queued work yield the existing not-at-safepoint and not-quiescent outcomes\" → \"A pc that is no pc of A and queued work yield …\". Line 4145 as UCF §7.8 step 9.\n   289\t\n   290\t### 3.7 `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`\n   291\t\n   292\tNew dated entry I-9: \"2026-10-16, any-pc. The lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) in violation of the owner's primary invariant (2026-10-17); slice 6d adds the `:run` state, root kind `:running`, validator rules R1-R3, custody for running roots and the harness at every pc; §8's 'check the pc against the static safepoint set' is the stack-shaped machine's rule and is historical.\" No compatibility promise.\n   293\t\n   294\t### 3.8 The assessment's §F wording\n   295\t\n   296\tReplace its disagreement 3 (\"Safe points are a scoping choice, not a necessity\") with: \"Safepoints are the parking transitions, a proper subset of the liftable boundaries, by the owner's invariant; the one necessity kept is the ready-queue refusal (§2.5)\". Its amendment text is superseded by §3.1-3.3 above. Its verdict line (\"NOT READY … Q1 … Q2\") is superseded by this report.\n   297\t\n   298\t---\n   299\t\n   300\t## 4. Slice breakdown, refreshed\n   301\t\n   302\tShape per the phase 7 plan. Hosts: JVM, Node, Dart for every slice. Verification per `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`: iterate on `bb test:clj`; the three-lane quick tier at landing (`bb test:sub yin.vm`; confirm \"Testing yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test\" and the other touched namespaces in the Node log); the slow tier once at landing of 6d-a and once at 6e (the every-pc laws are slow-tier). Every refusal test asserts zero serves (lift) or zero attaches (lower), the file's existing discipline.\n   303\t\n   304\t### 4.1 Slice 6d-a: the reference lane\n   305\t\n   306\t**Change.**\n   307\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc`: state `:run` in S1 (`:96-98`); `required` for a run `[:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/k]` with no optional key; C2 (`:117-123`): `(pos? n)` only for act and return frames; C3 (`:124-127`): skip the frame itself when run; `window-defect` (`:49-61`) takes the wanted set (`saved` or `live`) and omits the `:window-self` clause for a run; context `:running` in P1 (`:162-164`); A1-A6 and T1 skipped for a run; everything else unchanged. **R0** (the malformed-machine rule) lives in the lift, not the validator.\n   308\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`: `row-table` (`:49-69`): `:run [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k]]`; the task's slot 13 becomes `[:refs :wait :run]` (recommended; §5 decision 3); `lift-task*` (`:1650-1698`): the kind step as §3.1 §7.1 steps 1-2, the `:running` state node (`:control` + `(select-keys (:window m) (analysis/live (:analysis image) pc))` + `:env` + `(or (:k m) [])`, through `context!` for discharge), slot 13 `[run-cref]` for the kind; `state-node` (`:1508-1518`) gains the run arm (no `:site`, no `:deliver`); `validate-frames` (`:1109-1144`) judges slot 13's `:run` in context `:running`; `check-pendings!` (`:1877-1882`) `:running` arm; `task-ops` `:1261`, `custody-baseline` `:1297`, `header-custody` `:1704-1716` admit `:running` (the grammar half; the holder half is 6d-b); `restore-task` (`:2264-2267`) `:running` arm; `rows->frame`/`frame->rows` (`:1023-1058`, `:1082`) the run shape; `lower-defect`'s `state-prim` (`layout.cljc:863-874`) gets a `:run` arm (window plus lexical bindings, as `:act`), so a primitive in a running window refuses into the register backend before attach even before 6e lands.\n   309\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc`: public `run-fuel` (§2.2); no change to `vm-hot`'s loop.\n   310\t- Tests: `test/yin/vm/semantic/frame_test.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (the stepping driver, laws at every pc, the quick-tier sample, tampers, the refusal rows), `test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc` (the two static checks at every pc: no kernel involved, so it belongs here and not in 6e).\n   311\t- Docs: §3.1 (continuation design), §3.2 (frozen §4.1-4.3), §3.3 UCF §7.4.1-7.4.2, §7.9; §3.4 tuples §1, §5.2, §5.5, §9; §3.5; §3.7; a known-gaps row (§6).\n   312\t\n   313\t**Depends on.** Nothing in flight. Module-pins commit 2 is independent (§1.4).\n   314\t\n   315\t**Acceptance.**\n   316\t1. `frame-test/run-state-rules`: a `:run` built from `run-fuel` at every pc of `:worked-example` validates in context `:running`; one tamper per rule: C2′ (a float pc → `:pc-kind`; `pc = (count v)` → `:pc-bounds`); a **body-start pc validates** (today's C2 would refuse it); R1 (a key `≥ k(body)` → `:window-scope`); R2 (one live key removed → `:window-missing`; one dead register added → `:window-extra`); R3 (a deliver key → `:frame-shape`); P1 (`:run` with a pending → `:pending-presence`; `:run` in context `:wait` → `:pending-presence`; an `:act` in context `:running` → `:pending-presence`); an `act` at `p+1` and a `run` at `p+1` with `rd` present both validate and each is refused under the other's kind.\n   317\t2. `rows-test/run-row-bijection`: `frame->rows ∘ rows->frame` identity on every `:run` of item 1; `:run` is five slots; a sixth slot is `:row-shape` at decode step 2 (raw row edit with rehash); a `:wait` whose state cref names a `:run` row is `:undecodable` at step 3.\n   318\t3. `rows-test/running-kind`: a machine stopped by `run-fuel` mid-body lifts to kind `:running` with one `:run` frame, no waits, no pending and nil custody; lowering restores `:control` at the pc with the trimmed window and runs nothing (`:control` set, `(:value m)` nil, zero steps observed through the toy channel); `vm/run` then reaches the reference result and effect trace; the relift's root equals the lift's under `canonical-under` in a fresh receiver (law 2); a `:running` root with two frames, with a `:wait` frame, with a pending, with a non-empty wait set, with a parked-id or with a result is `:yin.k/undecodable :kind` (or `:parked-id`) before any attachment (attach count zero); a machine with `:control` set **and** a hand-added wait entry is refused at lift `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` with zero serves (R0).\n   319\t4. `harness/every-pc-matches-the-reference` (`^:slow`, `slow/guard`): the **stepping driver**: for each of the 89 cases build the world's program on the reference lane and step it by `run-fuel 1` (not `vm/step`, which emits telemetry per step) until the park, satisfy, continue stepping to the halt. At every `S_i` with `:control` set: lift → lower into a fresh machine (same composition, streams served as today) → `vm/run` to the end → **law 1** (result and effect trace equal the reference) → relift → **law 2** (root equality under `canonical-under`) → **law 6** (every frame validates; the window tampered by one live key removed or one dead register added is refused `:window-missing`/`:window-extra`). Law 3 does not apply (no pending). The step count to the park and to the halt is pinned per case (rows 1 and 22 under the four wrappers: 13/15/28/40 and 17/19/32/44 to the park, from the assessment's JVM probe; re-measure once and pin) and the total point count is asserted. The assessment's probe numbers (2,986 reachable pcs, 784 parking, roughly 2,500-3,500 pre-park points) are the size estimate; the pinned numbers are whatever the first green run measures.\n   320\t5. `harness/sampled-pcs-match-the-reference` (quick tier): per bare row (22 rows) five structural pcs: the first body-start reached, the pc after the first primitive `:var` (a host function live in the window), the pc before the parking instruction, the first `:jump` or `:branch-false` reached (rows 15, 21), the pc of the last `:return` before the halt; plus every step of rows 1 and 20 (tail, empty `K`); laws 1, 2, 6. About 150 points.\n   321\t6. `harness/run-refusals`: a non-empty ready queue after a hand-run `check-wait-set` is still `:yin.k/not-quiescent` with `:control` nil (today's test, `:1057-1066`) **and** with `:control` set (the two-waiter machine after `resume-from-run-queue` popped one entry and `run-fuel 1` ran one instruction of it); the same machine after `vm/run` to the next park lifts; a `:run` window holding a cursor-ref, a reified continuation and a nil-valued live register round-trips (rows 14, 16, 22 at the pc after the capture or the nil load); the `ghost` case (`:1330-1352`'s family) lifted at the pc **before** the constant-dead branch refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{ghost}}` (Q6 pinned as a documented false refusal).\n   322\t7. `layout-test/static-checks-at-every-pc`: A.3 (`virt` single-valued over `L(p)`'s physical image at every reachable pc of `layout-corpus` and the matrix's vectors, through `phys`) and A.4 (`(set (stack-layout p)) ∪ (set (uses v[p])) = L(p)` at every reachable non-tail pc; `(set (uses v[p])) = L(p)` at every reachable tail-call pc); the sizes asserted (reachable pcs per corpus segment).\n   323\t8. `semantic-vm-test/run-fuel`: `run-fuel n` on an active machine equals `n` applications of `vm-hot … 1` (state equality by `=` on the record minus telemetry fields); on a blocked machine it is one scheduler round.\n   324\t9. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log shows the four test namespaces; the slow tier run once at landing on three hosts.\n   325\t\n   326\t**Days.** 2 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = 3.5; docs 1 beside it.\n   327\t\n   328\t### 4.2 Slice 6d-b: holder and custody wiring for running roots\n   329\t\n   330\t**Can it ship with 6d-a?** Yes, as the second commit on the same branch, reviewed in the same gate. Reasons: (a) it edits only files 6d-a does not touch except `rows.cljc`'s `check-custody!` (`:2039`) which 6d-a leaves alone; (b) without it a `:running` root is liftable and lowerable as a fork but **not** exportable by a holder, offerable by the grantor or admitted by the ledger, so the invariant is honoured only for forks: a half state the owner should not be left in; (c) its tests reuse 6d-a's `running-kind` fixtures. Ship it before any composition exports a running root. If the review of 6d-a runs long, 6d-b lands as its own merge the same week.\n   331\t\n   332\t**Change.**\n   333\t1. `driver.cljc` `run-cycle` (`:2111-2144`): `:fuel` config key (checked in `check-common!`, positive integer or nil); `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under the existing gate and halted guards; `with-status` detail `:yin.k/at-boundary true` after a cycle that returned a machine with `:control` set.\n   334\t2. `export.cljc` `kind-of` (`:274-282`): `(some? (:control m)) :running` first.\n   335\t3. `semantic.cljc` `vm-hot` entry: `(when (contains? #{:exporting :ended} (vm/gate-mode machine)) (fail …))` before the loop; `run-fuel` inherits it. `register-restore` is unaffected (restore delivers nothing and the gate's `:resume` refusal is upstream).\n   336\t4. `rows.cljc` `check-custody!` (`:2039`): grant checks for `:running` (drop `(= :halted kind)` from the skip only; keep `fenced?`). `ledger.cljc:277`, `authority/grant.cljc:74-75`: admit `:running`.\n   337\t5. Docstrings: `driver.cljc:74-77` (`:safepoint` phase: \"the machine has nothing left to drive, **or** the composition armed the exit over a machine at a park or an instruction boundary\"), `:318-321`, `:1119-1123`; `compose.cljc:798-799`, `:842`; `export.cljc` header.\n   338\t6. Tests: `driver_test`: a fuelled run preempts a long pure loop (row 1's `(fn [x] …)` nested under a `(loop …)`-shaped segment of a few thousand steps) at a boundary; the phase stays `:running` and the next tick continues; `hand-off` on it mints the successor, journals, freezes and offers a `:running` pack; a candidate lowers it (`step-validating` → awaiting-grant → grant → `activate`) and reaches the reference result; `rehydrate-fenced` on the frozen recovery reproduces the pack byte for byte and the rehydrated machine refuses `vm/step` (\"Gate is closed\"). `ledger_test`/`grant_test`: a `:running` baseline is offerable; a `:halted` one is not (unchanged). `compose_rows_test`: an exclusive world whose source is handed off at a boundary completes on the successor (law 1 through the composition). `stage_e_scenarios`: one scenario `[:until \"h1\" :running n] [:hand-off \"h1\"]` with fuel set so the hand-off lands mid-body; recovery cut before and after the offer.\n   339\t7. Docs: §3.3 UCF §7.7.4, §7.8, §7.10, §7.11, §7.11.1; §3.6 linker.dht §14.1; continuation §7.3.\n   340\t\n   341\t**Depends on.** 6d-a (the kind and row).\n   342\t\n   343\t**Days.** 1.5 implementation + 1 review (2 rounds) = 2.5.\n   344\t\n   345\t### 4.3 Slice 6e: the cross-engine half\n   346\t\n   347\t**Change.** `layout.cljc`: `lift-running`/`lower-running` reading and writing the kernels' live fields (register `{:pc :registers :frames :continuation :store-of}`, stack `{:pc :stack :frames :continuation :store-of}`); `virt` over a given virtual set (`L(p)` for a run; today's `live-out*(p)` for parked entries, `:224-237`); `register-window` (`:458-461`) parameterised; the `:push` rule (`h->a` `:333-336`: a stack-kernel snapshot at a `:push` is the snapshot at its successor, which is never another `:push`); the tail-pc rule (at a tail-call pc the stack kernel holds the dead `stack-layout(p)` below `[f & args]`: the lift drops it, the lower refills it with nil, checked unobservable by item 8 generalised); the inverse fills `registers` to the body's count with nil outside `L(p)` (as `write-back`, `register.cljc`); `safepoint!` (`:318-330`) gains a run mode accepting any pc inside a body; `canonical-task` (`:724-725`) lifts an active kernel as `:running`; `kernel-task` (`:788-814`) lowers a `:running` machine into a running kernel. No kernel edit (the layouts read fields the kernels expose; the harness already reads them at `:1899-1904`). `layout_test.cljc`, the harness: laws 4 and 5 at every pc on the 61 lane cases.\n   348\t\n   349\t**Acceptance.** Mirrors §8.3 items 10-12 at every pc: law 4 (one root id on three lanes at step `i`, the stack lane's step index minus its `:push` steps, `correspondence` `:97-108`); law 5 (six ordered pairs at every pc, each continuing to the reference result) with **the register-target refusal allowlist**: a lowering into the register kernel at a pc where a primitive function is in `L(pc)` is `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` before any attach, and the harness asserts the refusal count per case **equals** the computed primitive-live pc set (every row loads `cat`/`conj`, so the set is non-empty on every row; the assertion is what keeps law 5 from degenerating to \"refused everywhere\"); the `:push` rule and the tail-pc rule each pinned on one row; `layout-refusals` item 12 rewritten: a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc **lifts** as a run state on both lanes with zero serves refused; a hand-built entry at a `:push` pc and one outside every body still refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`. The fragment is not widened (rows 7-12 and 17 excluded; row 4 not lowered into a backend).\n   350\t\n   351\t**Depends on.** 6d-a. Independent of 6d-b.\n   352\t\n   353\t**Days.** 2.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = 4.\n   354\t\n   355\t### 4.4 Node and Dart considerations (all slices)\n   356\t\n   357\t- **Determinism of the stepping driver.** A is host-independent and `vm-hot` executes one instruction per fuel unit on every host, so the step count to a park is identical across hosts; the pinned counts of item 4 are asserted on all three. The one host-visible difference is the effect trace of streams served by the toy channel, which the existing harness already normalises (`normalize`, `:238`).\n   358\t- **Dart**: `for` over a seq of thousands of snapshot points can hand the body nil (memory `project_cljd_for_chunked_nil`); the driver uses `loop`/`reduce`. Frames and windows start from `{}`, never `(assoc nil :a 1 :b 2)` (`project_cljd_multikey_assoc_nil`). The every-pc laws are the slowest thing in the tree on Dart; they run only under `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` (the `slow/guard` wrapper) and the quick tier samples. `(- x)` on a float is `+0.0` on Dart: no floats in the driver.\n   359\t- **Node**: `pc` comparisons through `cbor/numeric-kind` on wire values (the C2′ float tamper is the canary); a `:run` window with `[:lit nil]` must survive `select-keys` on JS (membership by `contains?`, never `(get W r)`).\n   360\t- Fresh worktree: `mise trust`, `npm ci` before the Node lane.\n   361\t\n   362\t### 4.5 Routing and review rounds\n   363\t\n   364\t- **Implementation**: codex (gpt-6.1-sol, workspace-write, stdin closed), one brief per slice, foreground lanes only, report inside the worktree's `collab/`. 6d-a and 6d-b to the same codex thread on one branch (`srvm-6d`); 6e on `srvm-6e` after 6d-a merges.\n   365\t- **Review**: Fable (non-author) at every gate; Q6 rule applies (stop on satisfied gates; P0/P1 or an in-scope P2 in claimed acceptance blocks; revert-not-fix outside scope; a disputed P1 goes to a third-family Architect, glm or cmd). glm/cmd as fallback reviewers when Fable is unavailable.\n   366\t- **Rounds**: 6d-a 3 (validator and row grammar; the stepping driver and its pinned counts; three-host lanes), 6d-b 2 (driver and custody surfaces; Stage E scenario), 6e 3 (layouts' running mode; the allowlist equality; three-host lanes), docs 1.\n   367\t- **Commit** on Architect sign-off before the commit, merge and push per the standing rule; never commit `collab/` or `docs/agents/routing-status.md`.\n   368\t\n   369\t### 4.6 Effort\n   370\t\n   371\t| Slice | Implementation days | Review rounds | Days total |\n   372\t|---|---|---|---|\n   373\t| 6d-a reference lane | 2 | 3 | 3.5 |\n   374\t| 6d-b holder/custody | 1.5 | 2 | 2.5 |\n   375\t| 6e cross-engine | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n   376\t| docs (§3), split across the three | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n   377\t| **Total** | **7** | | **11**, or 7 without 6e |\n   378\t\n   379\tUp from the assessment's 8.5 by 6d-b (which did not exist before phase 7) and the fuel driver.\n   380\t\n   381\t---\n   382\t\n   383\t## 5. Risks, and what the owner must still decide\n   384\t\n   385\t**Risks.**\n   386\t1. **A liveness bug surfaces at a pc no park ever looked at.** If `L(p)` under-approximates anywhere, law 1 fails at that pc. That is a finding, not a slice risk; it is the main reason to run the every-pc laws. Mitigation: none wanted.\n   387\t2. **Register-target refusals dominate law 5 (6e).** Mitigation: the allowlist equality assertion (§4.3).\n   388\t3. **Slow-tier time.** Thousands of lift/lower/run triples on Dart. Mitigation: slow tier only; the quick sample; measure once at 6d-a landing and record the wall time in the ledger row.\n   389\t4. **Fuel too small or too large.** Too small and the holder's per-tick overhead dominates; too large and preemption is late. The driver cannot know; it is a composition knob (decision 1). Mitigation: a default of nil (today's behaviour) so nothing changes until a composition opts in.\n   390\t5. **The `vm-hot` gate guard** is an engine edit outside the hot loop; a reviewer must confirm it costs one map read per `run-fuel` call, not per instruction.\n   391\t6. **Module-pins commit 2** lands between 6d-a and 6e and re-mints every root; 6d's own fixtures pin step counts and refusal kinds, not root ids, so the re-mint is a rebase, not a redo. Keep it so in the briefs.\n   392\t7. **Row grammar choice** (decision 3): reusing slot 13 for the `:run` cref keeps every existing root id; a new slot re-mints every fixture in the tree. Recommended: reuse.\n   393\t\n   394\t**Decisions for the owner** (none blocks 6d-a; defaults stated so work proceeds):\n   395\t1. **Fuel as a holder config key**, default nil (no change for existing compositions). Recommended as stated.\n   396\t2. **No automatic export at the lease bound**; `hand-off` stays the composition's explicit act; the driver only reports `:yin.k/at-boundary`. Recommended; the alternative (the driver arms the exit when tenure is below one renewal interval) is a composition policy that belongs in `compose`, not the driver, and can come later without changing the carrier.\n   397\t3. **The `:run` cref lives in root slot 13** (`frames`, `[:refs :wait :run]`), kind rule \"exactly one, a `:run`\"; no new slot. Recommended.\n   398\t4. **Q6** (continuation §11): keep edge-reachability liveness and pin the pre-branch false refusal (§4.1 item 6). Recommended; a \"no\" means a constant-propagation pass in `live-pcs`, which is a new static analysis and a separate slice.\n   399\t\n   400\t---\n   401\t\n   402\t## 6. Reclassification, and every sentence that asserts the safepoint-only rule\n   403\t\n   404\t**Plainly:** the gap list (`collab/1793430000000-architect-gap-list.report.md`, §1.2 and row G7) and the orchestrator log (`docs/orchestrator-log.md:11501`, `:11514`, \"slice 6d (any-pc snapshots) … capabilities, not defects\") classified the absence of any-pc snapshots as a **capability gap**: \"Risk of leaving it: none to correctness\". The owner's ruling reclassifies it as a **violation of a primary invariant**: the continuation format cannot express a state the kernel is in between any two instructions, and the holder cannot pause a computation except where the computation stops by itself. Severity P1; owner seat `yin.vm.semantic` (the register-VM track); closing slices 6d-a, 6d-b, 6e. The orchestrator's next log entry should say so, and `docs/agents/known-gaps.md` should gain the row:\n   405\t\n   406\t> | the lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`), the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc:2120`) and every custody kind table lists three kinds: the carrier cannot express, and the holder cannot pause at, an arbitrary pc | owner invariant 2026-10-17; Architect plan `collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md` | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | 6d-a, 6d-b, 6e | P1 |\n   407\t\n   408\t**Every sentence to amend or delete** (grep over `docs/`, `src/`, `test/` for `safepoint`, `not-at-safepoint`, `:blocked :parked`, today). The `yang.safepoint` stage, `yang.python.antlr.safepoint`, their tests and `yang.antlr.md` §8.5.2 use \"safepoint\" for **guest-visible** event delivery (signals, tracing, green-thread switches) and are not in this list: delivery at an arbitrary pc would be implicit control flow visible to the guest; migration at an arbitrary pc is invisible to the guest. One sentence in `yang.antlr.md` (line 1340-1341, \"arbitrary-point asynchrony … The signal is an event on a stream, polled at safepoints\") stays true and should gain a footnote making the distinction.\n   409\t\n   410\t**Design documents.**\n   411\t\n   412\t| File:line | Sentence | Disposition |\n   413\t|---|---|---|\n   414\t| `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:17` | \"The safepoint set must be exactly the machine's parking transitions\" | amend, §3.3 |\n   415\t| `:264-266` (§7.2.1, historical) | \"A frame's resume pc is a static safepoint whose kinds admit the pending's reason\" | historical section; add \"(versions 0-2; version 3 admits `:run` states at every pc)\" |\n   416\t| `:656` title, `:668-674` | \"Only safepoints lift\"; \"a continuation may be lifted only at a safepoint\" | replace, §3.3 |\n   417\t| `:762-765` | Quiescence paragraph | append, §3.3 |\n   418\t| `:810-812`, `:815-821`, `:823-825` | \"total on safepoints and undefined elsewhere\"; \"not a safepoint … :not-at-safepoint … may not invent safepoints\"; \"breakpoint set is a subset of the safepoint set\" | replace, §3.3 |\n   419\t| `:1901-1903` | \"the record's control and K are already absent (the machine is parked or blocked)\" | replace, §3.3 |\n   420\t| `:1941-1942` | \"A task is not at a liftable safepoint while it holds any of these\" | keep (holds still refuse); reword \"liftable\" → \"liftable boundary\" |\n   421\t| `:2161` | \"runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\" | amend, §3.3 |\n   422\t| `:2621-2623`, `:2627-2628`, `:2673` | §7.8 steps 1, 3, 9 | replace, §3.3 |\n   423\t| `:2690` | outcome row | redefine, §3.3 |\n   424\t| `:2839` | \"Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define\" | replace, §3.3 |\n   425\t| `:2931-2942` | harness blocker | rename and replace, §3.3 |\n   426\t| `:3100-3103`, `:3109`, `:3129-3130` | 7.11.1 invariant; \"a nonsafepoint pc\"; \"An undeclared pc gives :not-at-safepoint\" | replace, §3.3 |\n   427\t| `yin.vm.semantic.md:659` | \"A safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks.\" | replace, §3.2 |\n   428\t| `:680-689` | explicit park / reified / quiescence | keep; add the run state sentence |\n   429\t| `:733` | \"pc is a safepoint successor of the right kind for reason\" | amend (act only), §3.2 |\n   430\t| `:765-790` | §4.3 reconstruction obligation (act, tail) | add the run clause, §3.2 |\n   431\t| `:1134-1154` | §10 phase 6 record | append the note, §3.2 |\n   432\t| `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:142-147` | §0.2 \"one canonical frame … act or tail\" | replace, §3.1 |\n   433\t| `:178-205` | §1.1 \"Two closed state shapes\" | add `run`, §3.1 |\n   434\t| `:216-233` | §1.2 keyed forms | add, §3.1 |\n   435\t| `:293-305`, `:381`, `:392` | §2.3 table; §2.6 kind and frames | add, §3.1 |\n   436\t| `:473` (S1), `:483` (C2), `:484` (C3), `:528` (P1) | the rules | amend, §3.1 |\n   437\t| `:568` | \"frame from its state at a safepoint; lift(lower(frame)) = frame\" | \"at any instruction boundary\" |\n   438\t| `:1020-1025` | §4.5 \"never invents a safepoint … :not-at-safepoint\" | replace, §3.1 |\n   439\t| `:1122` | §5.1 \"lift at the safepoint\" | \"lift at every instruction boundary and at every safepoint\" |\n   440\t| `:1333-1335` | law 4 \"one program at one safepoint\" | amend, §3.1 |\n   441\t| `:1424-1436`, `:1450` | §5.6 items 2, 3, 5, 9 \"reachable parking pc / boundary pc / non-tail safepoint\" | generalise, §3.1 |\n   442\t| `:1590`, `:1613`, `:1626-1630`, `:1651-1658` | §7.1 \"refuse unless quiescent\"; `canonical-task` refuses `:not-at-safepoint`; steps 1-2 | replace, §3.1 |\n   443\t| `:2148-2149` | §8.3 item 12 \"stepped to a non-safepoint pc\" | replace, §3.1 |\n   444\t| `:2263-2268` | §9 checks | add, §3.1 |\n   445\t| `:2792-2797` | §11 Q6 \"transports only because the lift happens at a safepoint after the branch\" | answer, §3.1 |\n   446\t| `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:45-49` | \"Cross-VM safepoints are semantic-VM safepoints … a safepoint is (segment-address, pc)\" | append, §3.4 |\n   447\t| `:304-309`, `:433`, `:444`, `:455-460`, `:466-472` | §5.2 table, §5.5 kind, frames, kind rules, custody table | add, §3.4 |\n   448\t| `:875`, `:880`, `:929-933`, `:1027` | §9 lift; cross-engine law; §11 | amend, §3.4 |\n   449\t| `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md:564-566` | \"Only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks … Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth kind.\" | replace, §3.5 |\n   450\t| `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:602-603` | \"The lift driver must check the pc against the static safepoint set\" | historical; new entry I-9, §3.7 |\n   451\t| `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:3777-3788`, `:3798`, `:3827`, `:3900-3901`, `:4145` | 14.1.1 table; \"pc eligibility\"; \"Validate the safepoint\"; \"A non-safepoint pc\"; \"At the next safepoint or halt\" | amend, §3.6 |\n   452\t| `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:74`, `:780`; `yin.vm.linker.md:47`, `:2460`; `yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1544`; `yin.vm.semantic.derivations.md:653` | mentions of \"safepoints\" as a named concept | keep (they name the concept, not the rule) |\n   453\t\n   454\t**Ledgers and logs.**\n   455\t\n   456\t| Where | Sentence | Disposition |\n   457\t|---|---|---|\n   458\t| `collab/1793430000000-architect-gap-list.report.md` §1.2, row G7, §\"Verdict\" | \"capability gap only\"; \"none to correctness\"; \"owner-gated capability questions\" | superseded by this report (collab is never committed; the log entry records the supersession) |\n   459\t| `docs/orchestrator-log.md:11501`, `:11514` | \"slice 6d (any-pc snapshots) … (capabilities, not defects)\" | append-only; the next seat entry records the reclassification and cites this report |\n   460\t| `docs/agents/known-gaps.md` | no 6d row | add the row above |\n   461\t| `collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md` §F, Verdict | \"Safe points are a scoping choice\"; \"NOT READY\" | superseded, §3.8 |\n   462\t\n   463\t**Code: docstrings and comments.**\n   464\t\n   465\t| File:line | Text | Disposition |\n   466\t|---|---|---|\n   467\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1323`, `:1741` | \"walks a quiescent semantic machine\"; \"quiescent semantic machine m\" | \"a quiescent machine, parked, blocked, halted or between instructions\" |\n   468\t| `rows.cljc:1656-1657`, `:1663` | the two `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` refusals | 6d-a replaces the first by the `:running` kind and R0; the second stays for the unreachable default |\n   469\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc:33` | \"never invents a safepoint (§4.5)\" | \"never invents a state (§4.5)\" |\n   470\t| `layout.cljc:313-315`, `:318-321`, `:334` | `parking-ops` \"A1: the instructions a resume pc may follow\"; `safepoint!` docstring; `h->a` \"a :push has none and is no safepoint\" | 6e: `safepoint!` gains the run mode; docstrings say \"delivery state\" where they say \"safepoint\", `h->a` says \"is no A pc\" |\n   471\t| `layout.cljc:715-725` | `canonical-task` \"A running kernel is :yin.k/not-at-safepoint\" | 6e: \"a running kernel lifts as :running\" |\n   472\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:74-77`, `:318-321`, `:1119-1123`, `:2147-2154` | `:safepoint` phase; \"the machine at its liftable safepoint (parked, blocked or halted)\"; `hand-off` \"parked or blocked at a liftable safepoint\"; `at-safepoint?` | 6d-b: docstrings; `at-safepoint?` keeps its name (it detects the halt) with a docstring saying so |\n   473\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc:274-282` | `kind-of` \"else :halted\" | 6d-b: `:running` first |\n   474\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc:798-799`, `:842` | \"the machine at its liftable safepoint, holding no custody\"; \"arms over the safepoint\" | 6d-b: \"at a park, halt or instruction boundary\" |\n   475\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/ledger.cljc:277`; `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority/grant.cljc:74-75` | `#{:blocked :parked}` | 6d-b: add `:running` |\n   476\t| `rows.cljc:1261`, `:1297`, `:1704-1716`, `:1877-1882`, `:2039`, `:2264-2267` | the kind tables | 6d-a (grammar) and 6d-b (grant) |\n   477\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc:96`, `:122` | `#{:act :tail}`; `(pos? n)` | 6d-a |\n   478\t| `test/yin/vm/semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1012`, `:1018` | \"not at a safepoint\" refusal on a stepped reference machine | 6d-a: becomes the positive `running-kind` case; the refusal row is deleted |\n   479\t| `safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1887-1904` | `layout-refusals` \"a kernel stepped to a non-safepoint pc\" | 6e: becomes positive; the `:push` and out-of-body refusals replace it |\n   480\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_scenarios.cljc:184-186` | \"no liftable safepoint, UCF 7.4.1\" | 6d-b: \"a hold, UCF 7.7.4\" (the citation; the refusal is right) |\n   481\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:2`, `:331-333` | kernel docstring; `step`/`run` | 6d-a adds `run-fuel`; 6d-b adds the gate guard; the docstring names both |\n   482\t\n   483\tThe test file name `safepoint_harness_test.cljc` and the namespace stay (renaming it re-touches every requirer and the slow-tier namespace list for no semantic gain); its docstring (`:1-20`) says what it now proves.\n   484\t\n   485\t---\n   486\t\n   487\t## 7. Verification honesty\n   488\t\n   489\t- Every `file:line` above was read in `ce76c846` today. The assessment's A.1-A.6 analysis was re-checked against the renamed files; the only correction of substance is §2.5's \"one `vm/step`\" sentence (true only with `:control` nil).\n   490\t- **Not run**: nothing was executed. The step counts quoted for rows 1 and 22 are the assessment's 2026-10-13 JVM probe over the then-current builders; the harness builders have not changed since (G1 added tests, not rows), but the counts are to be **re-measured once and pinned** by 6d-a, not trusted from this report.\n   491\t- **Claims that are checks**: `L(p)` sufficient at every pc; injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc; the stack equation at every pc; the allowlist equality; the cross-host step-count equality.\n   492\t- **Not claimed**: that the de Bruijn kernels reconstruct a `run` state today (they do not; 6e); that the holder can preempt today (it cannot; `vm/run` at `driver.cljc:2120`); that module-pins commit 2's hunks are known exactly (they are inferred from the Q5 ruling's requirements; the conflict analysis holds for any edit confined to `requires` and the dependency step).\n   493\t- **Timestamps**: the brief is dated 2026-10-17 17:00 +07 and quotes the owner's ruling as of 2026-10-17; the machine clock at completion reads 2026-10-16 14:05 +07. The header uses the clock; the ruling's date is quoted as the brief gives it.\n   494\t\n   495\tPLAN-READY\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '107,280p' collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md; sed -n '280,347p' collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md; sed -n '1,110p' collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '107,280p' collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md; sed -n '280,347p' collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md; sed -n '1,110p' collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"### 2.2 How the holder pauses it: cooperative preemption at an instruction boundary\n\nToday (`driver/run-cycle` `driver.cljc:2111-2144`): `(vm/run m0)` when the gate is `:running` and the machine is not halted (`:2120-2122`); `require-tenure!` before (`:2118`) and after (`:2123`). `vm/run` is `ffi/maybe-run` over `register-run-scheduler` (`semantic.cljc:271-274`, `:333`), a `run-loop` that steps `(vm-hot % nil)` until the activation parks or halts. A pure loop of a billion instructions holds `run-cycle` for its duration; the next `require-tenure!` throws `:lease-bound`, `step-active` ends the run (`:2252-2253`), the machine is gated `:ended`, and the occurrence is regrantable from its **last checkpoint**, which is the pack this holder lowered. The computation since that checkpoint is lost. This is exactly the violation the owner names.\n\nThe change (6d-b item 1):\n\n- `yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`: `(if (engine/active-continuation? machine) (vm-hot machine n) (scheduler-round machine))`, public, telemetry-free; `vm-hot` with fuel `n` already answers `put-registers` at the boundary (`:142-143`). It is **not** `n` calls of `vm/step` (each emits a telemetry snapshot and runs `ready-for-ingress?`, `:331-332`).\n- `driver/run-cycle` reads a holder config key `:fuel` (positive integer or nil). With nil it runs `vm/run` as today (no behaviour change for existing compositions and tests). With `n` it runs `(run-fuel m0 n)`: the machine comes back either parked/halted (as before) or **at an instruction boundary with `:control` set**. The cycle continues (replay, emit, reader step) exactly as today; those read the machine's resources and wait set, not its control.\n- `cycle-result` (`:2166-2185`) is unchanged: the phase stays `:running`; the next program tick runs another `n` instructions. `require-tenure!` between cycles is the preemption point: tenure dying between two fuel slices ends the run with the machine **at a boundary**, and the holder, instead of losing it, can arm the exit (`hand-off`) **before** the bound. The driver's renewal cadence (half the duration, `:60-65`) already gives the composition a window; the composition picks `n` so that one slice is short against that window. The driver has no clock-to-instruction mapping and should not acquire one (no clock in the VM; `dao.lease` owns liveness).\n- `hand-off` (`:1118-1134`) on a machine at a boundary: `arm-exit` → `export/enter` → `prepare`/`encode` → `kind :running`. Nothing else in the exit half reads the kind except through `custody-baseline` and `kind-of`.\n\n**Whether the driver auto-exports at the bound** is a policy knob (§5, decision 2). Recommendation: no automatic export; `hand-off` remains the composition's explicit act (UCF §7.8 \"composition-driven, never automatic\"), and 6d-b exposes a status detail `:yin.k/at-boundary true` on the holder state after a fuel-exhausted cycle so a composition can decide.\n\n### 2.3 What the source does after the grant\n\nUnchanged from a parked root. The source's driver is a candidate over its own bytes (`driver.cljc:109-112`): it never resumes its local machine on data; it resumes only through a grant to itself. After `enter`, the local machine is gated `:exporting` with `:control` still set and `:wait-set []`; the driver does not run it (`:2120`); `vm-hot` refuses to step it (6d-b item 3); a direct `:resume` is refused (`resume-continuation` `engine.cljc:2359`). `abort` (`export.cljc:578-`) returns the gate to its prior value and the machine continues from the same boundary. On a grant to another holder the source's machine is ended and released as today; the successor occurrence lowers the pack as a `:running` root and continues from `pc`.\n\n### 2.4 Occurrence, lease, fencing, exclusive handoff for a running root\n\n- **Custody slot**: the blocked/parked form (`{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on a successor). A running root is a lease subject: it has a next observable step. `header-custody` (`rows.cljc:1704-1716`) takes the four-key arm for `:running`.\n- **Operation ids**: a running root carries no retained write, so `protect!` (`:1719-1737`) checks nothing and `custody-baseline`'s `:yin.k/ops` is empty; `next-op-seq` travels and the receiver's writer continues numbering from it. `:yin.k/issue` numbering (`with-custody` `:2052-2056`) numbers zero waits. The `:op-seq-exhausted` check (`:1769`) applies to a `:running` root as to blocked/parked.\n- **Grant on lower**: `check-custody!` (`:2023-2039`) runs `accept-grant!` for `:running` (today it skips only `fenced?` and `:halted`). Evidence, binding, lease, holder, arbitration, epoch, tenure and the input prefix are checked exactly as for a blocked root; the pack address is the carrier's.\n- **Offer**: `ledger/offer-defect` (`:277`) and `grant/offer-decision` (`:74-75`) admit `:running`; a `:running` offer is otherwise shaped as a blocked one. The checkpoint inspector follows `custody-baseline`.\n- **Gate on restore**: `with-custody` gates the tree `:running` (root not halted). The restored machine's `:control` is set, so the holder's next program tick runs it under the gate: `:stream-put` parks without observing (`handle-put` `:532-533`), `:stream-next` parks (`handle-next` `:677`), `:stream-cursor` mints an unminted cell (`:2654-2656`), `:stream-close` queues (`:754`), `:ffi-call`'s `ffi/put-request` answers `full` so the request is retained for the driver (`ffi.cljc:61-62`). The first effect the resumed computation reaches is therefore the driver's to perform, exactly as for a resumed parked root.\n- **In-flight host effects**: none exist at a boundary. Each `vm-hot` arm is one pure function of the pre-state and, where it observes, one observation's outcome inside the arm (assessment §A.5, re-verified: `observe-put` `engine.cljc:495-498` is called from `handle-put` inside `run-effect` inside the step; `:ffi-call` mints the park and appends inside one arm `semantic.cljc:237-254`). An exception inside an arm leaves the loop locals behind and the caller sees the pre-state. Under the `:running` gate the observe halves do not run at all (above). The owner's monad sentence is true of the gated machine and of every retry; the restatement stands: **a step is atomic; effects are observed only inside a step or inside the sweep; every state between steps is pure data.**\n- **Successor chain**: the exit mints the successor occurrence once (`exit-mint` `driver.cljc:1137-`), origin names the predecessor occurrence and lease; unchanged.\n- **Recovery format 2**: `freeze` stores the pack and the holder-local state (`export.cljc:344-375`); `recovery-metadata` records per-wait issue stamps (zero for a running root) and the gate; `rehydrate-fenced` restores `:control` set, gate `:exporting`, `:yin.k/recovery-waits []`. Re-preparation must reproduce the pack byte for byte, which holds because the `:run` frame is a pure function of the restored fields.\n\n### 2.5 The exporting phase and the quiescence rule\n\n`export/enter` (`export.cljc:121-149`) requires, in order: not already exporting/ended; **empty ready queue in every task of the tree** (`first-refusal` `:107-114`); no hold (`refusal-at` `:83-104`: `:observe`, `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`, `:link-cursor-not-installed`). It then sets the gate, empties the wait set and the parked map into the record. For a running root nothing is detached (no waits); the parked map still moves (a running root may hold parked records reachable by `:resume`). `lift-task*` repeats the first two refusals (`:1652-1655`) and keeps them.\n\n**The ready queue stays a refusal.** The assessment's §B.1 reasoning holds: a woken entry is a performed effect's result (a woken `:put` has appended; a woken `:next` holds the successor cursor in `:resource-updates`, `make-woken-run-queue-entries` `engine.cljc:442-463`) that no row kind carries, and the engine never leaves one at rest in its own API. One correction to the assessment's \"a caller crosses it with one `vm/step`\": that is true when `:control` is nil (`vm-hot` runs `scheduler-round`, which pops the first entry, `:2315-2326`). When `:control` is set, `vm/step` executes one instruction of the active activation and the queue is untouched; it drains only when the activation parks or halts and `run-loop` reaches the `(seq q)` arm (`:2216-2218`). So a running root with a non-empty ready queue lifts only once its activation has parked and the queued entry has been resumed, which is `vm/run` to the next park. Organically this needs two waiters woken in one sweep (the harness's `two-callers`, `:1069`); an organic one-control machine has at most one waiter and the queue is empty whenever `:control` is set. The holder under fuel sees this case only in multi-waiter machines; `hand-off` then answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent` and the composition retries after the next cycle, as it does today for a queue it observes. No `:ready` row kind (as before).\n\n**Nothing about parked roots regresses**: every refusal above applies to parked roots unchanged; the only parked-root behaviour that changes is that `kind-of` and the kind tables gain a branch that parked roots never take.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Design amendments, exact replacement text\n\nMarkers: each clause carries \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\". Cite the owner's sentence once, in the continuation design §0.1, and point to it elsewhere.\n\n### 3.1 `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md`\n\n**§0.1, new ruling 9 (after ruling 8):**\n\n> 9. **Preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm`** (owner, 2026-10-17: \"preemptive migration, the ability to pause and continue at any PC, is a primary invariant of yin.vm. It is why yin.vm is defined as a continuation VM based on the CESK model. The UCF being only defined at a wait or parked state is a violation of this invariant\"). The carrier therefore expresses the evaluator's state between **any** two instructions, not only its two delivery states; the parking pcs are where the evaluator stops by itself, and a safepoint is a delivery-bearing special case of the general state. Slice 6d lands the general state on the reference lane and in custody; slice 6e lands it in the backends' layouts.\n\n**§0.2, first sentence**, replace \"A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return` frames, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait (frozen §2.4, §4.2)\" with:\n\n> A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, which is a `run` state `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` at any instruction boundary (§1.3), or one of its two delivery states, `act` or `tail`, with the saved window, the delivery record and the pending wait the evaluator mints when it parks (frozen §2.4, §4.2)\n\nand after \"`:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and `:parked-table` rows\" insert \", the `:run` row (6d)\".\n\n**§1.1, after the three shapes**, add a fourth line and bullet:\n\n> ```clojure\n> run  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K}      ; between instructions; no deliver\n> ```\n> - `run` is the state `vm-hot` materialises when fuel runs out (`put-registers`, `semantic.cljc:142-143`, `:50-55`): `pc` is the instruction **about to execute**, `W` is the kernel's untrimmed body-local window and the carrier trims it to `L(pc)` (`analysis/live`), `K` as for `act`. A `run` at `n` differs by kind from an `act` at `n`: the `act` has executed `n−1`, carries `saved(n, rd)` and awaits `rd`; the `run` has not executed `n` and carries `L(n)`, which may contain that `rd`.\n\n**§1.2**, add the keyed form after the tail state:\n\n> ```clojure\n> ;; run state (6d)\n> {:yin.k/state :run\n>  :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n                ; the instruction about to execute, 0 ≤ n < (count v)\n>  :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n>  :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly L(n); sorted by r on the wire\n>  :yin.k/k [ … ]}                            ; no deliver, no pending\n> ```\n\n**New §1.3 \"States between instructions\"** (the assessment's §F text, updated):\n\n> **1.3 States between instructions.** The two delivery states of §1.1 are the states at which the evaluator parks. They are special cases of the evaluator's general state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, which `vm-hot` materializes whenever fuel runs out (`semantic.cljc:142-143`) and which the carrier expresses as the `run` state of §1.2 with no `deliver` and no `pending`. A step is atomic: effects are observed only inside a step or inside the scheduler's sweep, so no state between steps holds a half-issued effect. The static correspondence of §4 holds at every pc: distinct virtuals in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` hold distinct physical registers (`derive/allocation`, `derive.cljc:123-151`), and the stack kernel's operand stack at A pc `p` is `stack-layout(p) ++ uses(p)`; both are checks (§5.6). A machine with `:control` set and a non-empty wait set is malformed and refused `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state`. The one task state the carrier refuses by choice is a non-empty ready queue: a woken entry is a performed effect's result that lives in neither a pending nor a window, and the engine itself never leaves one at rest (`run-loop`); with `:control` nil a caller crosses it with one `vm/step`, with `:control` set by running the activation to its next park. A root whose task is a `run` state has kind `:running` (§2.6) and is a custody subject like a blocked or parked root (§7.3).\n\n**§2.3 row table**, add a row and amend `:wait`'s note:\n\n> | `:run` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref` | the between-instructions state; no delivery, no pending; five slots (an `:act` has six) |\n\nand `:wait`: \"`state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row, never a `:run`\".\n\n**§2.6 root**, `kind` comment: `; :blocked | :parked | :halted | :running`; `frames` comment: `; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order (:blocked, :parked); exactly one cref to a :run row (:running)`. Kind rules paragraph (also tuples §5.5): add \"`:running` has exactly one frame, a `:run`, and neither parked-id nor result; its wait set is empty.\"\n\n**§3.2 S1**: \"`:yin.k/state` is `:act`, `:tail` or `:run`; … a `run` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/k` and never `:yin.k/pending`\". **C2**: \"`0 < pc < (count v)` for an `act` and every return frame; `0 ≤ pc < (count v)` for a `run` (a body-start pc is a legal running state); `0 ≤ site < (count v)`\". **C3**: \"for the act and every return frame; **not** for a `run` (`body(pc−1) = body(pc)` is meaningless at a body start)\". New block after the tail site:\n\n> **The running activation (`run` only; a `run` skips A1-A6 and T1).**\n>\n> | # | Rule | Kind |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | R1 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n> | R2 | `(set (keys window)) = (analysis/live a pc)` exactly | `:window-extra` / `:window-missing` |\n> | R3 | no `:yin.k/deliver`, no `:yin.k/pending` (S1) | `:frame-shape` |\n>\n> There is no `:window-self` analogue: a `run` has no destination. `window-defect` is parameterised by the wanted set: `saved(pc, rd)` for an act and a return frame, `live(pc)` for a run.\n\n**P1**: \"`context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured`, `:parked` and `:running` require it absent; a `run` is admitted in context `:running` only, and `:running` admits only a `run`\" (`:pending-presence`). E1, E2, K1-K3, X1 unchanged (\"a `run`'s `E` is checked at its own `pc`'s chain\").\n\n**§3.3**: add \"and the root's `:run` frame in context `:running`\" to the list judged in step 7.\n\n**§4.5**, replace the last clause (\"never invents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking instruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2)\") with:\n\n> never invents a state: a kernel pc outside every body, or a `:push` pc of H (which is no A pc), is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2, as amended); every other kernel pc lifts, as a delivery state when the kernel is parked and as a `run` state when it is between instructions (6e). Until 6e lands, a de Bruijn kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` as a documented limit of the backends, not of the carrier.\n\n**§5.4 law 4**: \"one program at one **instruction boundary** (its parking boundaries included) lifts to row sets whose root ids are equal …\". Law 1, 2, 6: add \"at every reachable pc of the stepping driver (§5.7)\".\n\n**§5.6**, generalise items 3 and 5 and add 10:\n\n> 3. … at every reachable pc `p` (not only boundary pcs), `virt(p, s)` is single-valued on the physical image of `L(p)`; the existing `live-out*(p)` clause stays for parked entries.\n> 5. … `(set (stack-layout p)) ∪ (set (uses v[p])) = L(p)` for every reachable non-tail pc; `(set (uses v[p])) = L(p)` for every reachable tail-call pc; the parked-boundary form `(set (stack-layout p)) = saved(p+1, rd)` stays.\n> 10. Stepping: for every case the reference lane's step count to the first park and to the halt is pinned per host; the three lanes agree on it after the stack lane's `:push` steps are subtracted (`correspondence`).\n\n**New §5.7 \"The stepping driver\"**: the text of §4.1 item 4 below, verbatim.\n\n**§7.1 steps 1-2**, replace:\n\n> 1. **Quiescence.** Refuse `:yin.k/not-quiescent` on a non-empty ready queue; refuse the holds … as `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`; refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` when `:control` is set and the wait set is not empty.\n> 2. **Kind.** `:running` when `:control` is set; else `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked table holds, else `:blocked` on a non-empty wait set, else `:halted` with an empty queue; anything else is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a machine with no control, no wait, not halted: unreachable by construction).\n\nand in step 4 add \"a `:running` root's state node is the control's `run` state with the window trimmed to `L(pc)` (`select-keys`), contributing the context `(A, pc, E)` to discharge (`live-pcs` from `pc`)\". Delete \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set\" from the `canonical-task` paragraph and write \"a kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` until 6e, then a `run` state through `lift-running`\".\n\n**§7.2**, restore: \"`:running`: `:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set []`; nothing runs; `vm/run` continues from `pc`\". Add \"**§7.3 Custody of a running root**\": the content of §2.4 above in design voice (slot by kind table gains the row `root | running | exclusive | the four keys plus origin on a successor`; `check-custody!` runs the grant checks; `protect!` is vacuous; the exporting machine keeps `:control` and the kernel refuses to step it under `:exporting`/`:ended`).\n\n**§8.3**, item 12: \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel at a `:push` pc of H or outside every body; a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts as a `run` state (6e; until then the backend refusal is pinned as a limit)\". Add §8.6 (6d-a), §8.7 (6d-b), §8.8 (6e) = §4 of this report.\n\n**§9**: add \"**Claims that are checks**: `L(p)` is sufficient at every pc (law 1 at every step); injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc (item 3); the stack equation at every pc (item 5).\"\n\n**§11 Q6** becomes live and should be answered in 6d-a: with any-pc lifts, a lift **before** a constant-dead branch is routine, and `live-pcs` (edge-reachability, no constant propagation) refuses `ghost` there. Recommendation: keep \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\" (the owner's \"derive, don't persist\" and the linker's conservative discharge both argue for it), and make the harness's `ghost` case assert the refusal at the pre-branch pc as a pinned, documented false refusal.\n\n### 3.2 `yin.vm.semantic.md` (frozen design)\n\n**§4.1 first paragraph**, replace \"A safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** The machine's state between any two instructions, `⟨seg, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, is canonical and lifts as the `run` state (continuation design §1.3): preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm` (owner, 2026-10-17). A **safepoint** is the delivery-bearing special case: a transition at which the machine parks by itself, carrying a `deliver` record and, except for an explicit park, a pending wait. The table below is the table of those parking transitions; it is not the set of liftable pcs, which is every pc of every body. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n\n**§4.2**, after the tail state add the run state (as §3.1's keyed form) and amend \"The validator … site/pc pairing (`pc` is a safepoint successor of the right kind for `reason`; …)\" to \"(for an `act`, `pc` is a safepoint successor …; for a `run`, `pc` is any pc of a body and the window is exactly `L(pc)`)\". Row carrier sentence: add \"`:run [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`\".\n\n**§4.3**, replace \"The reconstruction obligation is: for an `act` state, …; for a `tail` state, …\" with the two existing clauses plus:\n\n> and for a `run` state at pc `p`, produce the segment `A`, `p`, every register of `L(p)`, `E`, `K` with each frame's validated window and `rd`, and no delivery record or pending; a physical location outside `L(p)`'s image is never read, and an engine that cannot hold a value of `L(p)` refuses before attachment (the register kernel's `:continuation-registers`). `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` holds for admitted canonical frames of all three states.\n\n**§10 phase 6** (record): append \"*(Amended 2026-10-16: the phase-6 harness proved the delivery states only; slices 6d and 6e extend the gate to every instruction boundary under the owner's any-pc invariant.)*\"\n\n### 3.3 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n\n**Blocker 1 (line 17)** \"The safepoint set must be exactly the machine's parking transitions\" → append \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc):** the parking transitions are where the machine stops by itself; the set of liftable configurations is every instruction boundary of the register-shaped machine (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1), and the safepoint table enumerates the delivery states within it.\"\n\n**§7.4.1 title and rule.** Title → \"7.4.1 Every instruction boundary lifts; the safepoint table is the machine's parking transitions\". Replace \"UCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be lifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the reference machine at which the machine parks.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17).** The register-shaped machine has no such interior: its state between any two instructions is `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, canonical and liftable (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1; continuation design §1.3). **A continuation may be lifted at any instruction boundary**; a *safepoint* is a boundary at which the machine parks by itself and carries a delivery record and a pending wait. The paragraph above describes the stack-shaped machine, which left at phase 8.\n\nThe keyed-form block gains the `run` state. **Quiescence** paragraph: append \"A machine between instructions (`:yin.k/state :run`, root kind `:running`) is quiescent when its ready queue is empty; its wait set is empty by construction.\"\n\n**§7.4.2**, rules: \"`lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on **every pc of A** the engine reaches and undefined only at pcs that are no A pc (a `:push` of the stack image) or outside every body.\" Replace the `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` bullet with:\n\n> - A request to lift at a pc that is no pc of A (a stack image's `:push`, a pc outside every body) returns `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc; the name is historical, the meaning is \"no canonical state exists here\". An engine may not invent states a record does not have, and two engines that disagree about the state set of one address are not both conforming. An engine that cannot hold a value of `L(pc)` at some pc refuses before attachment with its own kind (the register backend's `:continuation-registers`); that is a limit of the engine, not of the address.\n\nLast paragraph: \"a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of the safepoint set by construction\" → \"a debugger's breakpoint set is any subset of the pcs of A; the safepoint set is where the program will stop by itself\".\n\n**§7.7.4** step 3 \"Neutralize control: the record's control and K are already absent (the machine is parked or blocked)\" → \"Neutralize control: a parked or blocked machine has no control and no K; a machine between instructions (root kind `:running`) keeps its `:control`, `:window`, `:env` and `:k` as the state the lift reads, and the kernel refuses to step a machine whose gate is `:exporting` or `:ended` (`yin.vm.semantic/vm-hot`), so the exporting machine is pure data in both cases; direct `:resume` …\". In the \"(M-next D8/D9, as built.)\" paragraph, after \"under `:running` internal computation proceeds\" add \"for the holder's fuel budget per program tick (`driver/run-cycle`, `:fuel`), so a long computation is preempted at an instruction boundary and may be exported there\".\n\n**§7.8 Lift** step 1 → \"The machine is parked, blocked, or between instructions (`:running`), with a local record `r`, and its ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\" Step 3 → \"`lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at an instruction boundary of A (§7.4.1), else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; …\". Step 9 → \"At the next park, halt, or preemption boundary, produce the successor occurrence …\". §7.7.6 (line 2161) \"runs to its next safepoint\" → \"runs to its next park, halt, or preemption boundary\".\n\n**§7.9** row: `| :yin.k/not-at-safepoint | lift | :yin.k/segment, :yin.k/pc: the pc is no pc of A (a :push of a stack image, or outside every body) |`.\n\n**§7.10** \"No implicit control flow\": \"Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define\" → \"Resume happens only at an instruction boundary the canonical vector defines (a parking transition, or the boundary a holder's fuel budget stopped at); the successor chain …\".\n\n**§7.11 Safepoint conformance harness**: rename \"Continuation conformance harness\"; \"to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table — effect-producing calls included —\" → \"to **every instruction boundary** (the stepping driver, continuation design §5.7), the complete §7.4.1 table's parking transitions included,\"; the cross-engine sentence likewise \"one program at one instruction boundary\". **§7.11.1** Invariant → \"At every instruction boundary, lifting and lowering preserve the next observable step (and, at a parking transition, its wait); a pc that is no pc of A refuses.\" Setup: \"a nonsafepoint pc\" → \"a `:push` pc of the stack image and a body-start pc\"; expected: \"An undeclared pc gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`\" → \"A pc that is no pc of A gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; every pc of A lifts as a `run` state when the machine is between instructions\".\n\n### 3.4 `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`\n\n**§1 stance item 1**: append \"Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc): a *safepoint* is a parking `(segment-address, pc)`; the carrier expresses every `(segment-address, pc)` of A as a `:run` state when the machine is between instructions.\" **§5.2**: add the `:run` row; `:wait`'s note \"an `:act` or a `:tail` row, never a `:run`\". **§5.5**: kind comment and the kind-rules paragraph as §3.1 (`:running` has exactly one frame, a `:run`, …); custody table row `root | running | exclusive | the four keys plus origin on a successor` and `root | running | fork | nil`. **§8 step 2**: unchanged text, the §5.5 rules now include `:running`. **§9 Lift**: \"refuse unless quiescent\" stays; \"quiescence and safepoint, kind\" → \"quiescence, kind\"; **Cross-engine** law: \"Running the corpus to each safepoint\" → \"to each instruction boundary\". **§11**: \"Code identity (A), safepoints,\" → \"Code identity (A), the parking transitions,\".\n\n### 3.5 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md` §7.1\n### 3.5 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md` §7.1\n\nThe document is historical (versions 0-2), but §7.1 states a principle. Replace \"Only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks at the existing engine export boundary are eligible. Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth kind.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** For the stack-shaped machine of versions 0-2, only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks were eligible and running snapshots were not a fourth kind. For the register-shaped machine and body version 3 this is reversed by the owner's invariant: a task between instructions is the fourth kind, `:running` (continuation design §1.3, §2.6), and is eligible for export and custody.\n\n### 3.6 `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` §14.1\n\nTable 14.1.1: add the row `| Between instructions (:running) | No active wait; the window is exactly L(pc); continue at pc. |`. Line 3827 \"Validate the safepoint and empty ready queue\" → \"Validate the instruction boundary (§7.4.1 as amended) and empty ready queue\". Line 3900-3901 \"A non-safepoint pc and queued work yield the existing not-at-safepoint and not-quiescent outcomes\" → \"A pc that is no pc of A and queued work yield …\". Line 4145 as UCF §7.8 step 9.\n\n### 3.7 `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`\n\nNew dated entry I-9: \"2026-10-16, any-pc. The lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) in violation of the owner's primary invariant (2026-10-17); slice 6d adds the `:run` state, root kind `:running`, validator rules R1-R3, custody for running roots and the harness at every pc; §8's 'check the pc against the static safepoint set' is the stack-shaped machine's rule and is historical.\" No compatibility promise.\n\n### 3.8 The assessment's §F wording\n\nReplace its disagreement 3 (\"Safe points are a scoping choice, not a necessity\") with: \"Safepoints are the parking transitions, a proper subset of the liftable boundaries, by the owner's invariant; the one necessity kept is the ready-queue refusal (§2.5)\". Its amendment text is superseded by §3.1-3.3 above. Its verdict line (\"NOT READY … Q1 … Q2\") is superseded by this report.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slice breakdown, refreshed\n\nShape per the phase 7 plan. Hosts: JVM, Node, Dart for every slice. Verification per `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`: iterate on `bb test:clj`; the three-lane quick tier at landing (`bb test:sub yin.vm`; confirm \"Testing yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test\" and the other touched namespaces in the Node log); the slow tier once at landing of 6d-a and once at 6e (the every-pc laws are slow-tier). Every refusal test asserts zero serves (lift) or zero attaches (lower), the file's existing discipline.\n\n### 4.1 Slice 6d-a: the reference lane\n\n**Change.**\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc`: state `:run` in S1 (`:96-98`); `required` for a run `[:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/k]` with no optional key; C2 (`:117-123`): `(pos? n)` only for act and return frames; C3 (`:124-127`): skip the frame itself when run; `window-defect` (`:49-61`) takes the wanted set (`saved` or `live`) and omits the `:window-self` clause for a run; context `:running` in P1 (`:162-164`); A1-A6 and T1 skipped for a run; everything else unchanged. **R0** (the malformed-machine rule) lives in the lift, not the validator.\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`: `row-table` (`:49-69`): `:run [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k]]`; the task's slot 13 becomes `[:refs :wait :run]` (recommended; §5 decision 3); `lift-task*` (`:1650-1698`): the kind step as §3.1 §7.1 steps 1-2, the `:running` state node (`:control` + `(select-keys (:window m) (analysis/live (:analysis image) pc))` + `:env` + `(or (:k m) [])`, through `context!` for discharge), slot 13 `[run-cref]` for the kind; `state-node` (`:1508-1518`) gains the run arm (no `:site`, no `:deliver`); `validate-frames` (`:1109-1144`) judges slot 13's `:run` in context `:running`; `check-pendings!` (`:1877-1882`) `:running` arm; `task-ops` `:1261`, `custody-baseline` `:1297`, `header-custody` `:1704-1716` admit `:running` (the grammar half; the holder half is 6d-b); `restore-task` (`:2264-2267`) `:running` arm; `rows->frame`/`frame->rows` (`:1023-1058`, `:1082`) the run shape; `lower-defect`'s `state-prim` (`layout.cljc:863-874`) gets a `:run` arm (window plus lexical bindings, as `:act`), so a primitive in a running window refuses into the register backend before attach even before 6e lands.\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc`: public `run-fuel` (§2.2); no change to `vm-hot`'s loop.\n- Tests: `test/yin/vm/semantic/frame_test.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (the stepping driver, laws at every pc, the quick-tier sample, tampers, the refusal rows), `test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc` (the two static checks at every pc: no kernel involved, so it belongs here and not in 6e).\n- Docs: §3.1 (continuation design), §3.2 (frozen §4.1-4.3), §3.3 UCF §7.4.1-7.4.2, §7.9; §3.4 tuples §1, §5.2, §5.5, §9; §3.5; §3.7; a known-gaps row (§6).\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing in flight. Module-pins commit 2 is independent (§1.4).\n\n**Acceptance.**\n1. `frame-test/run-state-rules`: a `:run` built from `run-fuel` at every pc of `:worked-example` validates in context `:running`; one tamper per rule: C2′ (a float pc → `:pc-kind`; `pc = (count v)` → `:pc-bounds`); a **body-start pc validates** (today's C2 would refuse it); R1 (a key `≥ k(body)` → `:window-scope`); R2 (one live key removed → `:window-missing`; one dead register added → `:window-extra`); R3 (a deliver key → `:frame-shape`); P1 (`:run` with a pending → `:pending-presence`; `:run` in context `:wait` → `:pending-presence`; an `:act` in context `:running` → `:pending-presence`); an `act` at `p+1` and a `run` at `p+1` with `rd` present both validate and each is refused under the other's kind.\n2. `rows-test/run-row-bijection`: `frame->rows ∘ rows->frame` identity on every `:run` of item 1; `:run` is five slots; a sixth slot is `:row-shape` at decode step 2 (raw row edit with rehash); a `:wait` whose state cref names a `:run` row is `:undecodable` at step 3.\n3. `rows-test/running-kind`: a machine stopped by `run-fuel` mid-body lifts to kind `:running` with one `:run` frame, no waits, no pending and nil custody; lowering restores `:control` at the pc with the trimmed window and runs nothing (`:control` set, `(:value m)` nil, zero steps observed through the toy channel); `vm/run` then reaches the reference result and effect trace; the relift's root equals the lift's under `canonical-under` in a fresh receiver (law 2); a `:running` root with two frames, with a `:wait` frame, with a pending, with a non-empty wait set, with a parked-id or with a result is `:yin.k/undecodable :kind` (or `:parked-id`) before any attachment (attach count zero); a machine with `:control` set **and** a hand-added wait entry is refused at lift `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` with zero serves (R0).\n4. `harness/every-pc-matches-the-reference` (`^:slow`, `slow/guard`): the **stepping driver**: for each of the 89 cases build the world's program on the reference lane and step it by `run-fuel 1` (not `vm/step`, which emits telemetry per step) until the park, satisfy, continue stepping to the halt. At every `S_i` with `:control` set: lift → lower into a fresh machine (same composition, streams served as today) → `vm/run` to the end → **law 1** (result and effect trace equal the reference) → relift → **law 2** (root equality under `canonical-under`) → **law 6** (every frame validates; the window tampered by one live key removed or one dead register added is refused `:window-missing`/`:window-extra`). Law 3 does not apply (no pending). The step count to the park and to the halt is pinned per case (rows 1 and 22 under the four wrappers: 13/15/28/40 and 17/19/32/44 to the park, from the assessment's JVM probe; re-measure once and pin) and the total point count is asserted. The assessment's probe numbers (2,986 reachable pcs, 784 parking, roughly 2,500-3,500 pre-park points) are the size estimate; the pinned numbers are whatever the first green run measures.\n5. `harness/sampled-pcs-match-the-reference` (quick tier): per bare row (22 rows) five structural pcs: the first body-start reached, the pc after the first primitive `:var` (a host function live in the window), the pc before the parking instruction, the first `:jump` or `:branch-false` reached (rows 15, 21), the pc of the last `:return` before the halt; plus every step of rows 1 and 20 (tail, empty `K`); laws 1, 2, 6. About 150 points.\n6. `harness/run-refusals`: a non-empty ready queue after a hand-run `check-wait-set` is still `:yin.k/not-quiescent` with `:control` nil (today's test, `:1057-1066`) **and** with `:control` set (the two-waiter machine after `resume-from-run-queue` popped one entry and `run-fuel 1` ran one instruction of it); the same machine after `vm/run` to the next park lifts; a `:run` window holding a cursor-ref, a reified continuation and a nil-valued live register round-trips (rows 14, 16, 22 at the pc after the capture or the nil load); the `ghost` case (`:1330-1352`'s family) lifted at the pc **before** the constant-dead branch refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{ghost}}` (Q6 pinned as a documented false refusal).\n7. `layout-test/static-checks-at-every-pc`: A.3 (`virt` single-valued over `L(p)`'s physical image at every reachable pc of `layout-corpus` and the matrix's vectors, through `phys`) and A.4 (`(set (stack-layout p)) ∪ (set (uses v[p])) = L(p)` at every reachable non-tail pc; `(set (uses v[p])) = L(p)` at every reachable tail-call pc); the sizes asserted (reachable pcs per corpus segment).\n8. `semantic-vm-test/run-fuel`: `run-fuel n` on an active machine equals `n` applications of `vm-hot … 1` (state equality by `=` on the record minus telemetry fields); on a blocked machine it is one scheduler round.\n9. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log shows the four test namespaces; the slow tier run once at landing on three hosts.\n\n**Days.** 2 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = 3.5; docs 1 beside it.\n\n### 4.2 Slice 6d-b: holder and custody wiring for running roots\n\n**Can it ship with 6d-a?** Yes, as the second commit on the same branch, reviewed in the same gate. Reasons: (a) it edits only files 6d-a does not touch except `rows.cljc`'s `check-custody!` (`:2039`) which 6d-a leaves alone; (b) without it a `:running` root is liftable and lowerable as a fork but **not** exportable by a holder, offerable by the grantor or admitted by the ledger, so the invariant is honoured only for forks: a half state the owner should not be left in; (c) its tests reuse 6d-a's `running-kind` fixtures. Ship it before any composition exports a running root. If the review of 6d-a runs long, 6d-b lands as its own merge the same week.\n\n**Change.**\n1. `driver.cljc` `run-cycle` (`:2111-2144`): `:fuel` config key (checked in `check-common!`, positive integer or nil); `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under the existing gate and halted guards; `with-status` detail `:yin.k/at-boundary true` after a cycle that returned a machine with `:control` set.\n2. `export.cljc` `kind-of` (`:274-282`): `(some? (:control m)) :running` first.\n3. `semantic.cljc` `vm-hot` entry: `(when (contains? #{:exporting :ended} (vm/gate-mode machine)) (fail …))` before the loop; `run-fuel` inherits it. `register-restore` is unaffected (restore delivers nothing and the gate's `:resume` refusal is upstream).\n4. `rows.cljc` `check-custody!` (`:2039`): grant checks for `:running` (drop `(= :halted kind)` from the skip only; keep `fenced?`). `ledger.cljc:277`, `authority/grant.cljc:74-75`: admit `:running`.\n5. Docstrings: `driver.cljc:74-77` (`:safepoint` phase: \"the machine has nothing left to drive, **or** the composition armed the exit over a machine at a park or an instruction boundary\"), `:318-321`, `:1119-1123`; `compose.cljc:798-799`, `:842`; `export.cljc` header.\n6. Tests: `driver_test`: a fuelled run preempts a long pure loop (row 1's `(fn [x] …)` nested under a `(loop …)`-shaped segment of a few thousand steps) at a boundary; the phase stays `:running` and the next tick continues; `hand-off` on it mints the successor, journals, freezes and offers a `:running` pack; a candidate lowers it (`step-validating` → awaiting-grant → grant → `activate`) and reaches the reference result; `rehydrate-fenced` on the frozen recovery reproduces the pack byte for byte and the rehydrated machine refuses `vm/step` (\"Gate is closed\"). `ledger_test`/`grant_test`: a `:running` baseline is offerable; a `:halted` one is not (unchanged). `compose_rows_test`: an exclusive world whose source is handed off at a boundary completes on the successor (law 1 through the composition). `stage_e_scenarios`: one scenario `[:until \"h1\" :running n] [:hand-off \"h1\"]` with fuel set so the hand-off lands mid-body; recovery cut before and after the offer.\n7. Docs: §3.3 UCF §7.7.4, §7.8, §7.10, §7.11, §7.11.1; §3.6 linker.dht §14.1; continuation §7.3.\n\n**Depends on.** 6d-a (the kind and row).\n\n**Days.** 1.5 implementation + 1 review (2 rounds) = 2.5.\n\n### 4.3 Slice 6e: the cross-engine half\n\n**Change.** `layout.cljc`: `lift-running`/`lower-running` reading and writing the kernels' live fields (register `{:pc :registers :frames :continuation :store-of}`, stack `{:pc :stack :frames :continuation :store-of}`); `virt` over a given virtual set (`L(p)` for a run; today's `live-out*(p)` for parked entries, `:224-237`); `register-window` (`:458-461`) parameterised; the `:push` rule (`h->a` `:333-336`: a stack-kernel snapshot at a `:push` is the snapshot at its successor, which is never another `:push`); the tail-pc rule (at a tail-call pc the stack kernel holds the dead `stack-layout(p)` below `[f & args]`: the lift drops it, the lower refills it with nil, checked unobservable by item 8 generalised); the inverse fills `registers` to the body's count with nil outside `L(p)` (as `write-back`, `register.cljc`); `safepoint!` (`:318-330`) gains a run mode accepting any pc inside a body; `canonical-task` (`:724-725`) lifts an active kernel as `:running`; `kernel-task` (`:788-814`) lowers a `:running` machine into a running kernel. No kernel edit (the layouts read fields the kernels expose; the harness already reads them at `:1899-1904`). `layout_test.cljc`, the harness: laws 4 and 5 at every pc on the 61 lane cases.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 18:40 +07\n# Any-pc snapshots of the semantic-register VM: assessment (Architect, design note only)\n\nAuthor: claude-fable-5-1. Worktree `srvm-phase7-plan` at `781a9ba8` (phases 1-6 merged). Read: `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` (§3, §4, §5.2, §5.6, §7, §8.3, §11, every \"Amended after 6b/6c\" clause), the frozen design §4.1-4.3 and §10, `layout.cljc`, `derive.cljc`, `frame.cljc`, `rows.cljc` (`lift-task*`, `lower-task`, `live-pcs`), `semantic_register.cljc`, `engine.cljc` (the sweep, the ready queue, `handle-put`, `advance-install`, `run-loop`), `debruijn/stack.cljc` and `debruijn/register.cljc` (read only), `safepoint_harness_test.cljc`. One throwaway JVM probe was run over the harness corpus and deleted (its numbers are in §C.5); no code or doc was edited. Vocabulary per UCF §7.5.3: a *cursor* is the immutable dao.stream value, a *cursor cell* the carrier's box, a *cursor-ref* the sealed reference, a *cell* only the heap box.\n\n## Summary for the owner (15 lines)\n\n1. **The owner is right on the main claim.** Every instruction boundary of the semantic-register VM is a state the carrier can express canonically: `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` plus the task context phase 6 already carries. The kernel already materializes exactly this state at fuel exhaustion (`vm-hot`, `semantic_register.cljc:142-143`), and `vm/step` is `vm-hot` with fuel 1 (`:331-332`).\n2. **The static correspondence holds at every pc, not only at parking pcs.** Linear-scan allocation gives distinct physical slots to any two virtuals both in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` (`derive.cljc:173-189`), and `stack-layout` is computed for every pc (`layout.cljc:118-131`); the stack kernel's operand stack at A pc `p` is `stack-layout(p) ++ uses(p)`. Both are checks the harness must run, not theorems.\n3. **No half-issued effect exists at a boundary**, because a step is atomic. But the owner's wording \"performing it is the driver's job\" describes the custody-gated machine only (`handle-put` under the `:running` gate parks without observing, `engine.cljc:532-533`); the ungated step performs the observe half inside the step (`observe-put`, `:498`; `ffi/put-request` inside `:ffi-call`, `semantic_register.cljc:243`). The conclusion survives; the sentence does not.\n4. **The ready queue is scheduler state, not a one-task-per-carrier rule.** A ready entry is a performed effect's result (a woken `:put` has appended; a woken `:next` holds the successor cursor in `:resource-updates`) that no row kind carries. The engine's own API never leaves one at rest: `run-loop` and `scheduler-round` drain it in the same call (`engine.cljc:2171-2179`, `:2286-2287`), and an organic machine has at most one waiter. Keep the refusal; a caller crosses it with one `vm/step`.\n5. **Nothing else about the outside world lives at a non-parked pc.** Call ids and park ids come from the carried `id-counter`; `:yin.k/issue` numbers are minted at park time into the pending; the custody gate is phase 7's and orthogonal; streams, cursor cells and heap cells are exactly today's cases.\n6. **What changes:** one new state kind `:run` (`[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`, no deliver, no pending), one new root kind `:running`, validator rules R1-R3 beside A1-A6 (window = `L(pc)` exactly; `0 ≤ pc`; no body-continuity rule), the lift's `:control` branch becomes a kind instead of a refusal, the layouts gain a running-state entry point reading the kernels' live fields instead of a wait entry, and the harness gains a stepping driver.\n7. **Numbering, discharge and the tail seam need no change.** Canonical cursor-cell numbering walks the state (draft §6); `live-pcs` takes the `:run` pc as `at`; the tail-completion seam concerns delivering a `tail` wait and is untouched.\n8. **Real obstructions, all per-lane and all honest:** the register kernel refuses a primitive function in a register (`:continuation-registers`), and at any pc that is every boundary between loading a primitive operator and calling it; a `:push` pc of H is no A pc and maps to its successor; at a tail-call pc the stack kernel holds dead layout syntax below the operands, which the lift drops and the lower refills with nil (today's \"unobservable\" rule generalized).\n9. **The harness at every pc:** the reference matrix is 89 cases over 106 vectors with 2,986 reachable pcs (784 parking); dynamic steps to the park are 13-44 per case (rows 1 and 22 under the four wrappers), so roughly 2,500-3,500 pre-park snapshot points plus a comparable post-park tail. Every pc is a slow-tier law; the quick tier samples five structural pcs per bare row.\n10. **Value:** checkpoint at fuel exhaustion and preemption of a long pure computation (today a program with no effect for minutes has no safepoint at all), migration of a running task, step-level time travel where consecutive snapshots share most Merkle rows. **Derive, don't persist** answers the storage question: persist the last safepoint plus a step count, derive the `:run` state by replay (deterministic: no backward jumps, pure steps, effects only at parks); the `:run` row kind is still needed as the receiver-side and cross-engine canonical form.\n11. **Cost:** reference-lane half (validator, row, lift/lower, laws 1/2/6 at every step) about 2 opus days plus 2-3 codex rounds; cross-engine half (layouts' running mode, the two static checks at every pc, laws 4/5 at every step, the register-kernel refusal allowlist) about 2.5 days plus 3 rounds; docs 0.5 day plus one round. About 8-9 days in total, 4-5 if the cross-engine half is dropped.\n12. **Order:** after phase 7, before phase 8, as slice 6d. Phase 7 owns `rows.cljc` on the critical path; 6d would conflict with 7a and gains nothing from custody. The cross-engine half is worth doing only if the de Bruijn kernels survive phase 8 (see 13).\n13. **One discrepancy to rule on:** the brief says the old stack and register kernels die at phase 8. Frozen §10 phase 8 deletes `yin.vm.semantic` (old), `debruijn-resolve`, `debruijn-linearize` and `debruijn-register-compile`, and switches the REPL and linker to A-derived lowerings; it does not list `debruijn/stack.cljc` or `debruijn/register.cljc`. If the kernels keep executing published H/R images, the layouts and the cross-engine any-pc lanes are not dead work.\n14. **Recommendation:** adopt the generalization in the design now (amendment text in §F), implement the reference-lane half as slice 6d after phase 7, implement the cross-engine half only after the owner rules on 13, and never carry ready-queue entries.\n15. **Verdict:** NOT READY as a slice (two questions block: the phase-8 fate of the kernels, and whether a `:running` root may enter the holder's custody path in phase 7 or stays fork-only until 8); READY as a design amendment.\n\n---\n\n## A. Is every instruction boundary a canonical state?\n\n### A.1 The state the kernel holds between instructions\n\n`vm-hot` (`semantic_register.cljc:134-268`) keeps the activation in loop locals `seg pc W E K` and, when fuel runs out, materializes `(put-registers machine seg pc W E K)` (`:142-143`, `:50-55`): `:control {:segment seg :pc pc}`, `:window W`, `:env E`, `:k K` (nil when empty). `vm/step` is `(vm-hot machine 1)` (`:331-332`). So the kernel already produces the any-pc state; what phase 6 lacks is a carrier kind for it. `W` is the untrimmed body-local window (every register the body has assigned so far); the canonical form trims it to `L(pc)` (`analysis/live`), which is sufficient by the definition of liveness: a register outside `L(pc)` is never read before it is written again. Law 1 on the reference lane (lower the trimmed window, run to the end, compare) is what proves sufficiency; it is a check, not an assumption.\n\nThe canonical any-pc state, keyed form:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/state :run\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n          ; the instruction about to execute\n :yin.k/env E  :yin.k/window {r V …}    ; exactly L(n), sorted on the wire, nil present as [:lit nil]\n :yin.k/k [ …return frames… ]}          ; no deliver, no pending\n```\n\nIt is distinct from an `act` at the same pc: an `act` at `n` is \"resume at `n` once `rd` of `n−1` arrives\" (window `saved(n, rd)`, `rd` absent); a `run` at `n` is \"execute `n`\" (window `L(n)`, which may contain that `rd`). Both are legitimate states at one pc and the kind tells them apart; the parking instruction at `n−1` has either been executed (act) or not (run at `n−1`).\n\n### A.2 The instruction classes, the state before each, the layouts, obstructions\n\nNotation: the boundary is **before** instruction `p` executes; `W_p = W↾L(p)`; the register kernel holds a dense `:registers` vector, `:frames`, `:continuation`, `:store-of`, `:pc` (`register.cljc:627-630`); the stack kernel holds `:stack`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, `:store-of`, `:pc` (`stack.cljc:683-686`).\n\n| Class | A instructions | State before `p` | Register layout at `p` | Stack layout at `p` | Obstruction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| pure register ops | `:const :var :closure :jump :branch-false :define :gensym :store-get :store-put` | `⟨A, p, W_p, E, K⟩`; `L(p) = use(p) ∪ (L(succ) − def(p))` | `{virt_L(p)(s) ↦ registers[s]}` over the physical image of `L(p)`, injective (A.3) | `St = K-slices ++ stack-layout(p) ++ [W[u] for u in uses(p)]` (A.4) | none. A `:var` of a primitive leaves a host function in `rd`; the register kernel refuses it on lower (`:continuation-registers`, `layout.cljc:916-947`; `register-restore` → `effects/continuation-defect`), so every pc between that `:var` and its `:call` is unlowerable **into the register kernel** (not uncanonical: the reference carrier has `:prim` rows, the stack kernel holds it) |\n| non-tail `:call`, closure operand | `:call rd f args false` | before: as above with `uses = [f & args]`; after: `⟨A', entry, {}, merge(E_c, bind-params), K ++ [ret]⟩`, a **body-start** state | before: as row 1; after: `registers` = locals ++ nils (`register.cljc:683-687`), window `{}` or the params' virtuals, `L(entry)` | after: `St` = K slices only; the new frame's base is the caller's residual height (`stack.cljc:748-750`) | the validator's C2 (`0 < pc`) and C3 (`body(pc−1) = body(pc)`) refuse a body-start state; both are act-only rules and must not apply to `:run` |\n| non-tail `:call`, host-fn operand, plain result | same | before: as row 1; after: `⟨A, p+1, W[rd ↦ v], E, K⟩` | same | same | none; `(apply f args)` is a pure primitive application unless the primitive is `:host`-class, which discharge already refuses |\n| non-tail `:call`, host-fn yielding an effect | same | before: as row 1; after: today's `act` wait (`act-builders`, `:81-83`) or value | — | — | the observe half runs inside the step (A.5); never half-done at a boundary |\n| non-tail `:call`, continuation operand | same | before: as row 1; after: `register-restore` of the captured `act` with `rd` written (`:213-218`) | same | same | none |\n| tail `:call` | `:call rd f args true` | before: `⟨A, p, W_p, E, K⟩` with `L(p) = use(p)` (no successor, `analysis.cljc:27`); after: callee body-start with `K` unchanged, or the `tail` wait, or (host-fn, plain) a return into `K` | `registers` over `use(p)`'s image; the kernel's `live` operand is `[]` (unused here) | `St` = K slices ++ **dead layout syntax of the enclosing operand position** ++ `[f & args]` (`stack-layout(p)` may be non-empty, continuation §4.3) | the stack lift must drop `stack-layout(p)` at a tail site (dead in A) and the lower must refill it with nil, checked unobservable (§5.6 item 8 generalized); the `:run` at a tail pc has a window while the `tail` wait has none, and the two are different kinds |\n| `:return` | `:return r` | before: `L = {r}` ∪ nothing; after: caller's `⟨pc_k, W_k[rd_k ↦ v], E_k, pop K⟩`, which is a `:run` at the caller's `pc_k` | `return-transition` writes `dest` into the restored dense vector (`register.cljc:418-423`) | truncate to the frame's base and push (`stack.cljc:522`) | none |\n| `:halt` | `:halt r` | before: `L = {r}`; after: halted (today's `:halted` root) | — | — | none |\n| branches | `:branch-false r t`, `:jump t` | before: `L(branch) = {r} ∪ L(p+1) ∪ L(t)`; both successors' live sets are unioned (`analysis.cljc:26`), so a value live on only one arm travels on the other too (dead there; harmless, `L` is what the design says the window is) | as row 1 | H's `branch-false` pops the test (`stack.cljc:789`); before it, the test is the stack top = `uses(p)` | none |\n| closures | `:closure rd params entry` | before: `L(p)` = ancestors' pending; after: a closure value capturing `E` (`:156-161`) | the positional closure's `frames` ↔ `E` by the chain, unchanged (§4.4) | same | none |\n| `:stream-next :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-make :stream-close` | | before: `⟨A, p, W_p, E, K⟩`, `L(p) = use(p) ∪ saved(p+1, rd)`; after: value at `p+1` or today's `act` wait | as row 1 | `St` ends in `uses(p)` (`:stream-put`: target then value, the emitter's order, `derive.cljc:134-135`) | none; A.5 for where the observe half runs |\n| `:ffi-call` | `:ffi-call rd op args` | before: as above; after: today's parked bookkeeping under the call id plus the `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` wait, both minted **inside one step** (`:237-254`) | as row 1 | `St` ends in `args` | none at the boundary; the call id comes from `park-id` over the carried `id-counter` (`engine.cljc:2290-2294`) |\n| `:park` | `:park rd` | before: `L(p) = saved(p+1, rd)`; after: today's parked record | as row 1 | as row 1 | none |\n| `:current-continuation` | `:current-continuation rd` | before: `L(p) = saved(p+1, rd)`; after: a reified value in `rd` (`:255-258`) | as row 1 | as row 1 | none; the reified `act` is validated in `:captured` context as today |\n| `:resume` | `:resume rd pid r` | before: `L(p) ∋ r`; after: the parked record restored with `rd` written | as row 1 | H's `[:resume pid]` reads the top | none |\n| `:act` vs `:tail` frames (K) | | every `K[i]` is a return frame at a non-tail call site, whatever the current pc; K1-K3 apply unchanged | unchanged (`register-kframe`, `layout.cljc:464-473`) | unchanged (slices `[b_{i−1}, b_i)`) | none |\n| install children | | the parent is blocked on `:install`; `advance-install` runs the child with `vm/run` inside the sweep to a halt or a park (`engine.cljc:1437-1446`); a parent snapshot at any pc never sees a child mid-body | — | — | a child's own any-pc states are unreachable from outside the sweep unless the sweep takes fuel; not needed, note only |\n\n### A.3 The register pairing at every pc (static, a check)\n\n`allocation` (`derive.cljc:161-189`) builds one interval per virtual from `min` to `max` of its live points, where the points are `L(pc) ∪ def(pc)` over the body (`:165-168`); it frees a slot only when the interval's end is `< pc` (`:178-180`) and assigns a fresh or freed slot at each start. Two virtuals both in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` have `p` inside both intervals, so both are active at `p` and hold distinct slots. Hence `virt(p, s)` restricted to the physical image of `L(p)` is single-valued at **every** pc, not only at boundary pcs, and `phys` is total on the body's named virtuals as today. Today's `virt` (`layout.cljc:224-237`) takes the preimage set `live-out*(p)`; the any-pc version takes `L(p)` (and for a `:run` at `p` the window is `L(p)`, no destination subtracted). The harness's §5.6 item 3 generalizes to every reachable pc and remains a check (verification honesty, §9 of the continuation design).\n\n### A.4 The stack operand layout at every pc (static, a check)\n\n`stack-layouts` (`layout.cljc:118-131`) assigns every parse node its ancestors' pending pushes, so it is defined at every pc already. At the boundary before `p`, H has additionally evaluated `p`'s children, each landing on the stack (every value-producing H opcode conjes its result, `stack.cljc:719-731`), so `St[current] = stack-layout(p) ++ [W[u] for u in uses(p)]` with `uses` in table order, which is the emitter's child order (`derive.cljc:130-141`; `:stream-put` pushes target then value, `:call` operator then operands, `:ffi-call` the operands). The generalization of §5.6 item 5 is\n\n```\n(set (stack-layout p)) ∪ (set (uses v[p])) = L(p)        for every reachable non-tail pc p\n(set (uses v[p]))                          = L(p)        for every reachable tail-call pc p\n```\n\nand `|stack-layout(p)| + |uses(p)| = |St[current]|` at the kernel's state. A `:push` pc of H is a no-op pc advance (`stack.cljc:730-731`) and has no A pc; a stack-kernel snapshot at a `:push` is the snapshot at its successor (`h->a(h+1)`), which is never another `:push`. Duplicate uses (`(f x x)`) push one value twice; the set equation holds and the lift checks the duplicates' values agree.\n\n### A.5 Where the owner's monad sentence is true and where it is not\n\nThe design intent (frozen §4.1: a wait is a pending entry; the sweep retries it, `check-wait-set`, `engine.cljc:2126-2160`) and the custody-gated machine (`handle-put` under `:running`: \"nothing is observed: the put parks as it would on full\", `:527-533`) match the owner's statement. The ungated step does not: `:stream-put`'s first attempt appends inside the step (`observe-put`, `:495-498`, via `handle-effect` from `run-effect`, `semantic_register.cljc:91-98`), `:stream-next` reads inside the step, and `:ffi-call` mints the park and appends the request in one arm (`:237-254`). None of this creates a half-issued effect at an instruction boundary, because each arm is one pure function of the pre-state and one observation's outcome: either the pre-state (an exception leaves the locals behind) or the post-state is what a caller sees. So claim (2) should be restated: **a step is atomic; effects are observed only inside a step or inside the sweep; every state between steps is pure data.** Claim (3) follows from that restatement.\n\n### A.6 Cross-engine lowering at every pc (laws 4 and 5)\n\nLaw 4 (canonical equality) at step `i`: define the stop condition per kernel as \"after `i` A-instructions\": the reference kernel's `vm/step` count; the register kernel's step count (R is 1:1 with A, `register-image`, `derive.cljc:200-214`); the stack kernel's step count minus the `:push` steps (`correspondence`, `layout.cljc:97-108`). Parks and resumes happen at the same `i` on every lane (same program, same effects), so the three states at `i` lift to one root after `canonical-under`, by the same argument as today, plus A.3 and A.4. Law 5 (six ordered pairs) holds with one allowlisted refusal family: a lowering **into the register kernel** at a pc where a primitive function sits in `L(pc)` is `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` before any attachment, as `layout-refusals` already pins for parks (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1901-1925`). The harness must count those refusals per case instead of treating them as failures. There is no analogous refusal on the stack or reference lanes.\n\n**What survives phase 8 of the cross-engine value** depends on §13 of the summary. If the kernels die with the lanes, what survives is the two static checks (A.3, A.4), which read only `derive.cljc` and `layout.cljc`'s static half, and the reference-lane laws. If the kernels survive to execute published H/R images, the layouts survive and so does the dynamic half.\n\n---\n\n## B. The two remaining questions\n\n### B.1 The ready queue: the true reason for \"not quiescent\"\n\n`lift-task*` refuses a non-empty ready queue first (`rows.cljc:1456-1457`). A ready entry (`make-woken-run-queue-entries`, `engine.cljc:442-463`) is the wait entry plus `:value`, `:status`, `:cursor` and `:resource-updates` (the successor cursor the sweep already took, `:453-456`), with the handle dropped. Three facts decide the question:\n\n- **It is a performed effect's result, not a pending one.** A woken `:put` has appended (the datom is on the stream); a woken `:next` has advanced the cursor in the resource table the engine will merge on resume (`:2266-2268`). Putting the entry back as a wait would re-append the datom; dropping it would lose the read. So the queue cannot be normalized away by \"undoing the wake\"; this is the one place where the outside world has moved between two states the carrier might see.\n- **It is representable.** The entry is pure data by design (`run-loop` docstring, `:2166-2168`); a `:ready` row `[state-cref value-V status resource-updates-cref]` would carry it, and resuming it is `register-restore` (`semantic_register.cljc:101-126`), a pure function. A terminal outcome (`terminal-resume-outcome`, `:2194-2213`) raises on resume and is not a task state the carrier expresses, so such an entry stays refused.\n- **The engine never leaves it at rest.** `run-loop` sweeps and resumes in one call (`:2173-2179`); `scheduler-round` likewise (`:2286-2287`); `vm/step` on a blocked machine runs `scheduler-round`. A non-empty queue is observable only when a caller invokes `engine/check-wait-set` directly (the harness's refusal test does, `:1046`) or, with any-pc snapshots, **mid-run when two waiters woke in one sweep** and the second is queued while the first runs. An organic semantic-register machine has one control and parks by clearing it (`:control nil` on every park), so its wait set holds at most one entry and the queue at most one woken entry, which the same `run-loop` iteration resumes; the two-waiter machines in the harness are built by hand (`two-callers`, `:1055-1062`).\n\nSo the refusal is about **scheduler state with a performed effect inside it**, not about one task per carrier and not about a pending wake. Recommendation: keep `:yin.k/not-quiescent` for any non-empty queue, including under any-pc; a composition that wants to snapshot after a sweep steps once (`vm/step`, which resumes the entry) and snapshots the resulting `:run` state. The `:ready` row kind is specified here only so the choice is explicit; it is not recommended, because no engine path needs it and it would carry the one state in which an effect's outcome exists outside both the pending and the window.\n\n### B.2 Anything else genuinely about the outside world at a non-parked pc\n\n- **Operation ids.** `:yin.k/issue` is minted at park time under the `:running` gate onto the `:put` entry (`engine.cljc:2629-2633`); op-ids on `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` pendings are phase 7's slots. A `:run` state has no pending, hence no op-id; the counter that will mint the next one is custody state (phase 7's root slot), unaffected.\n- **Custody and the gate.** `:yin.k/gate`/`:yin.k/custody` are holder state the row carrier does not carry (phase 7 plan §3.1: lease, epoch, holder never enter a body). A `:run` snapshot under a holder is a preemption checkpoint; whether the holder may export a running task is a phase 7 policy question (§E.2, Q2), not a carrier obstruction.\n- **Call ids and park ids** derive from `id-counter`, which travels (`rows.cljc:1495`).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
