d amendments\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2326:### 10.1 To the frozen design (`yin.vm.semantic.md`)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2371:### 10.2 To UCF (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2457:### 10.3 To the row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2620:### 10.4 To code-as-tuples (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`) §7.2\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2635:### 10.5 Checked and consistent (no amendment)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2643:### 10.6 Amendments after slice 6b (revision 4, 2026-10-13)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2679:### 10.7 Amendments after slice 6c (revision 5, 2026-10-13)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2707:### 10.8 Amendments after phase 7 (2026-10-14)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2722:## 11. Owner questions\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2846:## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n    80\t                  (map (fn [x s] [pc x s])\n    81\t                       (operands (nth v pc))\n    82\t                       (register-operands (nth (:instructions image) pc)))))\n    83\t        (range (count v))))\n    84\t\n    85\t\n    86\t(defn- phys-maps\n    87\t  \"Per body, `{virtual physical}` read off the pairs; the first pc that\n    88\t   names a virtual wins (register-pairing checks they agree).\"\n    89\t  [bodies pairs]\n    90\t  (reduce (fn [acc [pc x s]]\n    91\t            (let [b (code/body-index bodies pc)]\n    92\t              (if (contains? (nth acc b) x) acc (assoc-in acc [b x] s))))\n    93\t          (vec (repeat (count bodies) {}))\n    94\t          pairs))\n    95\t\n    96\t\n    97\t(defn correspondence\n    98\t  \"§4.3 static item 1: H with every `:push` removed corresponds one to one,\n    99\t   in order, with A. Answers `{:a->h [h-pc …] :h->a {h-pc a-pc}}`.\"\n   100\t  [h]\n   101\t  (reduce (fn [acc hpc]\n   102\t            (if (= :push (nth (nth h hpc) 0))\n   103\t              acc\n   104\t              (-> acc\n   105\t                  (update :a->h conj hpc)\n   106\t                  (assoc-in [:h->a hpc] (count (:a->h acc))))))\n   107\t          {:a->h [] :h->a {}}\n   108\t          (range (count h))))\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\t(def ^:private pushing-ops\n   112\t  \"The parse nodes whose children before the current one are pushed:\n   113\t   `:call` (the operator and earlier operands), `:ffi-call` (earlier\n   114\t   operands) and `:stream-put` (the target, while the value runs).\"\n   115\t  #{:call :ffi-call :stream-put})\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\t(defn- stack-layouts\n   119\t  \"§4.3 static item 2 for every pc of every body: `{pc [virtual …]}`,\n   120\t   bottom to top, over the parse tree's ancestors outermost first.\"\n   121\t  [v bodies]\n   122\t  (letfn [(walk\n   123\t            [out node pending]\n   124\t            (let [out (assoc out (:pc node) pending)\n   125\t                  pushes? (contains? pushing-ops (:op node))\n   126\t                  uses (when pushes? (code/uses (nth v (:pc node))))]\n   127\t              (reduce (fn [out [i child]]\n   128\t                        (walk out child (if pushes? (into pending (take i uses)) pending)))\n   129\t                      out\n   130\t                      (map-indexed vector (:children node)))))]\n   131\t    (reduce (fn [out body] (walk out (:expr body) [])) {} bodies)))\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\t(defn segment\n   135\t  \"The static facts of one admitted A vector `v`, for both kernels.\"\n   136\t  [v]\n   137\t  (let [bodies (code/parse v)\n   138\t        r (derive/register-image v)\n   139\t        h (:image (derive/stack-image v))\n   140\t        pairs (register-pairs v r)]\n   141\t    (merge {:vector v\n   142\t            :address (jing/segment-key v)\n   143\t            :bodies bodies\n   144\t            :analysis (analysis/analyze v)\n   145\t            :register r\n   210\t  [seg p]\n   211\t  (reduce (fn [acc q] (into acc (analysis/live (:analysis seg) q)))\n   212\t          #{}\n   213\t          (analysis/successors (:vector seg) p)))\n   214\t\n   215\t\n   216\t(defn register-live\n   217\t  \"§5.6 item 3: the `live` operand R must carry at boundary `p`,\n   218\t   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`, the destination removed as\n   219\t   a virtual id before mapping; `[]` at a tail call.\"\n   220\t  [seg p]\n   221\t  (vec (sort (map #(phys seg p %) (disj (live-out seg p) (code/rd (inst seg p)))))))\n   222\t\n   223\t\n   224\t(defn virt\n   225\t  \"The restricted inverse of `phys` at boundary `p`: the virtual of\n   226\t   `live-out*(p)` held in physical `s`. A slot outside that image (only a\n   227\t   tampered entry names one) reads as the one virtual of the body R maps\n   228\t   there, so the frame validator judges the window; an ambiguous slot is\n   229\t   refused.\"\n   230\t  [seg p s]\n   231\t  (let [m (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg p))\n   232\t        live (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (live-out seg p)))\n   233\t        named (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (keys m)))]\n   234\t    (cond\n   235\t      (= 1 (count live)) (first live)\n   236\t      (and (empty? live) (= 1 (count named))) (first named)\n   237\t      :else (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s}))))\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\t(defn stack-layout\n   241\t  \"§4.3: the virtual registers pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   242\t   is inside the expression at A pc `p`, bottom to top.\"\n   243\t  [seg p]\n   244\t  (get (:layouts seg) p))\n   245\t\n   310\t        (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :image-not-attached {:yin.k/segment (:address seg)}))))\n   311\t\n   312\t\n   313\t(def ^:private parking-ops\n   314\t  \"A1: the instructions a resume pc may follow, besides a non-tail call.\"\n   315\t  #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation})\n   316\t\n   317\t\n   318\t(defn- safepoint!\n   319\t  \"Refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` unless the A instruction before `pc`\n   320\t   parks (a tail call for a tail state): a layout never invents a\n   321\t   safepoint (§4.5).\"\n   322\t  [seg pc tail?]\n   323\t  (let [t (when (and (integer? pc) (< 0 pc) (<= pc (count (:vector seg)))) (inst seg (dec pc)))\n   324\t        op (when t (nth t 0))]\n   325\t    (when-not (and t\n   326\t                   (= (body-of seg (dec pc)) (body-of seg (min pc (dec (count (:vector seg))))))\n   327\t                   (if tail?\n   328\t                     (code/tail-call? t)\n   329\t                     (or (contains? parking-ops op) (and (= :call op) (not (code/tail-call? t))))))\n   330\t      (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc pc}))))\n   331\t\n   332\t\n   333\t(defn- h->a\n   334\t  \"The A pc of H pc `hpc`; a `:push` has none and is no safepoint.\"\n   335\t  [seg hpc]\n   336\t  (or (get (:h->a seg) hpc) (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc hpc})))\n   337\t\n   338\t\n   339\t(defn- a->h\n   340\t  [seg pc]\n   341\t  (nth (:a->h seg) pc))\n   342\t\n   343\t\n   344\t;; =============================================================================\n   345\t;; Values: positional closures and kernel continuations, both ways\n   346\t;; =============================================================================\n   347\t;;\n   348\t;; A lift context `cx` is {:kind :kernel :space :free-env :owner}; a lower\n   349\t;; context `lx` adds :address, naming a canonical payload's or closure's\n   350\t;; :segment as A.\n   351\t\n   352\t(declare lift-state lower-state canonical-value positional-value)\n   353\t\n   354\t\n   355\t(defn- walk-value\n   356\t  \"`x` with its guest collections rebuilt around `f`, which converts\n   357\t   closures and continuations.\"\n   358\t  [f x]\n   359\t  (cond\n   360\t    (values/host-typed? x) (f x)\n   361\t    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(walk-value f k) (walk-value f v)])) x)\n   362\t    (vector? x) (mapv #(walk-value f %) x)\n   363\t    (set? x) (into #{} (map #(walk-value f %)) x)\n   364\t    (seq? x) (apply list (map #(walk-value f %) x))\n   365\t    :else x))\n   715\t(defn canonical-task\n   716\t  \"Quiescent kernel `k` presented as semantic machine `template`\n   717\t   (a fresh machine of the same composition) for `rows/lift-task`: code\n   718\t   by A from `space`, every wait, parked record and value reconstructed\n   719\t   through the layout; resources, stores and counters carried. A running\n   720\t   kernel is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; install children are not carried\n   721\t   by this phase's layouts; a closure or continuation in the free\n   722\t   environment has no finite canonical form (§6.3).\"\n   723\t  [k space template]\n   724\t  (when (engine/active-continuation? k)\n   725\t    (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (:pc k)}))\n   726\t  (when (seq (:installs k))\n   727\t    (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :installs {:yin.k/names (vec (keys (:installs k)))}))\n   728\t  (when-let [sym (some (fn [[sym x]] (when (holds-procedure? x) sym)) (sort-by (comp str key) (:free-env k)))]\n   729\t    (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}))\n   730\t  (let [cx (lift-context k space (:owner template))\n   731\t        conv #(canonical-value cx %)\n   732\t        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lift-state cx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n   733\t                     (:parked k))\n   734\t        value (:value k)\n   735\t        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))\n   736\t        code (into {} (map (fn [[a s]] [a (assoc {} :vector (:vector s) :address a :analysis (:analysis s))]))\n   737\t                   (:segments space))]\n   738\t    (assoc template\n   739\t           :code code\n   740\t           :code-aliases (into {} (map (fn [a] [a a])) (keys code))\n   741\t           :program nil :control nil :window {} :k nil\n   742\t           :resources (:resources k)\n   743\t           :capability-secret (:capability-secret k)\n   744\t           :free-env (:free-env k)\n   745\t           :env (or (:free-env k) {})\n   746\t           :store (conv (:store k))\n   747\t           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores k) {}))\n   748\t           :parked parked\n   749\t           :wait-set (mapv #(lift-state cx %) (:wait-set k))\n   750\t           :ready-queue (:ready-queue k)\n   751\t           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n   752\t           :id-counter (:id-counter k)\n   753\t           :installs {}\n   754\t           :halted? (:halted? k)\n   755\t           :blocked? (:blocked? k))))\n   756\t\n   757\t\n   758\t(defn lift-task\n   759\t  \"§7.1 for a de Bruijn kernel: `rows/lift-task` over its canonical\n   760\t   presentation. `template` is a fresh semantic machine of the\n   761\t   kernel's composition; opts as rows/lift-task's.\"\n   762\t  [k space template serve! opts]\n   763\t  (rows/lift-task (canonical-task k space template) serve! opts))\n   764\t\n   765\t\n   766\t(defn attach-space\n   767\t  \"Kernel `k` holding the derived image of every segment of `space`, in\n   768\t   A order (§7.2: derive and attach the image for each A).\"\n   769\t  [k space]\n   770\t  (let [kind (kernel-kind k)]\n   771\t    (reduce (fn [k a]\n   772\t              (let [seg (segment-of space a)]\n   773\t                (if (= :stack kind)\n   774\t                  (stack/attach-image k (:stack seg) vm/stack-contract)\n   775\t                  (register/attach-image k (:register seg) vm/register-contract))))\n   776\t            k\n   777\t            (sort-by str (keys (:segments space))))))\n   778\t\n   779\t\n   780\t(defn machine-space\n   781\t  \"The space of the A vectors semantic machine `m` holds, and\n   782\t   the map from its segment ids to A.\"\n   783\t  [m]\n   784\t  [(space (map :vector (vals (:code m))))\n   785\t   (fn [seg] (or (get-in m [:code seg :address]) (when (contains? (:code-aliases m) seg) seg)))])\n   786\t\n   787\t\n   788\t(defn kernel-task\n   789\t  \"Step 12 for a de Bruijn receiver: semantic machine `m` (what\n   790\t   `rows/lower-task` restored) as fresh kernel `k` of the same\n   791\t   composition: every image derived and attached, the free environment\n   792\t   set, every wait, parked record and value lowered through the layout.\n   793\t   Delivers nothing, runs nothing.\"\n   794\t  [m k]\n   795\t  (let [[sp address] (machine-space m)\n   796\t        k (attach-space k sp)\n   797\t        lx (lower-context k sp address)\n   798\t        conv #(positional-value lx %)\n   799\t        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lower-state lx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n   800\t                     (:parked m))\n   801\t        value (:value m)\n   802\t        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))]\n   803\t    (assoc k\n   804\t           :resources (:resources m)\n   805\t           :free-env (:free-env m)\n   806\t           :store (conv (:store m))\n   807\t           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores m) {}))\n   808\t           :parked parked\n   809\t           :wait-set (mapv #(lower-state lx %) (:wait-set m))\n   810\t           :ready-queue []\n   811\t           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n   812\t           :id-counter (:id-counter m)\n   813\t           :halted? (:halted? m)\n   814\t           :blocked? (:blocked? m))))\n   815\t\n   855\t   kernel's :free-env, never payload data).\"\n   856\t  [code a pc e w path]\n   857\t  (let [names (lexical-names (get code a) pc)]\n   858\t    (or (some (fn [[r y]] (value-prim code y (conj path :window r))) (partition 2 (:slots w)))\n   859\t        (some (fn [[sym y]] (when (contains? names sym) (value-prim code y (conj path :env sym))))\n   860\t              (partition 2 (:slots (first (:slots e))))))))\n   861\t\n   862\t\n   863\t(defn- state-prim\n   864\t  [code n path]\n   865\t  (let [kframes (fn [k]\n   866\t                  (some (fn [[i f]]\n   867\t                          (let [[a pc e w] (:slots f)]\n   868\t                            (frame-prim code a pc e w (conj path :k i))))\n   869\t                        (map-indexed vector (:slots k))))\n   870\t        [a pc e w k] (:slots n)]\n   871\t    (case (:kind n)\n   872\t      :act (or (frame-prim code a pc e w path) (kframes k))\n   873\t      :tail (kframes e)\n   874\t      nil)))\n   875\t\n   876\t\n   877\t(defn- row-procedure\n   878\t  \"The path to the first closure or continuation row carrier value `x`\n   879\t   holds, through guest collections; or nil.\"\n   880\t  [x path]\n   881\t  (when (and (vector? x) (= :row (first x)))\n   882\t    (let [n (second x)]\n   883\t      (case (:kind n)\n   884\t        (:closure :reified) path\n   885\t        (:vec :list :set :map) (some (fn [[i y]] (row-procedure y (conj path i))) (map-indexed vector (:slots n)))\n   886\t        nil))))\n   887\t\n   888\t\n   889\t(defn- free-env-procedure\n   890\t  \"§6.3: a de Bruijn kernel resolves free names through its one\n   891\t   `free-env` while a canonical closure snapshots its `env`, so a closure\n   892\t   or continuation held in the free environment has no finite canonical\n   893\t   form there. The path `[:free-env sym …]` to the first such value of\n   894\t   decoded task `task`, or nil.\"\n   895\t  [task]\n   896\t  (some (fn [[sym x]] (row-procedure x [:free-env sym]))\n   897\t        (partition 2 (:slots (nth (:slots task) 5)))))\n   898\t\n   899\t\n   900\t(defn- image-collision\n   901\t  \"§4.1: `[ident #{A …}]` for the first image of kind `kind` two carried A\n   902\t   derive, or nil. A de Bruijn kernel attaches one image once, so it\n   903\t   cannot hold both segments; no enumeration order selects an A. A\n   904\t   vector that derives no image is left to the carrier's own checks.\"\n   905\t  [code kind]\n   906\t  (let [ident (fn [a v]\n   907\t                (try\n   908\t                  (if (= :stack kind)\n   909\t                    (dcode/image-hash (:image (derive/stack-image v)))\n   910\t                    (rcode/register-hash (derive/register-image v)))\n   911\t                  (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ [::underivable a])))\n   912\t        by-image (reduce (fn [acc [a v]] (update acc (ident a v) (fnil conj #{}) a)) {} code)]\n   913\t    (some (fn [[i as]] (when (< 1 (count as)) [i as])) (sort-by (comp str key) by-image))))\n   914\t\n   915\t\n   916\t(defn lower-defect\n   917\t  \"What a kernel of `kind` cannot hold, judged on decoded task `task`\n   918\t   before anything is attached: two carried A of one image (§4.1), install\n   919\t   children (not carried by this phase's layouts), a closure or\n   920\t   continuation in the free environment (§6.3), a retained FFI request\n   921\t   whose response pair the backend's re-park cannot preserve, and for the register\n   922\t   kernel a primitive function in the payload data of a wait or parked\n   923\t   state (a window, a lexical binding, or reached through them), which\n   924\t   its restore refuses as `:continuation-registers` (`vm/machine-data?`).\n   925\t   nil when it can.\"\n   926\t  [task kind]\n   927\t  (let [slots (:slots task)\n   928\t        code (nth slots 11)\n   929\t        collision (image-collision code kind)\n   930\t        free (free-env-procedure task)]\n   931\t    (cond\n   932\t      collision\n   933\t      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :ambiguous-image\n   934\t             :yin.k/image (first collision) :yin.k/segments (second collision))\n   935\t\n   936\t      (seq (nth slots 12))\n   937\t      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :installs)\n   938\t\n   939\t      free\n   940\t      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :free-env-value :yin.k/path free)\n   941\t\n   942\t      (some (fn [w] (= :ffi-request (first (:slots (second (:slots w))))))\n   943\t            (nth slots 13))\n   944\t      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :retained-ffi)\n   945\t\n   946\t      (= :register kind)\n   947\t      (when-let [path (or (some (fn [[i w]] (state-prim code (first (:slots w)) [:frames i]))\n   948\t                                (map-indexed vector (nth slots 13)))\n   949\t                          (some (fn [[p st]] (state-prim code st [:parked p]))\n   950\t                                (partition 2 (:slots (nth slots 8)))))]\n   951\t        (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :continuation-registers :yin.k/path path))\n   952\t\n   953\t      :else nil)))\n   954\t\n   955\t\n   956\t(defn lower-task\n   957\t  \"§7.2 into a de Bruijn kernel: decode `wire`, refuse what kernel `k`\n   958\t   cannot hold (`lower-defect`) before any attach, then `rows/lower-task`\n   959\t   into `scratch` (a fresh semantic machine of the same\n   960\t   composition: the carrier's steps 1-11 and restore) and `kernel-task`\n     1\t(ns yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test\n     2\t  \"Phase 6b: the safepoint harness on the :semantic lane\n     3\t   (docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md §5, §8.2).\n     4\t\n     5\t   Each row of the §5.2 matrix is built twice under each of the four\n     6\t   segment wrappers: the reference build is satisfied and run on its own\n     7\t   machine; the handoff build is lifted with rows/lift-task, lowered with\n     8\t   rows/lower-task into a fresh receiver over the toy channel, relifted at\n     9\t   once, then satisfied through the source's streams. Result and effect\n    10\t   trace equal the reference (law 1); the relift equals the lift by root\n    11\t   id under rows/canonical-under (law 2); the pending's reason is the\n    12\t   observed wait's (law 3); every lifted frame passes the validator\n    13\t   (law 6). Row 20 runs once, outside the wrappers.\n    14\t\n    15\t   Phase 6c adds the two de Bruijn lanes (§5.3): a world built under\n    16\t   *lane* :stack or :register runs A's derived image on that kernel and\n    17\t   lifts and lowers through yin.vm.semantic.layout. Law 4 (one\n    18\t   root id on all three lanes under canonical-under), law 5 (six ordered\n    19\t   lowering pairs, each continuing to the reference result) and the\n    20\t   layout refusals close the file.\"\n    21\t  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n    22\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n   990\t  (testing \"E2 over row 21 with the free environment {x 99 y 7}\"\n   991\t    (let [world (free-env-world identity {'x 99 'y 7})\n   992\t          {:keys [serve! toy]} (served #{:reader})\n   993\t          wire (rows/lift-task (:machine world) serve! (lift-opts))\n   994\t          task (root-task wire)\n   995\t          env-path (conj wait-act 2 0)\n   996\t          edit (fn [f] (rows/emit-numbered (update-at task env-path #(rebuild % (fn [ps] (f (into {} ps))))) {}))]\n   997\t      (is (= [:lit 1] (second (first (filter #(= 'x (first %)) (pairs-of (get-in task [:slots 13 0 :slots 0 :slots 2 :slots 0]))))))\n   998\t          \"the lexical x is 1 at the read\")\n   999\t      (doseq [[label f] [[\"the unshadowed y changed\" #(assoc % 'y [:lit 8])]\n  1000\t                         [\"the lexical x removed\" #(dissoc % 'x)]]]\n  1001\t        (testing label\n  1002\t          (let [[r attaches] (lowered toy (edit f))]\n  1003\t            (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :free-env-mismatch] [(:yin.k/status r) (:yin.k/kind r)]) (pr-str r))\n  1004\t            (is (zero? attaches)))))\n  1005\t      (testing \"the lexical x changed trips nothing\"\n  1006\t        (let [[r] (lowered toy (edit #(assoc % 'x [:lit 98])))]\n  1007\t          (is (vm/blocked? r) (pr-str r))))))\n  1008\t  (testing \"lift refusals, before any stream is served\"\n  1009\t    (let [counting (fn []\n  1010\t                     (let [n (atom 0) {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})]\n  1011\t                       [(fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h)) n]))\n  1012\t          stepped (vm/step (load-program (machine) (s/lit 42)))\n  1013\t          m0 (load-program (machine) (s/app (s/v 'stream/poll!) (s/v 'c)))\n  1014\t          [sref m1] (engine/attach-resource m0 (s/ring 8))\n  1015\t          [cref m2] (engine/handle-cursor m1 {:stream sref} :k1)\n  1016\t          held (vm/run (assoc m2 :store {'c cref} :yin.k/gate :running))]\n  1017\t      (is (= [:observe] (mapv :reason (:wait-set held))))\n  1018\t      (doseq [[label m status kind] [[\"not at a safepoint\" stepped :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil]\n  1019\t                                     [\"a held :observe\" held :yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch]]]\n  1020\t        (testing label\n  1021\t          (let [[serve! n] (counting)\n  1022\t                d (error #(rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts)))]\n  1023\t            (is (= status (:yin.k/status d)) (pr-str d))\n  1024\t            (is (= kind (:yin.k/kind d)))\n  1025\t            (is (zero? @n))))))))\n  1026\t\n  1027\t\n  1028\t(deftest first-defect-order\n  1029\t  (let [[toy wire] (lift-bare 18)\n  1030\t        task (root-task wire)\n  1031\t        state-path [8 1]\n  1032\t        state (get-in task [:slots 8 :slots 1])\n  1033\t        ;; the park's window: rd added\n  1034\t        tampered (update-at task (conj state-path 3)\n  1035\t                            #(rebuild % (fn [ps] (conj ps [(nth (:slots state) 5) [:lit :x]]))))\n  1036\t        census (mapv first (pairs-of (slot task 9)))\n  1037\t        swapped (zipmap census (reverse census))]\n  1038\t    (is (= 2 (count census)))\n  1039\t    (is (= :window-self (:yin.k/kind (first (lowered toy (rows/emit-numbered tampered {}))))))\n  1040\t    (let [[r attaches] (lowered toy (rows/emit-numbered tampered swapped))]\n  1041\t      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells] [(:yin.k/status r) (:yin.k/kind r)]) (pr-str r))\n  1042\t      (is (zero? attaches)))))\n  1043\t\n  1044\t\n  1045\t;; =============================================================================\n  1046\t;; Acceptance 8: the two ported tests\n  1047\t;; =============================================================================\n  1048\t\n  1049\t(defn parked-reader\n  1050\t  []\n  1051\t  (let [m0 (vm/run (load-program (machine) (with-stream (s/then read-op read-op))))\n  1052\t        src (s/stream-of m0)]\n  1053\t    (stream/append! src \"A\")\n  1054\t    [(vm/run m0) src]))\n  1055\t\n  1056\t\n  1057\t(deftest a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent\n  1058\t  (let [[parked src] (parked-reader)\n  1059\t        _ (stream/append! src \"B\")\n  1060\t        woken (engine/check-wait-set parked)\n  1061\t        n (atom 0)\n  1062\t        {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n  1063\t        d (error #(rows/lift-task woken (fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h)) (lift-opts)))]\n  1064\t    (is (seq (:ready-queue woken)) \"the sweep queued the woken reader\")\n  1065\t    (is (= :yin.k/not-quiescent (:yin.k/status d)) (pr-str d))\n  1066\t    (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\")))\n  1067\t\n  1068\t\n  1069\t(defn two-callers\n  1070\t  []\n  1071\t  (let [call-in (s/ring 8) call-out (s/ring 8)\n  1072\t        m (vm/run (load-program (machine {:call-in call-in :call-out call-out\n  1073\t                                          :call-out-cursor (vm/mint-oldest call-out :test)})\n  1074\t                                (s/then (s/call-node :op/echo \"hello\") (s/call-node :op/echo \"world\"))))\n  1075\t        e1 (first (:wait-set m))]\n  1076\t    [(assoc m :wait-set [e1 (assoc e1 :call-id :parked-9)]) call-out (:call-id e1)]))\n  1077\t\n  1078\t\n  1079\t(defn answer-both!\n  1080\t  [call-out first-id]\n  1081\t  (stream/append! call-out (apply2/success-response :parked-9 \"second\"))\n  1082\t  (stream/append! call-out (apply2/success-response first-id \"first\")))\n  1083\t\n  1084\t\n  1085\t(defn sweep\n  1086\t  [m peers]\n  1087\t  (loop [m (engine/check-wait-set m) n 12]\n  1088\t    (if (or (zero? n) (empty? (:wait-set m)))\n  1089\t      m\n  1090\t      (do (run! s/serve-mirror! peers) (recur (engine/check-wait-set m) (dec n))))))\n  1091\t\n  1092\t\n  1093\t(deftest a-response-cursor-shared-by-two-waiters-crosses-as-one-cursor-cell\n  1094\t  (let [[ref-m ref-out id] (two-callers)\n  1095\t        _ (answer-both! ref-out id)\n  1096\t        expected (mapv :value (:ready-queue (sweep ref-m [])))\n  1097\t        [m call-out id] (two-callers)\n  1098\t        {:keys [serve! toy peer]} (served #{:reader})\n  1099\t        wire (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n  1100\t        task (root-task wire)\n  1101\t        pendings (mapv #(second (:slots %)) (slot task 13))\n  1102\t        own-out (s/ring 8)\n  1103\t        {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n  1104\t        recv (rows/lower-task (machine {:attach-stream attach! :call-in (s/ring 8) :call-out own-out\n  1105\t                                        :call-out-cursor (vm/mint-oldest own-out :test)})\n  1790\t      (testing (str \"row \" row)\n  1791\t        (let [pairs (cross-lowering row #(build identity))]\n  1792\t          (is (= (if (= 4 row) 2 6) (count pairs)) \"every admitted ordered pair ran\")\n  1793\t          (swap! ran + (count pairs)))))\n  1794\t    (testing \"row 20\"\n  1795\t      (is (= 6 (count (cross-lowering 20 tail-effect-halt))))\n  1796\t      (swap! ran + 6))\n  1797\t    (is (= 92 @ran) \"16 cases x 6 pairs, less row 4's 4 into a de Bruijn receiver\")))\n  1798\t\n  1799\t\n  1800\t(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-lowering\n  1801\t  ;; acceptance 11: law 5, six ordered pairs over §5.3's shared fragment,\n  1802\t  ;; 350 transfers (366 once unlowerable is emptied)\n  1803\t  (slow/guard\n  1804\t    \"cross-engine-lowering\"\n  1805\t    (fn []\n  1806\t      (let [ran (atom 0) cases (lane-cases)]\n  1807\t        (is (= 61 (count cases)))\n  1808\t        (doseq [[row label _ build] cases]\n  1809\t          (testing (str \"row \" row \" \" (name label))\n  1810\t            (swap! ran + (count (cross-lowering row build)))))\n  1811\t        (is (= 350 @ran) \"61 cases x 6 pairs, less row 4's 4 per segment\")))))\n  1812\t\n  1813\t\n  1814\t(defn register-judge\n  1815\t  \"The frame validator's verdict on register entry `entry` of m,\n  1880\t                                   (some? extra) (conj [extra :x]))))]\n  1881\t    {:site-pc (+ off p) :pc (+ off p 1) :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n  1882\t     :dest (when (some? rd) (layout/phys sg p rd)) :continuation [] :resume-mode :write-result\n  1883\t     :frames (mapv (fn [c] (vec (repeat (count (nth (nth v c) 2)) nil))) (layout/chain sg (inc p)))}))\n  1884\t\n  1885\t\n  1886\t(deftest layout-refusals\n  1887\t  (testing \"a kernel stepped to a non-safepoint pc\"\n  1888\t    (doseq [lane [:stack :register]]\n  1889\t      (testing (name lane)\n  1890\t        (scoped\n  1891\t          (fn []\n  1892\t            (let [m (on-lane lane #(vm/step (load-program (machine) (s/app (s/lam ['p] (s/lit 42)) (s/lit 1)))))\n  1893\t                  n (atom 0)\n  1894\t                  {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n  1895\t                  d (error #(lane-lift m (fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h))))]\n  1896\t              (is (not (vm/halted? m)))\n  1897\t              (is (= :yin.k/not-at-safepoint (:yin.k/status d)) (pr-str d))\n  1898\t              (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\")\n  1899\t              (testing \", its registers read as a payload\"\n  1900\t                (let [entry (if (= :stack lane)\n  1901\t                              (select-keys m [:pc :frames :stack :continuation])\n  1902\t                              (assoc (select-keys m [:pc :frames :continuation]) :regs [] :resume-mode :write-result))\n  1903\t                      cx (layout/lift-context m (kernel-space m) (:owner m))]\n  1904\t                  (is (= :yin.k/not-at-safepoint (:yin.k/status (error #(layout/lift-state cx entry)))))))))))))\n  1905\t  (testing \"a register entry whose live was tampered\"\n  1906\t    (scoped\n  1907\t      (fn []\n  1908\t        (let [m (:machine (on-lane :register #(bare 1)))\n  1909\t              entry (first (:wait-set m))\n  1910\t              live (:live entry)\n  1911\t              spare (spare-register m entry)]\n  1912\t          (is (nil? (register-judge m entry)) \"the untampered entry reconstructs a valid frame\")\n  1913\t          (is (seq live))\n  1914\t          (is (= :window-missing (register-judge m (assoc entry :live (vec (rest live)) :regs (vec (rest (:regs entry))))))\n  1915\t              \"a live register dropped\")\n  1916\t          (is (some? spare))\n  1917\t          (is (= :window-extra (register-judge m (assoc entry :live (vec (sort (conj live spare)))\n  1918\t                                                        :regs (vec (sort-by first (conj (:regs entry) [spare :x]))))))\n  1919\t              \"a dead register added\")))))\n  1920\t  (testing \"a register entry tampered with an ambiguous dead preimage: the layout refuses\"\n  1921\t    (let [fixtures (ambiguous-dead-slots)]\n  1922\t      (is (seq fixtures) \"C holds one: the allocator reuses slots\")\n  1923\t      (doseq [[v p s] fixtures]\n  1924\t        (let [sp (layout/space [v])\n  1925\t              k (layout/attach-space (on-lane :register machine) sp)\n  1926\t              cx (layout/lift-context k sp (:owner k))\n  1927\t              d (error #(layout/lift-state cx (register-entry k v p s)))]\n  1928\t          (is (map? (layout/lift-state cx (register-entry k v p nil))) \"untampered, the entry lifts\")\n  1929\t          (is (= [:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout] [(:yin.k/status d) (:yin.k/kind d)]) (pr-str d))\n  1930\t          (is (= {:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s} (select-keys d [:yin.k/pc :yin.k/register])))))))\n  1931\t  (testing \"a primitive in a window, a lexical binding, a closure's capture or a window's guest collection: the register kernel cannot hold it, refused before any attach\"\n  1932\t    (doseq [[label op expected path?]\n  1933\t            [[:window (s/app (s/v 'conj) (s/lit []) read-op) [\"B\"]\n  1934\t              #(= [:frames 0 :window] (subvec % 0 3))]\n  1935\t             ;; the read under cat, whose closure captures no primitive: s/then\n  1936\t             ;; would save its own closure, which captures the binding too\n  1937\t             [:lexical (s/app (s/lam ['q] (cat-of (cat-of (s/lit []) read-op) (s/app (s/v 'q) (s/lit []) (s/lit 1))))\n  1938\t                              (s/v 'conj))\n  1939\t              [\"B\" [1]] #(= '[:frames 0 :env q] %)]\n  1940\t             [:captured (s/let1 'g (s/lam ['h] (s/lam ['x] (s/app (s/v 'h) (s/lit []) (s/v 'x))))\n  1941\t                                (s/let1 'k (s/app (s/v 'g) (s/v 'conj))\n  1942\t                                        (cat-of (cat-of (s/lit []) read-op) (s/app (s/v 'k) (s/lit 2)))))\n  1943\t              [\"B\" [2]] #(= '[:frames 0 :env k h] %)]\n  1944\t             [:nested (s/app (s/lam ['w 'r] (s/v 'r)) (cat-of (s/lit []) (s/v 'conj)) read-op) \"B\"\n  1945\t              #(and (= 5 (count %)) (= [:frames 0 :window] (subvec % 0 3)) (= 0 (peek %)))]]]\n  1946\t      (testing (str \" \" (name label))\n  1947\t        (let [build #(reader % op)\n  1948\t              world (build identity)\n  1949\t              {:keys [serve! toy]} (served #{:reader})\n  1950\t              wire (rows/lift-task (:machine world) serve! (lift-opts))\n  1951\t              {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n  1952\t              d (error #(lane-lower :register {:attach-stream attach!} wire attach!))]\n  1953\t          (is (= [:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers] [(:yin.k/status d) (:yin.k/kind d)]) (pr-str d))\n  1954\t          (is (path? (:yin.k/path d)) (pr-str (:yin.k/path d)))\n  1955\t          (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\")\n  1956\t          (testing \", the stack kernel holds it\"\n  1957\t            (let [ref (reference (build identity))\n  1958\t                  got (crossed :semantic :stack #(build identity))]\n  1959\t              (is (= expected (:value ref)))\n  1960\t              (is (= (:value ref) (:value got))))))))))\n  1961\t\n  1962\t\n  1963\t(defn try-wrapper\n  1964\t  [try?]\n  1965\t  (if try? #(s/let1 'require (s/v 'try-require) %) identity))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '197,360p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '655,791p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '1056,1194p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '197,360p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '655,791p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '1056,1194p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md\"","aggregated_output":"### 2.4 Transitions\n\nLet `I = code[seg][pc]`, `W[r]` the value of register `r`,\n`W[r ← v]` the window with `r` written. $\\rho$ is `engine/resolve-var`.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{const}:&& \\langle seg,pc,W,E,S,K\\rangle \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W[rd \\leftarrow I.v],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{var}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W[rd \\leftarrow \\rho(E,S,I.name)],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{closure}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W[rd \\leftarrow \\mathrm{clo}(I.params,I.body,seg,E)],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{jump}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,I.target,\\,W,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{branch-false}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,(W[cond]\\ ?\\ pc{+}1 : I.target),\\,W,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{define}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W[rd \\leftarrow W[rs]],E,\\,S[I.name \\mapsto W[rs]],K\\rangle\\\\\n&\\text{halt}:&& \\to \\text{halted},\\ \\text{result} = W[I.value\\text{-}reg]\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n`define` writes through `engine/put-active`, which routes the write\nto the active module store or the task's store and calls\n`engine/store-put`, which refuses the reserved key (Rule R unchanged);\nthe definition operator is never resolved.\n\n**The saved window.** Wherever a transition saves the current\nactivation for later delivery of a value into `rd`, the window it saves\nis\n\n$$\\mathrm{saved}(p, rd) = W\\!\\restriction\\!(L(p) \\setminus \\{rd\\})$$\n\nwhere `L(p)` is the standard live-in set at the resume pc `p` (§8.1),\nwhich may well contain `rd` (later instructions read the delivered\nvalue), so the subtraction is explicit and is the rule everywhere:\ncall frames, parks, captures, waits, foreign-engine reconstruction and\nthe `live` operands of §8.2. Nothing else is ever saved from `W`.\n\n**call** with `f = W[fn-reg]`, `a = [W[r] for r in arg-regs]`:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&f = \\mathrm{clo}(ps,b,seg',E_c),\\ \\neg tail:&&\n\\to \\langle seg',\\,b,\\,\\{\\},\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K \\Vert [\\mathrm{ret}(seg,pc{+}1,E,\\,\\mathrm{saved}(pc{+}1,rd),\\,rd)]\\rangle\\\\\n&f = \\mathrm{clo}(ps,b,seg',E_c),\\ tail:&&\n\\to \\langle seg',\\,b,\\,\\{\\},\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K\\rangle\\\\\n&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a) = v,\\ \\neg tail:&&\n\\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W[rd \\leftarrow v],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a) = v,\\ tail:&&\n\\to \\mathrm{return}(v)\\ \\text{(below: the value goes to }K\\text{'s top frame, never to }rd)\\\\\n&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a) = \\epsilon,\\ \\neg tail:&&\n\\to \\mathrm{effect}(\\epsilon,\\ \\mathrm{act}(seg,pc{+}1,E,\\mathrm{saved}(pc{+}1,rd),K),\\ \\mathrm{deliver}(\\mathrm{rd}, rd))\\\\\n&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a) = \\epsilon,\\ tail:&&\n\\to \\mathrm{effect}(\\epsilon,\\ \\mathrm{tail}(seg,pc,K),\\ \\mathrm{deliver}(\\mathrm{return}))\\\\\n&f = \\kappa\\ \\text{(a reified continuation)}:&&\n\\to \\text{restore }\\kappa\\text{ and deliver }a_1\\text{ per }\\kappa\\text{'s own deliver; the caller's activation and }K\\text{ are discarded}\\\\\n&f\\ \\text{not callable}:&& \\to \\text{error, as today}\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nA callee activation starts with an **empty window**; its parameters are\nin `E` (duplicate-parameter resolution, nil-fill and extra-argument\ndropping are `bind-params`' and are unchanged). A non-tail call saves\nthe caller's window restricted by the saved-window rule, with the\ndelivery destination `rd`. **A tail call never writes `rd` and never\nresumes its own activation**: whatever the callee produces, a closure's\neventual `:return`, a pure primitive's immediate value, or an effect's\ncompletion, is delivered through `K`. This makes tail completion\nuniform and makes the instructions textually after a tail call layout\nsyntax (§3.3 item 4). Invoking a reified continuation is abortive, as\n`semantic/apply-call`'s `:continuation` arm is today: the argument is\nvalidated (exactly one), ownership of `κ` is checked under the UCF\nrules for source-owned continuation values, the current activation and\n`K` are discarded, `κ`'s captured state is restored, and the argument is\ndelivered exactly once per `κ`'s recorded `deliver`.\n\n**return** with `K = K' ∥ [ret(seg', pc', E', W', rd')]`:\n\n$$\\to \\langle seg',\\,pc',\\,W'[rd' \\leftarrow v],\\,E',\\,S,\\,K'\\rangle,\\qquad v = W[I.value\\text{-}reg]$$\n\nand with `K = []`: halted with result `v`. The result is written\n**exactly once**, into the popped frame's destination. `return(v)` as\nused above is this transition applied to a value that did not come from\nthe current window.\n\n**Delivery.** Every suspended or deferred computation records where its\nresult goes, as a closed typed record:\n\n```clojure\n{:deliver :rd     :rd r}        ; write-result: v → W[r], continue at the recorded pc\n{:deliver :return}              ; through-return: return(v) against K\n```\n\nand the suspended state it belongs to is one of two closed shapes:\n\n```clojure\nact  = {seg pc E window K}      ; a resumable activation; window = saved(pc, rd)\ntail = {seg site K}             ; no activation: the tail site (for diagnostics\n                                ;   and provenance), K, and nothing to resume into\n```\n\nA `tail` state carries no window and no `E` of its own; the store\ncontext any later code needs is in the frames of `K`. Delivery of `v`\nto an `act` with `{:deliver :rd r}` restores it, writes `W[r ← v]`, and\ncontinues at `pc`; delivery to a `tail` with `{:deliver :return}` runs\n`return(v)` against `K`, which pops a frame or halts. When a deferred\ntail completion halts, the machine's `:env` is the scheduler's current\n`:env` at the restore (`base`), cleared of the module-store key; when an\nimmediate tail completion halts (a pure primitive or a non-parking\neffect in a tail call), it is the current `E` so cleared, as `:halt`\nleaves it. The two are not equated: no `E` of the tail site travels in\nthe `tail` state. The engine seam already supports this: a VM's restore\nfunction `restore-fn(base, entry, value)` is called from exactly two\nplaces, `engine/resume-from-run-queue` (a woken wait entry, after the\nentry has left the ready queue and after the terminal-outcome check,\nwith `base` the blocked machine) and `engine/resume-continuation` (an\nexplicit `:resume`, with no terminal check and with `base` the\nresuming machine, which may be active). Neither path requires a\ncurrent activation: the helper reads only the entry, the value and the\nmachine's tables, treats the VM payload as data, and either resumes an\n`act` or runs `return(v)`, which may halt; the `:resume` transition\naccepts a halted result. No trampoline activation is synthesized. The\ntwo shapes cover primitive effects, `:park`,\n`:current-continuation`, stream and FFI waits (including the\n`request-sent` phase, which advances the FFI protocol without\ndelivering anything to guest code), link waits (whose three phases\npreserve the delivery record until install completes), and halting. An\neffectful tail call therefore completes by return, never by writing a\nregister of a body that no longer runs.\n\n**stream-next** (cursor ref `c = W[cursor-reg]`), by outcome:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&ok\\ v,\\ c':&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W[rd \\leftarrow v],E,\\,S[c \\mapsto c'],K\\rangle\\\\\n&blocked:&& \\to \\text{wait}\\ \\{\\mathrm{act}(seg,pc{+}1,E,\\mathrm{saved}(pc{+}1,rd),K),\\ \\{\\text{:deliver :rd :rd } rd\\},\\ \\text{pending: cursor cell } c\\}\\\\\n&end:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W[rd \\leftarrow \\mathrm{nil}],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n&gap\\ c':&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,W[rd \\leftarrow \\text{:dao.stream/gap}],E,\\,S[c \\mapsto c'],K\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n`stream-put`, `stream-make`, `stream-cursor`, `stream-close`, `gensym`,\n`store-get`, `store-put`: as today, reading their register operands\nfrom `W` and their literal operands from the instruction, and writing\n`rd`. A blocking `stream-put` waits as `act(seg, pc+1, E,\nsaved(pc+1, rd), K)` with `{:deliver :rd}` and the **retained value\n`W[value-reg]` in the pending record**, where it belongs even though\n`value-reg` may be absent from the saved window.\n\n**ffi-call** (`a = [W[r] for r in arg-regs]`): the wait\n`act(seg, pc+1, E, saved(pc+1, rd), K)` with `{:deliver :rd}` is\nrecorded under a fresh call id `p` (`eval-call`); `request(p, I.op, a)`\nis appended; a `full` append keeps the request retained\n(`request-sent` phase, which when it later appends advances to the\nresponse wait and delivers nothing to guest code); on the correlated\nresponse its `ok` value is delivered to `rd` at `pc+1`, its `error`\nraises, and the parked bookkeeping for `p` is removed.\n\n**park**: the record `act(seg, pc+1, E, saved(pc+1, rd), K)` with\n`{:deliver :rd}` is written to `:parked` under a fresh id; the task\nhalts with that record as its value. The not-yet-produced value of\n`rd` is excluded from the saved window by the saved-window rule.\nExplicit park raises **no wait entry** (UCF §7.4.3 r5: the parked\nrecord is the no-wait shape).\n**resume** (`v = W[value-reg]`, `I.id` the parked id): the parked\n`act` is restored and `v` is delivered per its `deliver`. `:resume`\nends its own path.\n**current-continuation**: `W[rd ← κ]` where\n`κ = act(seg, pc+1, E, saved(pc+1, rd), K)` with `{:deliver :rd}`,\nreified as data. `κ` is a captured value, never a safepoint. Invoking\nit (`:call` arm above) delivers the argument to `rd` in the captured\nwindow and continues at `pc+1`; `κ` is never captured inside its own\ndestination because the saved-window rule excludes `rd`.\n\n## 4. Safepoints and the continuation format\n\n### 4.1 The safepoint table (UCF §7.4.1, rewritten)\n\nA safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks. The semantic\nkinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n\nEvery wait is one of the two closed states of §2.4 (`act` with\n`{:deliver :rd}` or `tail` with `{:deliver :return}`); an effectful\ntail call raises the `tail` form of whichever row its effect falls in.\n`saved` is `saved(pc+1, rd)` of §2.4.\n\n| Safepoint kind | Raised by | State | Delivery |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| blocked read | `:stream-next rd c` → `blocked` | `act`, pending cursor cell `c` | `rd` ← read value; nil on `end`; `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n| blocked write | `:stream-put rd s v` → `full` | `act`, pending retains `W[v]` and the stream | `rd` ← written value on retry `ok` |\n| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call rd op args` → `ok` | `act`, pending `:ffi` (call id, response cursor cell); in-VM `:reason :next` with `:call-id` | `rd` ← correlated `ok` value; `error` raises; bookkeeping removed |\n| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `full` | `act`, pending `:ffi-request` (envelope verbatim); in-VM `:reason :put` with `:request-sent` | none at this phase; on append `ok` the entry becomes the sent phase |\n| effectful call, non-tail | `:call rd f args false` whose operator yields a blocking effect | `act`, pending per the effect kind | per the kind's row |\n| effectful call, tail | `:call rd f args true` likewise | `tail`, pending per the effect kind | **through return** against `K` |\n| link request | `:module/require` miss, request in hand | `act` or `tail`, pending `:link-request` | none at this phase; on append `ok` the entry becomes link response |\n| link response | request appended; polling for the correlated response | same state, pending `:link-response` | none at this phase; a matching `ok` makes it install; refusal raises |\n| install | the child runs | same state, pending `:install` + the body's install entry | on `linked`: the module symbol to `rd` or through return; on `refused`: raises |\n| halt | `:halt r`, or `:return r` with empty `K` | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/result` travels |\n\nTwo cases sit outside the table: an **explicit park** (`:park rd`) is a\n**no-wait safepoint**: it is a migration safepoint like every row\nabove, but it raises no wait entry; its `act` record is the body's\nparked record and a body of kind `:parked` names it by id (UCF §7.4.3\nr5, \"explicit park is the no-wait shape\"). A **reified continuation**\n(`:current-continuation`) is the one thing here that is *not* a\nsafepoint: it is a captured value, never a task state.\nWhole-task quiescence (empty ready queue, UCF §7.4.1) and the existing\nrefusal conditions (unsupported observation states, pending close) are\nunchanged.\n\nThe \"effectful call, tail\" row is the one place the rewrite is more\nthan notation: in the stack machine a tail effect's resume value landed\nin `val` and the body's `:return` then delivered it; here the body has\nno activation to resume into, so §2.4's `tail` state routes delivery\nthrough `K`. Write-result and through-return delivery are distinguished\nby the closed `deliver` record.\n\n### 4.2 The UCF frame\n\n`:yin.k/frame` (UCF §7.4.1) becomes, for the semantic profile:\n\n```clojure\n;; an act state (write-result delivery)\n{:yin.k/state    :act\n :yin.k/segment  A\n :yin.k/pc       n                       ; the resume pc, already pc+1\n :yin.k/reason   …                       ; unchanged\n :yin.k/window   {r (encoded) …}         ; saved(pc, rd), sorted by r\n :yin.k/deliver  {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n :yin.k/env      {sym encoded …}         ; E, unchanged\n :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return\n                    :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n                    :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/window {…} :yin.k/rd r} … ]\n :yin.k/pending  {…}}                    ; unchanged, §7.4.3\n\n;; a tail state (through-return delivery)\n{:yin.k/state    :tail\n :yin.k/segment  A :yin.k/site n         ; the tail call site, for provenance only\n :yin.k/reason   …\n :yin.k/deliver  {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n :yin.k/k        [ … ]                   ; the frames delivery will pop\n :yin.k/pending  {…}}\n```\n\n`:yin.k/val`, `:yin.k/stack` and `:stack-base` are gone. The window's\n**membership is validated**, not trusted: the receiver recomputes\n`saved(pc, rd)` from A (§8.1) and refuses a window with a register\noutside it (`:window-extra`), missing from it (`:window-missing`), or\ncontaining the delivery destination (`:window-self`). Those three are\nthe membership diagnostics; the full frame validator also checks\nrepresentation (integer ids, sorted unique keys), body scope (every\nregister of the window belongs to the body `pc` is in), site/pc pairing\n(`pc` is a safepoint successor of the right kind for `reason`;\n`site` is a tail call), destination equality (`rd` is the `rd` of the\ninstruction at `pc−1`), delivery mode (`:act` carries `:rd`, `:tail`\ncarries `:return` and no window or env), return-frame sites (each\n`:return` frame's `pc` follows a non-tail call whose `rd` it names, and\nits window is `saved(pc, rd)` of that body), `K` structure (frames\ninnermost last, each well-formed), and pending compatibility with\n`reason`. Two captures of equal state therefore encode identically, and\ndead values never travel.\n\nThe validator is the pure function `(defect frame code context task)`\nof `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` §3: `task` is decoded\ntask context (the parked key, the live FFI call ids, the free\nenvironment), never an engine; its rules are ordered, structural checks\nfirst, current-window rules on `act` only, return windows separately,\nand every `E` is checked to agree with the task's free environment off\nthe lexical names; it runs after canonical numbering, as the carrier\norders it.\n\nThe row carrier (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.2) changes\naccordingly: the frame rows are `:act [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\nk-cref rd]` and `:tail [segment-A site k-cref]`, the kind being the\ndelivery discriminant, so `deliver` is not a slot; `:window` is a keyed\nrow `[r₁ V₁ r₂ V₂ …]` sorted by `r` in which a nil-valued register is a\npresent `[:lit nil]`; `:kframe` is `[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\nrd]`; `:stack` and `stack-base` are removed; the root gains `free-env`,\nthe task's initial free environment (continuation design §2, §6.3).\nThat amendment is applied to the carrier draft with this design's\nphase 6 (§10; `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` §10.3).\n\n### 4.3 Foreign engines (UCF §7.4.2)\n\nA foreign engine's static safepoint map keeps `:yin.safepoint/segment`,\n`:yin.safepoint/pc` and `:yin.safepoint/engine`, replaces\n`:stack-effect` with the per-pc **def and use sets** and `L(pc)` derived\nfrom A (§8.1), and keeps `:yin.safepoint/layout` as a map from physical\nlocation to virtual register. The reconstruction obligation is: for an\n`act` state, produce the segment `A` and resume `pc`, every register in\n`saved(pc, rd) = L(pc) − {rd}` (never the not-yet-produced\ndestination), `E`, `K` with each frame's validated window and `rd`, the\ndelivery record, the pending state, and the captured store context; for\na `tail` state, produce the segment and the tail `site`, `K` with its\nvalidated frames, the delivery record and the pending state, and **no**\ncurrent window or `E`. A physical-slot map alone does not discharge it\nwhen values are spilled, rematerialized, or shared between locations;\nthe engine owes the values, however it kept them. `lift(lower(frame)) =\nframe` holds for admitted canonical frames, as today. The register\nkernel pairs physical and virtual registers pc by pc from `v` and\n`R(v)`, recovers every destination from A's call (its `dest` is\nphysical), and resolves each return frame's image and relative pc\nindependently; the stack kernel corresponds the non-push instructions of\n`H(v)` with `v`, slices its operand stack `[previous-base, base_i)` per\nreturn frame and `[last-base, count)` for the current activation, and\nneeds a tail-completion seam to deliver a tail state through `K`\n(continuation design §4.3, slice 6c-pre); both kernels' positional\nframes become `E` through the owner chain and `bind-params`, with the\ntask's free environment carried explicitly. The AST walker is not a\nframe reconstructor.\n\n## 10. Migration\n\nEight phases, each gated. The dated migration record below retains the\ncoexistence names: the old evaluator survived as an oracle until phase 8\n(2026-10-15).\n\n1. **Identity.** Amend the A-only identity contracts. **Complete**\n   (merged to master 2026-10-10, `2daa17fb`). Independent of this VM.\n2. **Freeze.** This document's §3 (grammar, minting), §4 (windows,\n   delivery, safepoint table), §6 (stamps), §3.4 (rejection rules) are\n   reviewed and frozen. Gate: Architect sign-off on the frozen text.\n3. **Linearizer, validator, static analysis.** `project` per §3.3, the\n   loader rules of §3.4 (both load paths), **and the §8.1 analysis\n   (def, use, `L`, `saved`)**, which the phase-4 evaluator needs for\n   every call save and capture and so cannot wait for phase 5. Gate:\n   deterministic vectors across JVM/Node/Dart on the corpus (the same\n   inline goldens and pinned addresses on all three); malformed-input\n   refusal rows for every rule including item 13, on the direct vector;\n   both AST projection lanes and the direct vector load through the one\n   validator to equal images (§3.4, direct-vector form; the\n   vector→datoms equivalence is retired); `L` agrees with an\n   independent reference implementation on the corpus. The image\n   `load-vector` returns is `{:vector :address :bodies [{:start :end\n   :owner :registers}]}` with `:end` **exclusive**; phase 4 computes\n   `analysis` once per admitted image as engine-local derived data, and\n   neither parsed trees nor saved sets enter canonical content.\n   *(Met 2026-10-10, commit 7a010962 on `srvm-phase3`; Architect\n   review `collab/1791530000000-architect-srvm-phase3-review.*`.)*\n4. **Evaluator.** `yin.vm.semantic-register` beside `yin.vm.semantic`.\n   During coexistence the evaluator links under the interim format\n   `:yin.semantic-register/code`, contract `\"v4\"`; phase 5 supplies its\n   production linker format record (the dependency-closure scanners over\n   the new table), and phase 8 retires it in favour of\n   `:yin.semantic/code` `\"v4\"`.\n   Gate: walker parity on the full B0 corpus (values, store, effects,\n   halting) under the §2.5 correspondence; Rule R rows; effects,\n   non-tail and tail recursion, pure and effectful tail completion,\n   continuation invocation (including abortive invoke and ownership\n   refusal), park/resume, gensym, module `store-of`, FFI\n   retained-request and response phases.\n   *(Met 2026-10-10/11, slices 4a `44fbafb4` and 4b `993d5411` on\n   master; Fable adversarial review per slice, three-host lanes green.\n   The evaluator's name and interim format keyword were retired at\n   phase 8 as this item said.)*\n5. **Derivations.** §8.2-8.3 implemented; the lexical-address law\n   (`addresses(H) = addresses(R) = addresses(resolve(A))`) and\n   behavioural parity of all three kernels on the corpus; the\n   execution oracle, validators, goldens and determinism checks of §8.4\n   green on three hosts, the slow C4 rows actually run; **the\n   dependency-closure scanners (UCF §7.6.1) reimplemented over the\n   new table agree with the old ones and with the tree-side oracles on\n   the B0 corpus, the register corpus and the C4 linked-prelude modules\n   by name**, under three obligations (derivations design §7.3): (i)\n   unordered agreement, old = new = the tree scanners and queries, on\n   free-name sets, the multiset of free-name occurrences as\n   `[name in-body?]`, the multiset of definitions as `[name conditional?]`,\n   and the store-key, FFI-op, parked-id and effect-kind sets (positions\n   excluded since the layouts differ; the tree scanners order by\n   structural path and are compared unordered); (ii) ordered agreement,\n   old = new = an independent emission-order tree oracle (main sequence\n   then bodies FIFO; a definition visits only its value operand), on the\n   occurrence and definition sequences and on the application-site count\n   (definition applications excluded, FFI sites included); (iii) join\n   agreement through `linker/verify`, old = new on the **complete\n   normalised outcome**: a success with its retained obligations as\n   `[name in-body?]` in order, or a refusal with its reason and name\n   (`:use-before-definition` included, exercised by a row that must\n   refuse on both sides). The old scanners' answers, normalised joins\n   included, are pinned as goldens before cutover deletes them, since the\n   C4 track's module declarations are built against those answers; the\n   C4 gate is read-only and compares declarations against the discharged\n   external-name set (module-defined keys and `yin/def` removed,\n   requirement-covered names removed), not raw free names.\n   *(Met 2026-10-11/12, slices 5a `71b4f4d7`, 5b `5e6c6f79`, 5c\n   `290c4476`; the scanner goldens are pinned in\n   `test/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc` (derivations design\n   §7.4), and after the cutover they are historical evidence with the\n   tree oracles as the independent check.)*\n6. **Continuation format.** §4.2 frames and both state shapes, the\n   full frame validator, the safepoint harness of §4.1 (bidirectional\n   lift/lower rows for every kind and both delivery modes, pending\n   waits, nested and reified captures, module contexts, refusals); the\n   row carrier amended to body version 3 with one profile,\n   `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`, carrying canonical\n   vectors by A, `:yin.k/trees` and the task's free environment, never\n   a native image (continuation design §2, §6). Gate: UCF §7.11's\n   harness green **for every engine intended to reconstruct canonical\n   frames** (the semantic kernel, the stack and register kernels through\n   their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam; the\n   walker is excluded), not the semantic profile alone.\n   *(Met 2026-10-12/13, slices 6a (row codec, frame validator,\n   discovery-order cursor-cell numbering), 6b (`lift-task`/`lower-task`),\n   6c (layouts, the stack tail-completion seam, the cross-engine laws)\n   and `6c-pre` `296f75fd`. The owner accepted the native lane matrix\n   at its measured extent — 61 law-4 cases and 350 law-5 transfers on\n   the native lanes against the reference lane's full 89 and 534 —\n   justified by the documented backend fragment (continuation design\n   §4.6), not by any deletion; see the lane runs recorded with slice\n   6c (89/534 cases green on the reference lane).)*\n7. **Stage D/E rerun.** Handoff, fencing, custody, durability,\n   crash-cut and cross-process acceptance over the new transport, three\n   hosts.\n   *(Met 2026-10-14, phase 7: custody on rows, the rows checkpoint\n   inspector, holder export with recovery format 2, the driver lower,\n   install checks, compose/fencing and Stage E over row packs; the\n   Stage E process matrix ran on jupiter over four host pairs with 196\n   SIGKILLed lives, and the slow tier was green on the landed head.\n   The `dht-end-to-end` failure Dart showed on master during these\n   lanes is the other seat's (the linker/admission track) and\n   pre-existing, not a phase-7 regression.)*\n8. **Cutover.** Delete `yin.vm.semantic` (old), rename\n   `yin.vm.semantic-register` to `yin.vm.semantic`, remove the old H/R\n   request paths and the `raise` dependency, remove the `:dim/lift-to`\n   row from both de Bruijn descriptors without a version bump and\n   regenerate the descriptor hashes and every pinned H and R\n   (derivations design §8.1-8.2); delete `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`,\n   `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize`, `yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile` and\n   their tests only after the replacement coverage of §8.4 is green and\n   the descriptor-hash golden has moved to a surviving namespace; switch\n   `linker/relowered`, `publish-closure!` and the REPL to the A-derived\n   lowerings (derivations design §8.3); regenerate the manifests'\n   derivation records as `A → H`, `A → R` and the format index in its\n   final shape `{format storage-address}` (derivations design §7.6,\n   §8.1); run the C4 suite over the newly published images as the\n   install-child gate (derivations design §6.1.4 (c)); finalize\n   documentation. No compatibility shim.\n   *(Met 2026-10-15, phase 8 (slices 8ab, 8c, 8d, 8e, 8f-1, 8f-2,\n   8f-3, 8g) per the phase-8 plan, `collab/1792560000000-*`: the\n   descriptors also flipped their `:hash` row to `:blake3` (pinned\n   constants regenerated once, 8ab); the model keyword, lane label and\n   record are `:semantic`/`SemanticVM` (8f-2b); the old transport\n   sources and `yin.vm.completion` died in 8f-1 rather than 8d; the\n   v3 halves of `yin.vm.ucf` died with the old kernel in 8f-3; item 13's tuple equality is\n   the encoder's content equality so NaN literals load on JS and Dart\n   (8f-3d); the two native kernels remain as backends over the\n   documented fragment (continuation design §4.6). BLAKE3 is the\n   digest throughout `yin.vm`; `dao.jing` still permits explicit SHA-256\n   minting.)*\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
