               (tuple-defect :body-partition\n   319\t                                      (:owner (nth bodies i)))\n   320\t                        :else (recur (body-of (:owner (nth bodies j)))\n   321\t                                     (conj seen j)))))\n   322\t              (range 1 (count bodies)))\n   323\t        (some (fn [pc]\n   324\t                (let [t (nth v pc)]\n   325\t                  (when (and (contains? #{:jump :branch-false} (nth t 0))\n   326\t                             (not= (body-of pc)\n   327\t                                   (body-of (peek t))))\n   328\t                    (tuple-defect :body-partition pc))))\n   329\t              (pcs v))\n   330\t        (some (fn [i]\n   331\t                (let [last-pc (dec (:end (nth bodies i)))]\n   332\t                  (when-not (= (if (zero? i) :halt :return)\n   333\t                               (nth (nth v last-pc) 0))\n   334\t                    (tuple-defect :body-partition last-pc))))\n   335\t              (range (count bodies))))))\n   336\t\n   337\t\n   338\t(defn- child-count\n   339\t  \"The number of child expressions §3.1 gives a tuple's production.\"\n   340\t  [t]\n   341\t  (case (nth t 0)\n   342\t    :call (inc (count (nth t 3)))\n   343\t    :ffi-call (count (nth t 3))\n   344\t    :stream-put 2\n   345\t    (:stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close :define :resume) 1\n   346\t    0))\n   347\t\n   348\t\n   349\t(defn- parse-range\n   350\t  \"Parse pcs `[s, e)` into a sequence of complete §3.1 expressions by\n   351\t   shift-reduce: an instruction node reduces the child expressions it\n   352\t   takes from the top of the stack; `[:branch-false c Lalt]` reduces the\n   353\t   test and recursively parses the consequent up to the `[:jump Lend]` at\n   354\t   `Lalt - 1` and the alternate over `[Lalt, Lend)`, each to exactly one\n   355\t   expression. Returns `[exprs nil]`, or `[nil pc]` naming the defect.\n   356\t\n   357\t   A node is `{:op mnemonic :pc pc :children […]}`; a conditional is\n   358\t   `{:op :if :pc branch-pc :jump jump-pc :end Lend :children [test then\n   359\t   else]}`.\"\n   360\t  [v s e]\n   361\t  (loop [pc s\n   362\t         stack []]\n   363\t    (if (>= pc e)\n   364\t      [stack nil]\n   365\t      (let [t (nth v pc)\n   366\t            op (nth t 0)]\n   367\t        (case op\n   368\t          (:jump :return :halt) [nil pc]\n   369\t          :branch-false\n   370\t          (let [alt (nth t 2)\n   371\t                jpc (dec alt)]\n   372\t            (if (or (empty? stack)\n   373\t                    (< jpc (inc pc))\n   374\t                    (> alt e)\n   375\t                    (not= :jump (nth (nth v jpc) 0)))\n   376\t              [nil pc]\n   377\t              (let [end (nth (nth v jpc) 1)]\n   378\t                (if (or (< end alt) (> end e))\n   379\t                  [nil jpc]\n   380\t                  (let [[then d1] (parse-range v (inc pc) jpc)]\n   381\t                    (cond d1 [nil d1]\n   382\t                          (not= 1 (count then)) [nil jpc]\n   383\t                          :else\n   384\t                          (let [[else d2] (parse-range v alt end)]\n   385\t                            (cond d2 [nil d2]\n   386\t                                  (not= 1 (count else)) [nil jpc]\n   387\t                                  :else (recur end\n   388\t                                               (conj (pop stack)\n   389\t                                                     {:op :if,\n   390\t                                                      :pc pc,\n   391\t                                                      :jump jpc,\n   392\t                                                      :end end,\n   393\t                                                      :children\n   394\t                                                      [(peek stack)\n   395\t                                                       (nth then 0)\n   396\t                                                       (nth else 0)]}))))))))))\n   397\t          (let [n (child-count t)\n   398\t                split (- (count stack) n)]\n   399\t            (if (neg? split)\n   400\t              [nil pc]\n   401\t              (recur (inc pc)\n   402\t                     (conj (subvec stack 0 split)\n   403\t                           {:op op,\n   404\t                            :pc pc,\n   405\t                            :children (subvec stack split)})))))))))\n   406\t\n   407\t\n   408\t(defn- parse-body\n   409\t  \"§3.1 `body ::= expr(r) [:return r] | expr(r) [:halt r]`: the body's\n   410\t   pcs before its terminator parse to exactly one expression. Returns the\n   310\t  [observed]\n   311\t  (when (and (stream/valid-outcome? :cursor observed)\n   312\t             (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome observed)))\n   313\t    (:dao.stream/cursor observed)))\n   314\t\n   315\t\n   316\t(defn- apply-candidate\n   317\t  \"`root` with `candidate`'s observation `observed` applied, through the\n   318\t   engine's public applies, and the live scan's resume point advanced\n   319\t   past the candidate's source.  The mint positions come out of the\n   320\t   observed outcome's own cursor, never a live one; a mint outcome with\n   321\t   no successful position installs nothing, leaving cell or link entry\n   322\t   exactly as it was.\"\n   323\t  [root candidate observed]\n   324\t  (let [ap (:ap candidate)\n   325\t        m (get-in root ap)\n   326\t        applied (case (:kind candidate)\n   327\t                  :mint (if-some [position (mint-position observed)]\n   328\t                          (engine/apply-mint m (:cell-id candidate) position)\n   329\t                          m)\n   330\t                  :link-mint (if-some [position (mint-position observed)]\n   331\t                               (engine/apply-link-cursor m\n   332\t                                                         (:link-id (:entry candidate))\n   333\t                                                         position)\n   334\t                               m)\n   335\t                  :next (engine/apply-next m (:entry candidate) observed)\n   336\t                  :poll (engine/apply-observation m (:entry candidate) observed)\n   337\t                  :ffi (engine/apply-ffi-read m (:call-id candidate) observed)\n   338\t                  :link-read (engine/apply-link-read m (:link-id candidate)\n   339\t                                                     observed))\n   340\t        root' (if (seq ap) (assoc-in root ap applied) applied)]\n   341\t    (assoc-in root' [:yin.k/custody :input :yin.k/scan] (:source candidate))))\n   342\t\n   343\t\n   344\t;; =============================================================================\n   345\t;; The held observation and its four states\n   346\t;; =============================================================================\n   347\t\n   348\t(defn- held-map\n   349\t  [k source observed state]\n   350\t  {:yin.k/input-seq k\n    41\t## 1. Stance\n    42\t\n    43\tThree owner rulings fix the shape of this document.\n    44\t\n    45\t1. *Cross-VM safepoints are semantic-VM safepoints.* The Universal AST is\n    46\t   the canonical code; the instruction vector is its deterministic\n    47\t   lowering; a safepoint is `(segment-address, pc)` into that vector\n    48\t   (UCF §7.3.2, §7.4.1). This carrier changes nothing about code\n    49\t   identity, safepoints, or execution profiles.\n    50\t2. *The transported state is a tuple set.* Sharing and the literal/marker\n    51\t   distinction stop being special cases: a reference is a row id in a\n    52\t   typed position, sharing is Merkle, and a program's data is wrapped\n    53\t   where it can be confused with a reference. What does not collapse is\n    54\t   the per-entity decision between content and identity, which this\n    55\t   carrier makes a property of the row kind and then commits to the root\n    56\t   through a census.\n    57\t3. *Tuples are the transport form only.* They exist between a lift and a\n    58\t   lower. At rest they are rows like any other and queryable by the\n    59\t   ordinary interpreter. Nothing executes them; the destination lowers\n    60\t   them into its native representation.\n    61\t\n    62\tThis proposal chooses positional rows: a row kind fixes a slot list, one\n    63\ttable serves encoder and decoder, and a row's Merkle id commits to its\n    64\tchildren inline, as code rows already do. EAV is expressive enough and\n    65\tnothing in the invariants rules it out; positional rows are chosen for\n    66\tcompactness, closedness, and consistency with the existing row\n    67\tconventions, not because EAV was rejected by review.\n    68\t\n   297\t### 5.2 Frame rows: states, windows, waits, frames (content rows)\n   298\t\n   299\t(Replaced 2026-10-11 by `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md`\n   300\t§2.3, under frozen `yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.2.)\n   301\t\n   302\t| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n   303\t|---|---|---|\n   304\t| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n   305\t| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n   306\t| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n   307\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n   308\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n   309\t| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n   310\t| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n   311\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n   312\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (§5.1) |\n   313\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n   314\t\n   315\t`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\n   316\tstructural rows, never a `V`; §4's \"a reference-free collection must be\n   317\t`[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is redundant\n   318\twith the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (continuation §3.2 rule\n   319\tA2), as `pc` is redundant with the pending and checked. The frame rows\n   320\tare in bijection with the keyed canonical frame of continuation §1.2\n   321\t(`rows->frame`, `frame->rows`, continuation §2.7).\n   322\t\n   427\t### 5.5 The root\n   428\t\n   429\t```clojure\n   430\t[T :task\n   431\t   version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n   432\t   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}, the one profile map (§2)\n   433\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   434\t   role             ; :root | :child\n   435\t   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n   436\t   free-env-cref    ; a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (continuation §6.3)\n   437\t   store-cref\n   438\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   439\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   440\t   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   441\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (continuation §6.1)\n   442\t   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   443\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   444\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n   445\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   446\t   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   447\t   custody]         ; plain, see below\n   448\t```\n   449\t\n   450\t(Root replaced 2026-10-11 by continuation §2.6: `free-env-cref` added,\n   451\t`contract` is the one profile map, `code` holds `\"v4\"` canonical vectors,\n   452\t`requires` gains `:yin.k/trees`.) No `layout` slot and no native image:\n   453\tthose were the positional profiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1).\n   454\t\n   455\tKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\n   456\tparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\n   457\tnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\n   458\tneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\n   459\tnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\n   460\tordered waits.\n   461\t\n   462\t**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner chose nil forks and phase 7 restricts custody keys.\n   463\t`custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n   464\t§7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n   465\t\n   466\t| role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n   467\t|---|---|---|---|\n   468\t| root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n   469\t| root | blocked / parked | fork | nil |\n   470\t| root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n   471\t| root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n   472\t| child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n   473\t\n   474\tFork custody is nil in every row; it proposes nothing and is never\n   475\tupgraded to custody. An exclusive-only receiver refuses a fork as\n   476\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. The slot admits only `:yin.k/policy`,\n   477\t`:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/arbitration`, `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and\n   478\t`:yin.k/origin`. No current lease, epoch or holder enters a body;\n   479\torigin's predecessor lease is lineage evidence.\n   480\t\n   481\tThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n   482\tsemantic identity**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n   483\tare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n   484\tplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n   485\tkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n   486\tthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n   873\t## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n   874\t\n   875\t**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cursor cells (§6);\n   876\twalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the initial free\n   877\tenvironment (continuation §6.3, §7.1), the result and each install\n   878\tchild, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n   879\t§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation\n   880\t§7.1 B4):** the merged order is quiescence and safepoint, kind, free\n   881\tenvironment, the walk (which fixes the segments, contexts and module\n   882\tstores), code, the per-context dependency closure over live code, tree\n   883\tcoverage from the composition's `:source-index`, frame validation of\n   884\tthe assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\n   885\twhole root store and every parked record.\n   886\t\n   887\t**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 adds the header and custody checks after frame validation.\n   888\t`:header` nil means fork; otherwise enrollment remains local and the root\n   889\tslot is written by kind after exhaustion/protection checks. The lift then\n   890\truns `custody-baseline` for the tree and emits sorted rows (§7). It can\n   891\tserve streams during the walk before a custody refusal; the holder\n   892\tretains successful answers for retries. The nested carrier's value table\n   893\t(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\n   894\ttable becomes the census.\n   895\t\n   896\t**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n   897\t\n   898\t**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: format-2 recovery uses an administrative fenced lower.\n   899\t`rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` runs the carrier checks and custody\n   900\tgrammar before attach, skips grant/protection, then gates every task\n   901\t`:exporting`. Waits and all parked records move to\n   902\t`:yin.k/recovery-waits` and `:yin.k/recovery-parked`, outside the scheduler;\n   903\tthe id counter is the carried value exactly and\n   904\t`:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` retains resource-key/cursor-cell associations.\n   905\tChild spawning disables stream construction and initializes nothing.\n   906\tThe recovery reader checks its local header and `:yin.k/state` against\n   907\tthe pack before lower, extracts the export record, and requires identical\n   908\tre-encoded pack bytes. It restores no tenure or runnable gate and refuses\n   909\tabort after restart. Ordinary resume still adopts `max(local, carried)`.\n   910\t\n   911\tThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n   912\t\n   913\t- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n   914\t  version-3 state `S` under the one profile of §2 **for which `lower(S)`\n   915\t  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n   916\t  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n   917\t  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n   918\t  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n   919\t  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n   920\t  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n   921\t  record, not minted; cursor-cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n   922\t  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n   923\t  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n   924\t  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n   925\t- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n   926\t  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n   927\t  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n   928\t  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n   929\t- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n   930\t  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n   931\t  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n   932\t  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n   933\t  reference machine yields.\n   934\t\n   935\tThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. The Stage D/E corpus,\n   936\tre-encoded as version-3 rows, is the oracle for the first law.\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:27:| what died at phase 8, for the record: `yin.vm.completion` (its agreement facts survive in `semantic/scan_test`), and `yin.vm.ucf`'s v3 canonicalizer and stack-shaped safepoint halves (un-ported: completion in 8f-1, UCF halves in 8f-3; R2/R3; `yin.vm.ucf` is now only `code-address`) | 8f-1 ruling | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | recorded in 8g (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` I-6, the dependency-completion banner, linker §5.2 note) | P3 |\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:36:| Closed 2026-10-16: `:try?` carried by all three UCF link pending variants; lifted try waiters retain their return contract | G1 / L-d-1b slice: row/frame and remote lift/lower; portable refusal, install and malformed-boolean tests in `semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`, frame bijection in `ucf/rows_test.cljc` | UCF | L-d-1b closed | P1 |\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:9575:  prelude.cljc + safepoint tests, hard stop and architect mob if dao.jing.cbor semantics must change. The r3\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:10223:Delegates: fable-5.1 sessions per question (refusal ruling 1379a095-..., range-elem c0ef9c80-..., C3-S2 NaN b01ce68c-..., safepoint limit cell 655c00a9-..., test-suite audit 40e7eefa-...);\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:10262:  S5-scope work), safepoint slices 3 and 4, then the recorded prelude follow-ups (py/is float behavior across hosts, range-count and membership cost, fmod-pos termination, stale genexp refusals, integer-key digit\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:11086:  (1) `docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`: the transported state of UCF as positional rows with merkle ids (body version 3, semantic profile only). Owner rulings encoded: semantic-VM safepoints; state as tuples; tuples transport-only, execution native. Design choices (labelled as proposals in §10): body version 3 with the existing outer gate; two id spaces (content rows / identity cells) with a root-committed cell census; disjoint [:lit|:row|:cell] value union; closed row table mirroring the v2 body (ordered waits, parked table, installs with child tasks, custody arm by role/kind/policy, r5 pending union with op-ids, env bindings + store-of); canonical cell numbering defined as the output of a renaming-invariant refinement-and-search byte procedure with validation by re-emission; validation in amendment §8 order; three parity laws with explicit domains; SHA-256 pinned for row ids.\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:11103:  (3) Phase 2 (design): `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` -- C = <seg, pc, W> with a sparse virtual-register window, parameters in E; body-local pre-order minting; instruction table = r2 shapes with names, no live operands; exhaustive expression-structured grammar with a recursive result-or-terminal outcome relation and exclusive definitions by structured paths; saved(p, rd) = W restricted to L(p) minus {rd}; closed act/tail states and deliver records, tail completion through K (no trampoline); rewritten safepoint table; stamp v4; A->R (resolve + pinned interval allocation, conservative overlap, structural slot for definition-less ids) and A->H (parse + adapter + existing lower-stack walk); b2/r2 retained only if bytes AND contracts unchanged; blast radius; eight-phase migration. Same thread: R1 CHANGES_REQUESTED (13), R2 (4), R3 (3), R4 (2), R5 READY_TO_FREEZE. Merged as a Draft; freeze is the phase-2 gate, not implementation acceptance.\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:11148:Done: I2 landed as c63ba6d6 (9 files, +3211/-169): dotted imports (bodies once, in order, parent attributes set), aliases (attribute-first per component with the sys.modules fallback), `from a import b [as c]` and `from m import *` (with __all__ and name validation), relative forms with CPython error texts, packages and __package__ ('' for top-level modules), bundled profile refuses every import statement except plain `import sys`/`import builtins`. Linked profile, three vector VMs (the AST walker still waits on L-b), as in I1. Review: codex gpt-6.1-sol round 1 REJECT (four P2 defects, all fixed by opus), round 2 REJECT on one remaining P2, landed on the owner's decision. Evidence (opus, final tree): JVM focused 172 tests / 1715 assertions, Node 201 tests, Dart 201 tests, kondo and cljstyle clean, seven goldens re-minted once (float_address x5, py manifest, pysp manifest). My own check on the rebased tree: golden tests (float-address, linked-prelude, linked-safepoint) 39 tests / 303 assertions green. I did not rerun the Node/Dart lanes after the final rebases (master moved through srvm phases only).\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:11217:NEXT, in order: (1) read the fable round-2 design; send it to codex for re-review (scope contract: block only on in-scope P0/P1); bring design + review to the owner BEFORE dispatching engineers (it moves the link-pair interpreter and admission from yin.repl.* into yin.vm.linker.*, slices AL-0 docs, AL-1 pure namespace relocation, AL-2 REPL reduction + handoff, AL-3 subject/context plumbing, AL-4 trust commands as configuration; the pinne