{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | sed -n '790,809p;906,918p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '2836,2844p'; nl -ba collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md | sed -n '106,115p'; git diff --stat; git status --short; wc -l collab/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"   790\t   decode its task context (parked key, live FFI call ids, the root's\n   791\t   free environment from `free-env-cref`) and judge it once by\n   792\t   `yin.vm.semantic.frame/task-defect` (continuation §3.2 rule\n   793\t   X1: the free environment is a sorted, unique symbol-keyed map with no\n   794\t   reserved name); this runs for every body, a `:halted` body with no\n   795\t   frames included, since no frame rule establishes X1. Then every\n   796\t   `:act`, `:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is\n   797\t   decoded to its keyed frame and judged by\n   798\t   `yin.vm.semantic.frame/defect` with its context (`:wait`,\n   799\t   `:captured`, `:parked`) and that task context; a defect of either\n   800\t   function is `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path\n   801\t   (continuation §3.3). This step runs after canonical numbering (step 6)\n   802\t   and before pending validation (step 8).\n   803\t8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\n   804\t   agreement of §5.3 (cursor-cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\n   805\t   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\n   806\t   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\n   807\t9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\n   808\t   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, the initial free\n   809\t   environment `free-env`, store, module stores, result, children; the\n   906\tThe recovery reader checks its local header and `:yin.k/state` against\n   907\tthe pack before lower, extracts the export record, and requires identical\n   908\tre-encoded pack bytes. It restores no tenure or runnable gate and refuses\n   909\tabort after restart. Ordinary resume still adopts `max(local, carried)`.\n   910\t\n   911\tThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n   912\t\n   913\t- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n   914\t  version-3 state `S` under the one profile of §2 **for which `lower(S)`\n   915\t  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n   916\t  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n   917\t  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n   918\t  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n  2836\t| Invariant | How UCF honors it |\n  2837\t|---|---|\n  2838\t| No hidden global state | Every dependency is declared in `:yin.k/requires` — *as computed by the declared fixed point, with its completeness stated* (`:yin.k/discovery`); the store travels as an explicit slice restored into an isolated store; fresh-name state and parked records travel explicitly; custody is facts on a named, possessed medium. |\n  2839\t| No implicit control flow | Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define; the successor chain is explicit `:yin.k/origin` links; the exporting state is an observed data state, never a notification. |\n  2840\t| No callbacks | Lift and lower are step functions; custody is observed, never notified; a lapsed holder stops at its own bound; an unanswered proposal is a step status, not a wait. |\n  2841\t| No shared mutable state | A UCF value is content-addressed and immutable; occurrence identity separates content variants from the one leased computation; two resumers of one value under `:fork` diverge into distinct incarnations; the isolated execution store shares nothing with the resumer's tasks. |\n  2842\t| No layer collapsing | Canonicalize, encode, discover, arbitrate, load, restore, execute are separate functions with data between them. The engine's hot loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the blocked machine's value, and the exporting state is that value with an emptied wait-set and a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery. |\n  2843\t| No assumed graphs | Sharing is an explicit value table keyed by address; cursor aliasing is an explicit cursor-cell graph, not inferred from positions; code refs are pcs relative to an addressed vector; nothing is resolved by pointer; foreign engines reconstruct dynamic state from the frame, never from layouts they assume. |\n  2844\t\n   106\t### B.2 Anything else genuinely about the outside world at a non-parked pc\n   107\t\n   108\t- **Operation ids.** `:yin.k/issue` is minted at park time under the `:running` gate onto the `:put` entry (`engine.cljc:2629-2633`); op-ids on `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` pendings are phase 7's slots. A `:run` state has no pending, hence no op-id; the counter that will mint the next one is custody state (phase 7's root slot), unaffected.\n   109\t- **Custody and the gate.** `:yin.k/gate`/`:yin.k/custody` are holder state the row carrier does not carry (phase 7 plan §3.1: lease, epoch, holder never enter a body). A `:run` snapshot under a holder is a preemption checkpoint; whether the holder may export a running task is a phase 7 policy question (§E.2, Q2), not a carrier obstruction.\n   110\t- **Call ids and park ids** derive from `id-counter`, which travels (`rows.cljc:1495`).\n   111\t- **Streams, cursor cells, cursor-refs, heap cells, host objects** are exactly today's cases (`lift-task*`'s walk; `:cell` and `:host-object` are refused as now).\n   112\t- **Telemetry, gc pins, callable-effects index** are engine-local derived data, recomputed by the receiver, as today.\n   113\t- **Host-fn application** (`(apply f args)`) is inside a step; a `:host`-class primitive is refused by discharge at lift (`:1418-1420`) whatever the pc.\n   114\t\n   115\tNothing at a non-parked boundary reaches the outside world that a parked boundary does not already reach. The owner's claim (3) stands with the restatement of A.5.\n?? collab/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md\n?? collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md\n?? collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md\n?? collab/1793640000000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793640000000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.prompt.md\n     156 collab/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
